/* Chapter 11 — canonical topics, questions, flashcards and exam prompts. */
IFL_DATA.registerChapter({"number":11,"title":"Ijarah – Leasing","part":"part-iii","partRoman":"III","pages":[279,306],"minutes":75,"estimates":{"f1":75,"f2":75},"difficulty":"Intermediate","difficultyAlt":"intermediate","learningObjectives":[{"id":"lo-ch11-1","text":"Define Ijarah and its essentials, and compare it with Bai‘.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch11-2","text":"State the general juristic rules of Ijarah, including what can be leased and when rent becomes due.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch11-3","text":"Explain execution of Ijarah, determination of rent (including benchmarks), sub-lease, security and liabilities of the parties.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch11-4","text":"Explain termination, amendment and treatment of default in rent.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch11-5","text":"Evaluate conventional financial, security and operating leases from the Shari’ah angle.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch11-6","text":"Describe Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek: procedure, methods of ownership transfer, sale and lease-back, Takaful and destruction of assets.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch11-7","text":"Explain Ijarah Sukuk and the potential of Ijarah; apply Box 11.1 risk mitigation and the Box 11.3 case study.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch11-8","text":"Define Ijarah as a sale of usufruct (not corpus) and state its essential elements and general juristic conditions.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch11-9","text":"Explain when an Ijarah contract can be executed for future enforcement and when rent actually begins to accrue.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch11-10","text":"Analyze the rules governing rent determination, benchmarking (including the LIBOR debate), and sub-leasing.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch11-11","text":"Explain the liabilities of lessor and lessee, and the rules on termination, amendment, and failure to pay rent.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch11-12","text":"Distinguish conventional financial, security, and operating leases from Shari'ah-compliant Ijarah, and identify the specific Shari'ah objections to conventional lease structures.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch11-13","text":"Explain the 'two contracts kept separate' principle behind Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek and the three permissible methods for transferring ownership at lease end.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch11-14","text":"Walk through the full bank procedure for Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek, including risk points and mitigation tools.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch11-15","text":"Explain how Ijarah Sukuk enable securitization and identify Ijarah's financing potential and risk profile.","origin":["f2"]}],"why":"Ijarah is the mode the author regards as having the greatest potential — for autos, housing, machinery and sovereign Sukuk. Its key test is simple but strict: ownership and its risk must stay with the lessor, and lease and sale must stay separate. Most exam questions on leasing turn on these two principles.","whyExpanded":"Ijarah is one of the most flexible and widely used Islamic financing modes - covering everything from auto and equipment leasing to housing and large-scale public infrastructure financed through Ijarah Sukuk. Because it closely resembles conventional leasing on the surface, getting the Shari'ah distinctions right (who bears ownership risk, when rent starts, why sale and lease must stay separate contracts) is essential to avoid structuring what is, in substance, an interest-bearing loan disguised as a lease.","overview":"The chapter defines Ijarah and its essentials, sets out general juristic rules (execution, rent, sub-lease, security, liabilities, termination, default), reviews modern leases (financial, security, operating) and their Shari’ah problems, and explains Islamic banks’ Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek with procedure, issues, securitisation and potential, ending with guidelines, risk mitigation, a comparison with conventional auto leasing, a case study and accounting.","summary":"Ijarah is the sale of usufruct: a known benefit of a specified non-consumable asset for a specified period against agreed rent, with ownership, its risks and ownership costs remaining with the lessor and the lessee liable only for negligence. Rent is due only when the usable asset is delivered; it may be based on cost and benchmarked (even to interest rates, per the author, though other benchmarks are preferable) for future periods with the first period fixed and a cap/floor; it cannot be increased for late payment (penalties to charity), and on early termination only due rent may be claimed. Conventional financial and security leases shift risk to the lessee and start rent before delivery; Islamic banks instead use Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek with ownership transfer through a separate unilateral promise to sell or gift, sale and lease-back only exceptionally with a time gap, and Takaful borne by the lessor. Leased assets can be sold with the lease and securitised as Ijarah Sukuk, giving Ijarah great potential for retail, corporate and sovereign finance. (Chapter synthesis — Section 11.6 gives the author’s guidelines.)","summaryExpanded":"Chapter 11 establishes Ijarah as the sale of usufruct (not corpus) - a contract where ownership and its associated risk always remain with the lessor, distinguishing it fundamentally from Bai' (sale). The chapter works through the general juristic rules (valid subject matter, rent determination, sub-leasing, liability allocation, termination), then critiques conventional financial and security leases for shifting rent-accrual timing and ownership risk onto the lessee in ways that conflict with Shari'ah. It presents Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek as the solution - a genuine lease kept structurally separate from a unilateral, lessor-binding promise to transfer ownership at lease end - and walks through the full bank procedure, operational pitfalls, and risk-management toolkit. The chapter closes by showing how leased assets (though not bare rental rights) can be securitized into Ijarah Sukuk, giving Ijarah substantial - and still largely unrealized - potential across retail, corporate, and public-sector financing.","summarySection":null,"takeaways":["Five essentials: contract, known usufruct, particular asset, period, agreed rent.","Only non-consumable assets can be leased; leasing money = loan with interest.","Rent due only after usable delivery; first-period rent fixed; floating rent with cap/floor.","Lessor bears ownership risk, major repairs, Takaful; lessee usage costs and negligence.","Early termination: only due rent; late rent: charity undertaking.","IMBT: lease + separate unilateral promise to sell or gift.","Sale and lease-back: exceptional; sale first; ~1-year gap before transfer back.","Ijarah Sukuk: pro rata ownership; quasi-fixed return; rent-only assignment prohibited.","Ijarah transfers usufruct (right to use) only; ownership and its associated risk always remain with the lessor throughout the lease - this is the core distinction from Bai' (sale) and from conventional financial/security leases.","Rent under Shari'ah begins accruing only once the asset is delivered to the lessee in usable form - not from the date the lessor pays the supplier, as in conventional finance leases.","Ownership-related expenses (major repairs, Takaful, defect rectification) are the lessor's responsibility; only day-to-day usage expenses fall on the lessee.","Ijarah is a binding contract; on lessee default, the bank can only collect rent already due - never rent for the remaining unused period - and any late-payment penalty must go to charity.","Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek solves the ownership-transfer problem by keeping the lease and the eventual sale/gift as two separate, independent transactions, joined only by a unilateral promise binding on the lessor alone.","Using an interest-rate benchmark (like LIBOR) to price rent does not itself make a lease non-compliant - what matters is that the lessor genuinely bears ownership/corpus risk throughout.","Ijarah does not give banks a guaranteed fixed return - they bear genuine ownership expenses and risks (Takaful shortfalls, repair costs, usufruct instability from external events).","Selling a leased asset (with the lease continuing) is permitted and underpins Ijarah Sukuk-based securitization, but merely assigning the right to collect rent without an ownership transfer is not - Ijarah Sukuk returns are only quasi-fixed, not guaranteed."],"takeawayOrigins":["f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2"],"checklist":["Can you list the essentials of Ijarah and compare it with sale?","Can you state Kasani’s conditions and when rent becomes due?","Can you explain benchmarked rent and the LIBOR debate?","Can you allocate liabilities between lessor and lessee?","Can you explain termination rules and remaining-rent prohibition?","Can you critique conventional leases on four points?","Can you describe IMBT procedure and AAOIFI transfer methods?","Can you solve the Box 11.3 case numbers?"],"topics":[{"id":"t11.1","section":"11.1","title":"Introduction: Meaning and Legitimacy of Ijarah","pages":[279,279],"tier":"core","concepts":["ijarah"],"intuition":"What exactly is being ‘sold’ in a lease?","simpleExplanation":"Ijarah (from al-’Ajr — compensation) is the transfer of a known usufruct of a specified asset for a specified period against a specified lawful rent — or hiring work for a wage. Jurists call it the sale of usufruct, not of the asset. It is permissible by Qur’an, Sunnah and Ijma‘a.","academicExplanation":["Contemporary jurists see great potential in Ijarah as an alternative to interest. It is permissible by Ijma‘a; Imam Shafi‘i and others see two Qur’anic verses as general proof. Literally from al-’Ajr — compensation, consideration, counter value (al‘Iwad); as a contract, hiring or renting an asset for its usufruct, and hiring labour or work against a wage; it covers rules of labour, renting, Ju‘alah and contracts for usufruct of goods and services; also termed Kira’a and Istijar. In Islamic law it is a contract of a known and proposed usufruct of specified assets for a specified time against a specified lawful consideration — rent for things, wages for people. Jurists: the sale of usufruct (not of ‘Ain) against Ujrah — houses, shops, animals, jewellery, clothes. It lets people use assets they cannot own. The chapter focuses on Ijarah of assets as a mode of finance, usually Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek."],"examExplanation":"Ijarah = transfer of known usufruct of a specified asset for a specified period against specified lawful rent (or work for wages); sale of usufruct; legitimacy: Qur’an, Sunnah, Ijma‘a.","keyPoints":["Also called Kira’a, Istijar.","Covers asset hire and labour hire.","From al-Ajr: compensation or counter value; also called Kira’a and Istijar."],"definitions":[{"term":"Ijarah","definition":"A contract transferring a known usufruct of a specified asset for a specified period against a specified lawful rent (or hiring work against wages) — the sale of usufruct."}],"examples":[{"title":"Two kinds of Ijarah","kind":"textbook","text":"Renting a delivery van is Ijarah of things, and the consideration is rent (Ujrah). Hiring a doctor or tailor is Ijarah of persons, and the consideration is a wage (Ajr) (p. 279)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Ijarah covers only the renting of property.","right":"Ijarah covers the hire of assets (rent) and of people’s work (wages); Ju‘alah and other usufruct contracts fall under its rules (p. 279)."}],"relatedTopics":["t11.2","t3.2.1.b"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In Ijarah, what is transferred to the lessee?","options":["Ownership of the asset","The usufruct of the asset","The asset’s risk","A loan"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 279."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch11-t1"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch11-t1","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"11.1","sectionTitle":"Introduction","title":"Ijarah as a Contract of Usufruct","covers":["t11.1"],"overview":"Introduces Ijarah's linguistic and legal meaning as the sale of usufruct (not the underlying asset), its Shari'ah basis, and its scope covering both asset leasing and hired labour.","simpleExplanation":"Ijarah means renting out the USE of something, not selling the thing itself. If you lease a car, you're paying for the right to drive it, not buying the car. The same concept covers hiring a person's labor for a wage. It's permitted by the Qur'an, the Prophet's Sunnah, and unanimous scholarly agreement, because it's a practical way for people to use assets they can't afford to own outright.","academicExplanation":"Ijarah derives from al-'Ajr, meaning compensation, substitute, consideration, or counter-value (al-'Iwad). As a contract, it refers to hiring/renting an asset to benefit from its usufruct, and also encompasses hiring labour or contracting work for a wage - broadly, the rules of labour, renting, Ju'alah, and all usufruct-of-goods-and-services contracts fall under Ijarah (less common synonyms: Kira'a, Istijar). In Islamic law, Ijarah is a contract for a known, proposed usufruct of specified assets over a specified period, against a specified lawful return - i.e. the transfer of usufruct for consideration (rent for asset-hiring, wages for hiring people). Jurists describe it as the sale of usufruct (not 'Ain/corporeal goods) in exchange for Ujrah. Permissibility rests on the Qur'an (Imam Shafi'i and others cite two general verses), the Sunnah, and Ijma'a (consensus) - it is a convenient means for people to access the use of assets they cannot afford to own, subject to conditions in Hadith/Fiqh literature. The chapter focuses primarily on Ijarah of goods/leasing as an investment and financing mode (typically taking the form of Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek), with Ijarah of labour/services discussed only briefly where relevant.","examExplanation":"Ijarah = sale of USUFRUCT (right to use), not the corpus/'Ain (the asset itself); consideration = Ujrah/rent (assets) or Ajr/wages (labour). Basis: Qur'an, Sunnah, Ijma'a. Chapter focus: Ijarah of goods/leasing as financing (mainly Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek); labour/services Ijarah discussed only briefly.","keyPoints":["Ijarah derives from al-'Ajr (compensation/consideration); it is the sale of USUFRUCT, not the corpus (underlying asset) of a commodity.","Ijarah covers both asset leasing (rent as consideration) and hiring of labour/services (wages as consideration) - both fall under the same broad Fiqh category.","Permissibility rests on the Qur'an, Sunnah, and Ijma'a (unanimous jurist consensus), subject to specific conditions detailed in Hadith and Fiqh literature.","The chapter's primary focus is Ijarah as a financing mode for Islamic banks, which normally takes the form of Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek (lease ending in ownership transfer)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Ijarah","definition":"A contract of a known and proposed usufruct of specified assets for a specified time period against a specified and lawful return or consideration - the sale of usufruct, not of the underlying corporeal asset."},{"term":"Ujrah","definition":"Rent or hire consideration paid in exchange for the usufruct of a leased asset."},{"term":"Ajr","definition":"Wages paid in exchange for hired labour or services."}],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think Ijarah only applies to physical asset leasing. It equally covers hiring labour/services for a wage - both are usufruct-transfer contracts under the same broad Fiqh umbrella."],"distinctions":["Sale of usufruct (Ijarah - right to use) vs sale of corpus ('Ain - the asset itself, as in Bai')."],"relatedConcepts":["Ju'alah","Wakalah","Bai'"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.1","pages":[279]}}]},{"id":"t11.2","section":"11.2","title":"Essentials of Ijarah Contracts","pages":[280,280],"tier":"core","concepts":["ijarah"],"intuition":"What five things must every Ijarah have?","simpleExplanation":"Five essentials: a contract; known usufruct transferred; of a particular asset; for a specified period; against agreed rent. The lessee is ‘Ameen’ (trustee) — liable only for negligence. The leased asset must be like ‘land’ — not consumed in use, not money. Rent: Ujrah (things) or Ajr (wages); Ajr al-Musammah if fixed, Ajr al-Mithl if set by a judge.","academicExplanation":["Essentials: (1) a contract; (2) known usufruct transferred; (3) of a particular asset; (4) for a specified period; (5) against agreed rental. Parties must be capable. The lessee is Ameen, entitled to use the asset only for the specified purpose; liable for loss by negligence but not for loss beyond his control (AAOIFI 7/1/4). Subject matter: property/assets (houses, vehicles) or labour (engineer, doctor, tailor); the Majallah adds letting animals — analogous to modern leasing. The leased asset must belong to the category of land — real assets not altered in form by use — not things consumed in use nor financial/monetary assets; the lessor as owner bears ownership expenses and risks. Consideration is Ujrah (rent) or Ajr (wages); fixed in the contract — Ajr al-Musammah; determined by judge/arbitrator — Ajr al-Mithl."],"examExplanation":"Essentials: contract; known usufruct; particular asset; specified period; agreed rent. Lessee = Ameen (liable only for negligence). Asset not consumed in use. Ajr al-Musammah vs Ajr al-Mithl.","keyPoints":["Lessor bears ownership risks and expenses."],"definitions":[{"term":"Ameen","definition":"Trustee — the lessee holds the leased asset in trust, liable only for negligence or misuse."},{"term":"Ajr al-Musammah / Ajr al-Mithl","definition":"Rent/wage fixed in the contract / rent or wage determined by a judge or arbitrator (customary)."},{"term":"Ajr al-Musammah","definition":"Rent or wage fixed in the contract."},{"term":"Ajr al-Mithl","definition":"Rent or wage determined by a judge or arbitrator (the customary rate)."}],"examples":[{"title":"Lessee as trustee","kind":"textbook","text":"A leased car is stolen although the lessee locked and parked it properly. The lessee, as Ameen, is not liable; the lessor bears the loss. Had he left it unlocked with the keys inside, he would be liable for negligence (p. 280)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"The lessee is liable for any damage to the leased asset.","right":"The lessee is Ameen (trustee): liable only for loss from his negligence or misuse, not for loss beyond his control (p. 280)."}],"relatedTopics":["t11.2.1","t2.5.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"When is the lessee liable for damage to the leased asset?","options":["Always","Only when caused by his negligence or misuse","Never","Only in operating leases"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 280."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch11-t2"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch11-t2","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"11.2-11.2.1","sectionTitle":"Essentials of Ijarah Contracts / Ijarah and Bai' Compared","title":"Essentials of Ijarah and How It Differs from Bai' (Sale)","covers":["t11.2","t11.2.1"],"overview":"Lists the five essential elements of a valid Ijarah contract and explains the fundamental structural difference between leasing (usufruct transfer, ownership retained) and sale (ownership transferred).","simpleExplanation":"For a lease to be valid, you need: a contract, a clearly known usufruct being transferred, a specific asset, a fixed time period, and an agreed rental. The renter (lessee) is trusted to use the asset properly and is only responsible for damage they cause through carelessness - not damage from factors outside their control. The big difference from a sale: in a sale, ownership passes to the buyer immediately; in a lease, ownership always stays with the person renting out the asset (the lessor), who bears all the ownership-related risks and costs.","academicExplanation":"The five essentials of Ijarah: (1) it is a contract; (2) known usufruct is transferred; (3) of a particular asset; (4) for a specified time period; (5) against agreed-upon rental. Parties must have contractual capacity. The lessor provides the asset for benefit against rental; the lessee is considered 'Ameen' (trustee), entitled to use the asset per the agreement's specified purpose only, liable for loss due to his own negligence but not for loss from factors beyond his control. Ijarah's subject matter divides into property/assets (houses, vehicles) and labour (engineer, doctor, tailor work) - Majallah further divides leasing subject matter into three types, the third being animal-letting. The asset leased should belong to the 'land' category of production factors - real assets not altered in physical form by use (not consumed with usage, not a financial/monetary asset) - and the lessor, as owner, must bear ownership-related expenses and risks. Consideration is Ujrah (rent) or Ajr (wages); if fixed contractually it's Ajr al-Musammah (agreed rent/wage), if determined by a judge/arbitrator it's Ajr al-Mithl. On the Ijarah-Bai' comparison: both transfer something for valuable consideration, requiring comprehensive mutual knowledge of the benefit/consideration to avoid conflict - but in sale, ownership of the corpus transfers to the purchaser; in Ijarah, the corpus remains with the lessor (transferor), and only usufruct (the right to use) transfers to the lessee, with ownership-related risks/expenses remaining the lessor's. If the lessee somehow becomes owner of the corporeal property (e.g. by gift or inheritance), the Ijarah automatically ceases. A further key difference: lease contracts are always time-bound (terminating at some defined point), while sale implies a definite, immediate transfer of ownership (with its risk and reward) upon execution.","examExplanation":"5 Ijarah essentials: contract + known usufruct + particular asset + specified time period + agreed rental. Lessee = 'Ameen' (trustee) - liable only for negligence-caused loss, not for loss beyond his control. Ijarah vs Bai': Ijarah transfers usufruct only (corpus/ownership stays with lessor, who bears ownership risk); Bai' transfers ownership (corpus) immediately to the buyer. Lease is always time-bound; sale is a definite, immediate, permanent transfer.","keyPoints":["The five essentials of Ijarah: a contract, known usufruct transferred, of a particular asset, for a specified time period, against agreed rental.","The lessee is an 'Ameen' (trustee) - liable only for loss caused by his own negligence, not for loss from factors beyond his control.","The leased asset should be a 'land'-type factor of production - real, not consumed with use, not a financial/monetary asset - and the lessor must bear all ownership-related expenses/risks.","The fundamental structural difference from Bai' (sale): in Ijarah, only usufruct (right to use) transfers to the lessee, while ownership/corpus remains with the lessor throughout; in Bai', ownership transfers immediately and definitely to the buyer.","If a lessee somehow becomes owner of the leased property (e.g. via gift or inheritance) during the lease term, the Ijarah contract automatically ceases.","Lease contracts are inherently time-bound (must terminate at some point); sale contracts involve a definite, permanent transfer of ownership upon execution."],"definitions":[{"term":"Ameen","definition":"A trustee - the legal status of a lessee with respect to the leased asset, entitling him to use it per the agreement while being liable only for loss caused by his own negligence."},{"term":"Ajr al-Musammah","definition":"Rent or wage fixed by agreement in the Ijarah contract itself."},{"term":"Ajr al-Mithl","definition":"Rent or wage determined by a judge or arbitrator, rather than fixed by the parties' agreement."}],"conditions":["A valid Ijarah requires a contract, known/ascertained usufruct, a particular specified asset, a specified time period, and agreed-upon rental."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think a lessee bears full risk of the leased asset, similar to an owner. In fact, ownership-related risk always stays with the lessor - the lessee is only liable for loss he causes through negligence or misuse."],"distinctions":["Ijarah (usufruct transfer, ownership/risk stays with lessor, time-bound) vs Bai' (ownership/corpus transfer, immediate and permanent, risk moves to buyer)."],"relatedConcepts":["Manafa'ah","'Ayn","Ownership Risk"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.2-11.2.1","pages":[280,281]}}]},{"id":"t11.2.1","section":"11.2.1","title":"Ijarah and Bai‘ Compared","pages":[280,281],"tier":"core","concepts":["ijarah","bai"],"intuition":"Both lease and sale transfer something for value. What is the decisive difference?","simpleExplanation":"In sale, ownership of the corpus passes permanently with risk and reward; in Ijarah only the usufruct passes and ownership, risks and ownership expenses stay with the lessor. A lease is always time-bound. If the lessee becomes owner (gift, inheritance), the Ijarah ends.","academicExplanation":["Ijarah resembles sale since something is transferred for valuable consideration, so the benefit and consideration must be known. In sale, ownership of the corpus transfers to the purchaser; in Ijarah the corpus remains with the lessor and only usufruct passes; ownership-related risks and expenses are borne by the lessor. If the lessee becomes owner in any way (gift, inheritance), the Ijarah ceases (Majallah Art. 442). A lease is always time-bound; sale is a definite transfer of ownership with its risk and reward at execution."],"examExplanation":"Sale: corpus ownership, permanent, risk to buyer. Ijarah: usufruct only, time-bound, ownership risk/expenses with lessor; ends if lessee becomes owner.","keyPoints":["Lease must terminate at some point.","A lease is always time-bound; a sale transfers ownership permanently."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Inheritance ends the lease","kind":"textbook","text":"A tenant leases a flat from his father for five years; the father dies in year two and the tenant inherits the flat. The Ijarah ceases, because one cannot lease from oneself (pp. 280–281)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"If the lessee inherits the leased asset, the lease continues.","right":"Once the lessee becomes owner by gift, inheritance or otherwise, the Ijarah ceases (p. 280)."}],"relatedTopics":["t3.2.1.b","t5.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A lessee inherits the leased house from the lessor. What happens to the Ijarah?","options":["It continues","It ceases, because the lessee has become owner","Rent doubles","It becomes Murabaha"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 280."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"table":{"caption":"Ijarah vs Bai‘ (Section 11.2.1)","head":["Aspect","Bai‘ (sale)","Ijarah (lease)"],"rows":[["What passes","Ownership of the corpus","Usufruct only"],["Duration","Permanent","Time-bound"],["Ownership risk and expenses","Buyer (after sale)","Lessor"],["Consideration","Price","Rent"],["If transferee becomes owner","—","Ijarah ceases"]]}},{"id":"t11.3","section":"11.3","title":"General Juristic Rules of Ijarah","pages":[281,282],"tier":"core","concepts":["ijarah"],"intuition":"What can be leased, and when does rent start?","simpleExplanation":"Only things with usufruct whose corpus is not consumed (not money, food, fuel, candles, ammunition); leasing money is a loan and its rent is interest. Asset and rent should not be of the same genus. Kasani’s conditions: ascertained usufruct; specified period; possible benefit; delivery before rent is due; capable worker; lawful use; conventional usufruct. Contemporary jurists: rent is due only once the asset is in the lessee’s possession in usable form. Earnest money and advance rent are allowed.","academicExplanation":["Jurists are almost unanimous that Ijarah is valid for things with usufruct whose corpus is not consumed; candles, cotton, food, fuel suit sale, not lease; Kasani: dirhams, dinars and bullion are ‘Ain not usufruct. The genus of asset and rent should not be the same (house for house). Leasing money, edibles, fuel or ammunition is deemed a loan; rent charged is interest. Assets from which usufruct is almost impossible (saline, unproductive land) cannot be leased. Valid for permissible usufruct with consent; the asset belongs to the lessor as owner or lessee with permission to sub-lease; joint assets may be leased to more than one lessee; free of Gharar about usufruct and counter value.","Rent can be fixed only when the property is known (inspection or description); conditions expediting or deferring rent are allowed; rent should follow local convention and be just (Qur’an 65:6 on nursing women’s recompense). Kasani’s conditions: usufruct ascertained; lease period specified (for service, either work or time known); benefit possible — no lease of a nonexistent asset not precisely describable; delivery essential — no rent due merely on execution unless the asset is available, though advance rent may be taken if availability is ensured; the worker must be capable (no hire of a runaway animal or usurped assets); usufruct lawful; usufruct conventional.","Hanafis: rent is not due by the contract alone — usufruct must emerge; Shafi‘is: due on finalisation, presuming use, i.e. once in the lessee’s possession and usable. Contemporary jurists: rent due only after the asset is in the lessee’s possession in usable form (AAOIFI 5/2/2). If the asset is to be purchased at the prospective lessee’s request, the lessor may take earnest money; if the customer breaches, the lessor retains actual loss (processing, purchase, re-leasing or disposal) and returns the excess. AAOIFI: rent paid when due, in instalments or as agreed; advance rent adjusted later; earnest money at execution may be advance rent."],"examExplanation":"Leasable: non-consumable assets with usufruct (not money, food, fuel). Leasing money = loan; rent = interest. Kasani’s conditions. Rent due only when asset delivered in usable form. Earnest money/advance rent allowed; actual loss retained on breach.","keyPoints":["Asset and rent of same genus not allowed (house for house).","Unproductive saline land cannot be leased.","No rent merely because the contract is signed.","Leasing money, edibles or fuel is really a loan, and “rent” on it is interest.","Contemporary jurists agree rent accrues only once the asset is with the lessee in usable form (Hanafi and Shafi‘i views converge)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Saline land","kind":"textbook","text":"Land so saline it cannot produce anything cannot be leased — benefit from the asset must be possible (p. 281)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A house can be leased in exchange for the use of another house.","right":"Kasani: the genus of the leased asset and the rent should not be the same (house for house, ride for ride) (p. 281)."}],"relatedTopics":["t11.3.1","t4.2.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Under contemporary scholars’ view (AAOIFI), when does Ijarah rental become due?","options":["When the bank pays the supplier","When the contract is signed","After the asset is in the lessee’s possession in usable form","At the end of the lease"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 282."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch11-t3"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch11-t3","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"11.3","sectionTitle":"General Juristic Rules of Ijarah","title":"General Juristic Rules of Ijarah: Valid Subject Matter and Conditions","covers":["t11.3","t11.3.1","t11.3.2","t11.3.3","t11.3.4","t11.3.5","t11.3.6"],"overview":"Details which assets can be leased (not consumed with use), why money/edibles/fuel cannot be leased, and Al-Kasani's list of essential validity conditions.","simpleExplanation":"You can only lease things that give benefit without being 'used up' - like a house or a car, not money or food, because using those things means consuming them entirely (which makes the transaction effectively a loan, and any extra charged on top would be interest). For a lease to be valid: both parties must agree, the asset must be clearly known, the lease period must be specified, using the asset must actually be possible, the asset must be handed over before rent is owed, and the purpose must be lawful.","academicExplanation":"Jurists are nearly unanimous that Ijarah is valid only for things possessing Manafa'ah (usufruct/benefit) whose corpus/substance ('Ayn) is not consumed by use - goods like candles, cotton, food, fuel are suitable for SALE, not leasing. Kasani explains dirhams, dinars, bullion (which are 'Ain, not usufruct) and anything whose benefit requires consumption cannot be leased; further, the genus of the leased asset and the rent should not be the same (e.g. house-for-house, ride-for-ride). If money/edibles/fuel/ammunition are 'leased,' the transaction is deemed a loan, and any rent charged on it amounts to interest. Assets from which usufruct is virtually impossible (e.g. land too saline to produce) also cannot be leased. Validity requires mutual consent, the asset belonging to the lessor (as owner or as a permitted sub-lessor), freedom from Gharar regarding the nature of usufruct/consideration, and mutual knowledge of the contracted usufruct's nature. Rent can only be fixed once the property is known (via inspection, viewing, or description); it's permissible to stipulate expedited or deferred/delayed rent payment terms as agreed. Al-Kasani's key validity conditions: the contracted usufruct must be ascertained to avoid dispute; the lease period must be specified (for wage/service work, either the amount of work or the time period must be known); benefiting from the hired goods must actually be possible (leasing a nonexistent asset whose usufruct can't be precisely described is disallowed, due to Gharar); handing over/delivery of the leased goods is essential - NO rent becomes due merely upon contract execution unless the subject is delivered and made available (though advance rent CAN be taken once availability for the lease period is ensured); the hired party (for services) must be capable of the job (hiring a runaway animal or usurped assets is invalid); the usufruct must be lawful (not Haram purpose); the usufruct should follow local convention/tradition. On WHEN rent becomes due: Hanafi jurists hold rent is due only once usufruct actually emerges (i.e. after delivery in usable form), while Shafi'i jurists hold rent becomes due upon contract finalization (though they too presume usufruct materializes through the lessee's use) - in practice, even under the Shafi'i view rent becomes due once the asset is possessed and usable by the lessee. If the asset to be leased is yet to be purchased at the prospective lessee's request, the lessor can demand earnest money to secure the lessee's commitment; if the lessee breaches this promise, the lessor may retain the amount of loss actually incurred (processing costs, purchase costs, cost of disposing/re-leasing elsewhere), returning any excess. Per AAOIFI, rental can be paid in instalments or as mutually agreed, and advance rent (effectively serving as earnest money at contract execution) is permissible.","examExplanation":"Ijarah valid ONLY for assets whose usufruct doesn't consume the corpus (not money, edibles, fuel, ammunition - leasing these = deemed a loan, rent on them = Riba). Al-Kasani's conditions: ascertained usufruct, specified lease period, usufruct must actually be achievable (no Gharar), delivery required before rent accrues (though advance rent is allowed once availability is ensured), capable subject (no runaway animals/usurped assets), lawful purpose, conventional usufruct. Rent timing debate: Hanafi = due only after usufruct emerges (post-delivery, usable form); Shafi'i = due at contract finalization (but converges with Hanafi in practice, since usufruct is presumed to materialize via use).","keyPoints":["Ijarah is valid only for assets whose usufruct can be enjoyed without consuming the corpus - money, edibles, fuel, and ammunition cannot be leased (doing so is deemed a loan, and any rent charged on it is Riba).","The genus of the leased asset and the rent charged should not be identical (e.g. house-for-house, ride-for-ride is disallowed).","No rent becomes due merely upon contract execution - the subject must actually be delivered and made available to the lessee first, though advance rent CAN be taken once the asset's availability for the lease period is ensured.","Leasing a nonexistent asset whose usufruct cannot be precisely described is disallowed due to Gharar; likewise, hiring a runaway animal or usurped assets is invalid because benefiting from them is not actually possible.","Hanafi jurists hold rent is due only once usufruct actually emerges (post-delivery, usable form); Shafi'i jurists hold rent is due at contract finalization, though even they presume usufruct materializes through actual use - so in practice both views converge on rent being payable once the asset is possessed and usable.","If a prospective lessee's promised asset (yet to be purchased) falls through due to his breach, the lessor may retain only the actual loss incurred (processing, purchase, and disposal/re-lease costs), returning any excess."],"definitions":[{"term":"Manafa'ah","definition":"Usufruct or benefit - the intangible right to use an asset, which is the actual subject matter transferred in an Ijarah contract (as opposed to the corpus/'Ayn of the asset itself)."}],"conditions":["The leased asset must possess Manafa'ah (usufruct) without its corpus being consumed by use.","The genus of the leased asset and the rent should not be the same (e.g. house-for-house).","The contracted usufruct must be ascertained and the lease period specified to avoid dispute.","Delivery of the leased asset is required before rent accrues, though advance rent may be taken once availability is ensured.","The usufruct of the leased asset must be lawful and actually achievable."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students often think 'leasing' money (e.g. charging a fee for its use over time) could be structured as Ijarah. It cannot - money is 'Ain (not usufruct-generating without consumption), so any such 'lease' is deemed a loan, and any charge on it is Riba."],"distinctions":["Assets suitable for Ijarah (usufruct without consuming the corpus - houses, vehicles) vs assets suitable only for sale (consumed with use - candles, food, fuel, money)."],"relatedConcepts":["Gharar","Riba","Ayn"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3","pages":[281,282]}}],"conditions":["Usufruct ascertained","Lease period specified","Benefit possible (asset exists or precisely describable)","Asset delivered/available before rent is due","Lessor/worker capable of delivering","Usufruct lawful","Usufruct conventional"]},{"id":"t11.3.1","section":"11.3.1","title":"Execution of an Ijarah Contract","pages":[282,283],"tier":"core","concepts":["ijarah","wad"],"intuition":"Can a bank sign a lease for an asset it doesn’t own yet?","simpleExplanation":"For an existing asset (e.g. a liveable house) Ijarah may take effect immediately or from a future date — unlike sale — since the lessor keeps ownership risk. Ijarah Mosufah bil Zimmah (described asset, replaceable units) can also be future-dated. But a specific identified asset cannot be leased before it exists or is owned; the lessor enters into a promise to lease, may take advance rent, then buys the asset (in sale and lease-back, the sale precedes the lease).","academicExplanation":["Depending on the asset, Ijarah may be executed before or after the lessor’s possession for instant or future commencement. For an existing asset, execution for instant or future enforcement is allowed because usufruct is clear; future enforcement, unlike Bai‘, is allowed because the lessor remains owner bearing risk. Ijarah Mosufah bil Zimmah (quality specified; damage to a particular unit does not terminate) may be instant or future. If a particular asset is specified, the contract cannot be executed before its existence or ownership (or usufruct, for sub-lease); if destroyed, the contract ends. A lessor not owning the asset can enter a promise to lease; advance rent is adjusted against rent falling due. Islamic banks’ leases usually fall here; the lessor buys the asset from the market or, specially, from the promisee — sale and lease-back — where the sale must precede the lease and the lease must not be a condition (AAOIFI 3/1, 3/2)."],"examExplanation":"Existing asset/Mosufah bil Zimmah: Ijarah may be future-dated (unlike sale). Specific asset not yet owned: only a promise to lease (advance rent allowed); buy, then lease. Sale and lease-back: sale first, not conditional.","keyPoints":["Future-dated Ijarah allowed; future-dated sale not.","Ijarah (unlike sale) may be made effective from a future date because ownership and risk stay with the lessor.","In sale and lease-back the sale contract must precede the lease and must not be a condition of it."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Future-dated lease","kind":"textbook","text":"On 1 January a landlord leases a completed house to a client with effect from 1 March. This is valid, although a sale effective from a future date is not, because the lessor keeps ownership and its risks until then (pp. 282–283)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A bank may sign the Ijarah of a specific asset before it owns the asset.","right":"For a specified asset, the lease cannot be executed before the lessor owns it; the bank should take a promise to lease and may take advance rent to be adjusted later (p. 283)."}],"relatedTopics":["t5.7","t11.5.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A bank has not yet bought a specific machine a client wants leased. What should it sign now?","options":["The Ijarah agreement","A promise to lease (advance rent may be taken)","A sale deed","Nothing at all"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 283."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch11-t4"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch11-t4","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"11.3.1","sectionTitle":"Execution of an Ijarah Contract","title":"Execution of an Ijarah Contract: Instant vs Future Enforcement","covers":["t11.3.1"],"overview":"Explains when an Ijarah contract can be signed for future enforcement (unlike Bai'), and why a specific but not-yet-owned asset requires special treatment via a promise to lease.","simpleExplanation":"Unlike a sale (which must happen immediately), a lease can be agreed now but take effect later, because ownership stays with the lessor the whole time anyway - the risk of the asset is still theirs. But there's a catch: if the lease is for one SPECIFIC asset that the bank doesn't yet own, they can't sign the actual lease contract until they've actually acquired it. In the meantime, they can only make a 'promise to lease,' and any advance rent taken isn't counted as real rent yet - it just gets applied later once rent actually starts.","academicExplanation":"Depending on the asset's nature, an Ijarah contract can be executed before or after the lessor takes possession, for instant or future enforcement/commencement. If the asset already exists (e.g. an existing liveable house), the lease contract can be for instant or future enforcement, since its usufruct is clear to both parties who can stipulate rental accordingly. Future enforcement is allowed in Ijarah (unlike Bai') because ownership remains with the lessor throughout, who bears damage risk. Similarly, in Ijarah Mosufah bil Zimmah (where the asset's nature/quality is specified rather than a particular unit, and destruction of one unit doesn't terminate the contract), the contract can be for instant or future enforcement. HOWEVER, if a PARTICULAR asset is specified for Ijarah, the lease contract cannot be executed before the lessor obtains ownership/possession of that asset (or its usufruct, in sub-lease cases) - if the asset is destroyed before this, the contract terminates. If the lessor does not yet own the asset, he can instead enter a PROMISE to lease with the prospective lessee; advance rent taken at this stage is NOT considered accrued rent - it is simply adjusted against rental becoming due later. Islamic banks' leases normally fall into this category. Per the promise, the lessor can purchase the asset from the market or, in special cases, from the promisee himself - in which case it becomes a 'sale and lease-back' arrangement, and the sale/purchase contract must precede the lease contract (the Ijarah contract must not be stipulated as a precondition of the sale contract).","examExplanation":"Existing/described (not asset-specific) leases: can be executed for instant OR future enforcement (ownership/risk stays with lessor throughout, so no Gharar issue like in Bai'). Particular/specific asset not yet owned: lease contract CANNOT be executed before the lessor acquires ownership/possession - instead, a PROMISE to lease is used, with any advance rent NOT counted as accrued rent (just adjusted against future rent). This 'promise' structure is how most Islamic bank leases work. Sale-and-lease-back: sale/purchase contract must precede the lease contract, and the Ijarah cannot be a precondition of the sale.","keyPoints":["Ijarah, unlike Bai', can be validly agreed for future enforcement/commencement - because ownership (and its risk) remains with the lessor throughout, unlike a sale where ownership transfer is definitive and immediate.","If a PARTICULAR, specific asset is the subject of the lease, the contract cannot be executed before the lessor actually obtains ownership/possession of that asset - if destroyed before then, the contract terminates.","When the lessor does not yet own the specific asset, a 'promise to lease' (not the actual lease contract) is used; advance rent taken at this stage is not accrued rent but is adjusted against rent becoming due later - this is the standard structure for most Islamic bank leases.","In sale-and-lease-back arrangements, the sale/purchase contract must precede the lease contract, and the lease cannot be stipulated as a precondition of the sale."],"definitions":[{"term":"Ijarah Mosufah bil Zimmah","definition":"A lease where the asset's nature/quality is specified (rather than a specific individual unit), such that destruction of a particular unit does not terminate the contract - the lessor must simply replace it with an asset of the agreed specification."}],"conditions":["A lease contract for a particular, specifically identified asset cannot be executed before the lessor obtains ownership/possession of that asset.","The sale/purchase contract in a sale-and-lease-back arrangement must precede the lease contract, and the lease must not be a precondition of the sale."],"principles":["Future enforcement of a lease is permissible (unlike Bai') because ownership and its associated risk remain with the lessor throughout, avoiding the Gharar concerns that would arise from a future-dated sale."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think all Ijarah contracts can be signed before the asset is acquired. This is only true for non-particularized/described assets (Ijarah Mosufah bil Zimmah) or already-owned assets - for a specific, particular asset not yet owned, only a promise to lease (not the actual contract) can precede acquisition."],"distinctions":["Ijarah Mosufah bil Zimmah (asset specified by description, can be leased before acquiring a specific unit) vs a lease on a particular identified asset (contract execution must wait until the lessor owns that specific asset)."],"relatedConcepts":["Bai' al 'Inah","Wakalah","Sale and Lease-Back"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.1","pages":[282,283]}}]},{"id":"t11.3.2","section":"11.3.2","title":"Determination of Rent (Benchmarks and Floating Rentals)","pages":[283,284],"tier":"core","concepts":["ijarah","benchmark"],"intuition":"Can Ijarah rent be linked to LIBOR or inflation?","simpleExplanation":"Rent may be based on the lessor’s total asset cost if agreed. Once fixed it can’t be raised unilaterally, but different rates for different phases, periodic increases (e.g. 5% a year), a well-defined benchmark, tax pass-through or inflation linkage may be agreed for long leases. A minority objects to LIBOR; the author: any benchmark can price a lease if the lessor bears asset risk, though non-interest benchmarks are desirable. First-period rent must be specified; a cap/floor (e.g. ±5%) avoids Gharar.","academicExplanation":["Determining rent on the aggregate cost incurred by the lessor is permissible if agreed and other conditions are met. After agreement the lessor cannot increase rent unilaterally. Different rates for phases based on an agreed benchmark are allowed; parties may review period or rent by consent. Long leases may provide increases after a period, tie rent to a variable well-defined benchmark, or enhance it by a stipulated proportion (e.g. 5% yearly) — benchmarks are recommended to avoid disputes given the binding contract (AAOIFI 5/2/3); rent may increase with property or government taxes, or with inflation (5% inflation → 5% rent rise).","LIBOR: a minority says it makes the transaction interest-like; the author says this is not correct — benchmarking to an interest rate does not render it Haram so long as Shari’ah requirements are met. Inflation, price indices, growth rates or real-sector returns can be used; scholars dislike interest benchmarks for periodic increases but allow them because the essential difference is that the lessor bears full risk of the corpus: if usufruct is lost without lessee fault, no rent is due; in interest-based lease finance the lessee bears ownership expenses. Floating rent is valid if the first period’s rent is specified; non-interest benchmarks are desirable. A ceiling/floor on changes (e.g. not more than 5%) avoids Gharar/Jahala (AAOIFI 5/2/5)."],"examExplanation":"Rent: may reflect asset cost; no unilateral increase; phased/benchmark/indexed rents for future periods allowed; LIBOR usable (author) but non-interest benchmarks preferred; first-period rent fixed; cap and floor.","keyPoints":["Essential difference from interest: lessor bears corpus risk.","Cap/floor e.g. ±5%."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Rent tied to inflation and taxes","kind":"textbook","text":"Rent may rise with the inflation rate (e.g. 5% inflation → 5% more rent) or with increases in property tax, if agreed in advance (p. 283)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Using LIBOR as a benchmark makes Ijarah interest-based.","right":"A benchmark only prices the rent; what distinguishes Ijarah is that the lessor bears ownership risk. Scholars prefer non-interest benchmarks, and a cap/floor avoids Gharar (p. 284)."}],"relatedTopics":["t4.2.5","t4.7.4","t17.4.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which condition must be met for a floating (benchmarked) Ijarah rental to be valid?","options":["The rent must never change","The rental for the first period must be specified","It must use LIBOR","The lessee bears asset risk"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 284."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch11-t5"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch11-t5","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"11.3.2","sectionTitle":"Determination of Rent","title":"Determination of Rent: Cost-Based Pricing, Benchmarks, and the LIBOR Debate","covers":["t11.3.2"],"overview":"Explains how rent can be based on the lessor's acquisition cost, how long-term leases can use variable benchmarks (including interest-rate benchmarks, controversially), and the Gharar-limiting ceiling/floor requirement.","simpleExplanation":"Banks are allowed to set rent based on what they paid for the asset, as long as both sides agree - and once fixed, the bank can't just raise the rent on its own. For long leases, rent can even be tied to something that changes over time, like inflation or a market benchmark - and surprisingly, the book argues that using an interest-rate benchmark like LIBOR just to set the NUMBER doesn't make the lease un-Islamic, as long as the lessor still genuinely bears the ownership risk (the real difference between a real lease and a disguised loan). To avoid disputes, any variable-rent formula should have a cap and floor so rent can't swing wildly.","academicExplanation":"Determining rental based on the lessor's aggregate cost of purchase/construction/installation is not against Shari'ah if both parties agree and all other valid-lease conditions are met. Once agreed, the lessor cannot unilaterally increase rent (any contrary agreement is void), though different rates for different phases can be agreed via a benchmark, and both parties can mutually agree during the lease to review the period or rental. In long-duration leases, scholars allow rent increases after specified periods, tying rent to a variable well-defined reference rate/benchmark, or periodic percentage increases (e.g. 5% yearly) - a well-defined benchmark/price index is recommended for subsequent periods to avoid dispute/injustice from market fluctuations, given the lease's binding nature. Rent can also be tied to tax increases or inflation rates. On the LIBOR question: a minority of scholars object that using an interest-rate benchmark makes the transaction resemble interest and is therefore impermissible - the author explicitly rejects this view, arguing that as long as basic Shari'ah requirements are met, ANY benchmark can be used to price sale or lease transactions; benchmarking to an interest-based rate does not itself render the transaction Haram. The rationale: the essential difference between valid lease and interest-based financing is that the LESSOR assumes full risk of the leased asset's corpus - if the asset loses usufruct without lessee misuse/negligence, the lessor cannot claim rent and bears the loss (whereas in interest-based lease financing, the lessee bears all ownership-related expense/responsibility). As long as this risk-bearing distinction is maintained, using an interest-rate index as a mere pricing benchmark does not recategorize the transaction as interest-bearing - though it is DESIRABLE to prefer non-interest benchmarks so an Islamic transaction is visibly distinguished. A floating rental structure is valid provided the FIRST period's rental amount is specified. To avoid Gharar/Jahala, scholars recommend a ceiling/floor limiting how much the rent-benchmark relationship can swing (e.g. rent cannot increase or decrease by more than 5%).","examExplanation":"Rent can be based on lessor's aggregate cost (mutual agreement required); once fixed, cannot be unilaterally increased. Long-term leases can use benchmarks (inflation, price index, or even LIBOR) for periodic adjustment. LIBOR debate: minority view = impermissible (resembles interest); author's/majority contemporary view = PERMISSIBLE, because the essential Shari'ah distinction is the LESSOR bearing full ownership/corpus risk (unlike interest-based lease financing, where the lessee bears it) - benchmarking to an interest rate is just a pricing mechanism and doesn't itself create Riba, though non-interest benchmarks are preferred for optics/clarity. Floating rent requires the FIRST period's rent to be specified; a ceiling/floor cap (e.g. max 5% swing) is recommended to avoid Gharar/Jahala.","keyPoints":["Rent can be determined based on the lessor's aggregate acquisition cost, by mutual agreement; once fixed, the lessor cannot unilaterally increase it.","Long-term leases can validly use variable, well-defined benchmarks (inflation, price index, growth rate, or even interest-rate benchmarks like LIBOR) for periodic rent adjustment.","The author explicitly rejects the minority view that using an interest-rate benchmark (e.g. LIBOR) makes a lease resemble - and thus become - an interest-based transaction; benchmarking pricing to a rate does not itself render a transaction Haram.","The essential Shari'ah-compliance distinction between a valid lease and interest-based financing: the LESSOR must bear full ownership/corpus risk (losing the right to rent if the asset loses usufruct without lessee fault) - as long as this risk allocation holds, the benchmark choice does not affect Shari'ah compliance, though non-interest benchmarks are still preferable for clear distinction.","A floating rental structure is valid provided the rental amount for the FIRST period of the lease is specified.","To avoid Gharar/Jahala, a ceiling and floor should limit how much rent can move relative to the benchmark (e.g. no more than a 5% increase or decrease)."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["Once rent is mutually fixed, the lessor cannot unilaterally increase it.","A floating/benchmarked rental structure requires the first period's rental amount to be specified.","A ceiling and floor should be set to limit rent fluctuation relative to any reference benchmark, to avoid Gharar and Jahala."],"principles":["The essential distinction between a Shari'ah-compliant lease and interest-based financing is that the lessor bears full risk of the leased asset's corpus, regardless of what benchmark is used to price the rent."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students often assume that using LIBOR (an interest rate) as a lease pricing benchmark automatically makes an Ijarah non-compliant. The author explicitly argues this is NOT the correct view - what matters for Shari'ah compliance is genuine risk-bearing by the lessor, not the choice of pricing benchmark, though non-interest benchmarks remain preferable for clarity/optics."],"distinctions":["A valid Islamic lease (lessor bears full ownership/corpus risk, regardless of benchmark used) vs interest-based lease financing (lessee bears all ownership-related expense/responsibility, making it economically equivalent to a loan)."],"relatedConcepts":["Gharar","Jahala","Riba"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","pages":[283,284]},"calculations":[{"title":"Practice Example — generated for learning: Cost-Based Rent with a Benchmark Ceiling/Floor","formula":"First-period monthly rent = (Lessor's aggregate acquisition cost x agreed annual return rate) / 12; subsequent-period rent = benchmark-implied rent, capped by a mutually agreed ceiling/floor around the prior rent.","inputs":[{"label":"Lessor's aggregate acquisition cost of the leased asset","value":"$120,000"},{"label":"Agreed target annual return for year 1 (mutually agreed, cost-based)","value":"10% p.a."},{"label":"Agreed ceiling/floor collar on rent movement (Gharar/Jahala safeguard)","value":"±5% of the prior period's rent"},{"label":"Benchmark-implied target return for year 2 (e.g. from a rising reference rate)","value":"12% p.a."}],"steps":["Year 1 rent is fixed by mutual agreement on the lessor's aggregate cost: monthly rent = ($120,000 x 10%) / 12 = $1,000 per month - this is the 'first period's rental amount' that must be specified for a floating structure to be valid.","For year 2, suppose the agreed benchmark (e.g. a price index or reference rate) implies a higher target return of 12% p.a., which on its own would give a new monthly rent of ($120,000 x 12%) / 12 = $1,200.","Before applying it, check the agreed ceiling: rent may not move by more than 5% from the prior period's $1,000, so the ceiling = $1,000 x 1.05 = $1,050 and the floor = $1,000 x 0.95 = $950.","Since the benchmark-implied $1,200 exceeds the $1,050 ceiling, the actual year-2 rent is capped at $1,050, not the full $1,200 the benchmark would otherwise imply.","If instead the benchmark had implied a lower rent of, say, $900, it would be raised to the $950 floor rather than allowed to fall further."],"result":"Year 1 rent = $1,000/month (fixed by mutual agreement on cost). Year 2 rent is capped at $1,050/month (not the $1,200 the raw benchmark would imply), because the agreed ±5% ceiling/floor limits the swing.","interpretation":"This illustrates 11.3.2's three rules together: rent can be set from the lessor's aggregate cost by mutual agreement; a floating structure is valid once the first period's amount is fixed; and a ceiling/floor collar is what keeps benchmark-linked adjustments (even one based on an interest-rate-like reference) from producing the kind of open-ended Gharar/Jahala the scholars want to avoid. The book gives no worked numeric example in 11.3.2 itself - the numbers here are for practice only, though the mechanism (cost-based first rent, benchmark adjustment, capped collar) is exactly what the text describes.","generated":true,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","pages":[283,284]}}]}],"debate":[{"issue":"May Ijarah rentals be benchmarked to LIBOR?","criticism":"A minority of scholars: interest benchmarks make the transaction similar to interest and impermissible.","response":"The author and Usmani: as long as Shari’ah requirements (especially lessor bearing corpus risk) are met, any benchmark can price a lease; benchmarking to interest does not render it Haram.","alternative":"Scholars dislike interest benchmarks for periodic increases and recommend inflation, price indices or real-sector returns.","takeaway":"Permissible in principle; desirable to use non-interest benchmarks for distinctiveness."}]},{"id":"t11.3.3","section":"11.3.3","title":"Sub-lease by the Lessee","pages":[284,285],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["ijarah"],"intuition":"Can a lessee sublet at a higher rent and keep the difference?","simpleExplanation":"Sub-lease is permissible with the lessor’s consent. All schools allow it at equal or lower rent. Higher rent: Shafi‘is and Hanbalis allow keeping the surplus; Abu Hanifa requires giving it to charity unless the sub-lessor improved the property or used a different currency. Contemporary scholars prefer the Shafi‘i/Hanbali view. But selling rights to share rentals without transferring ownership is selling a receivable at a discount — Riba.","academicExplanation":["Sub-lease is permissible with the lessor’s consent (can be in the agreement). All schools permit it if the sub-rent is equal to or less than the rent paid. If higher: Shafi‘is allow the sub-lessor to keep the surplus (preferred Hanbali view too); Abu Hanifa: surplus to charity, unless the sub-lessor developed the property or rented it in a different currency. Contemporary scholars prefer the Shafi‘i and Hanbali view (AAOIFI 3/3). However, if a lessee sub-leases to many and invites others to share rentals without transferring ownership, or charges them a specified amount to participate, this is not acceptable — assigning the right to receive rent for payment amounts to selling a receivable at a discount, which is Riba."],"examExplanation":"Sub-lease with consent; higher rent surplus: Shafi‘i/Hanbali — keep (preferred); Abu Hanifa — charity unless improved/different currency. Selling rent-sharing rights without ownership = Riba.","keyPoints":["Rent receivables cannot be sold at a discount."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Sub-letting at a higher rent","kind":"textbook","text":"A lessee pays 20,000 a month and, with the lessor’s consent, sub-lets for 25,000. On the Shafi‘i and Hanbali view preferred today, he may keep the 5,000. Abu Hanifa would send it to charity unless he improved the property or charged in another currency (pp. 284–285)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A lessee may sell shares in future rentals to investors without selling any part of the asset.","right":"That assigns a receivable for money — selling debt at a discount, which is Riba (p. 285)."}],"relatedTopics":["t11.5.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to contemporary scholars, can a sub-lessor keep a surplus when sub-renting at a higher rent?","options":["No, it must go to charity in all cases","Yes — the Shafi‘i/Hanbali view is preferred","Only if the lessor agrees to share","Only for land"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 284."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch11-t6"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch11-t6","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"11.3.3-11.3.5","sectionTitle":"Sub-lease by the Lessee / Security/Guarantee in Ijarah / Liabilities of the Parties","title":"Sub-leasing, Security, and Liability Allocation Between Lessor and Lessee","covers":["t11.3.3","t11.3.4","t11.3.5"],"overview":"Covers the permissibility of sub-leasing (and the scholarly dispute over keeping a sub-lease profit margin), the lessor's right to demand security/guarantees, and the clear-cut liability split between ownership expenses (lessor) and usage expenses (lessee).","simpleExplanation":"A lessee can generally sub-lease an asset to someone else with the owner's permission. If they charge the sub-lessee the same or less than what they themselves pay, everyone agrees that's fine. But if they charge MORE and pocket the difference, scholars disagree - some (Hanafi) say that extra profit must go to charity, while others (Shafi'i, Hanbali - and the view the book prefers) say the sub-lessor can keep it. On costs: anything related to owning the asset (like major repairs, taxes) is the landlord/bank's job; day-to-day running costs (like electricity, routine upkeep) are the tenant's job. The bank can ask for a security deposit to protect itself, but if the tenant defaults, it can only recover its ACTUAL loss - never a bonus on top.","academicExplanation":"Sub-lease is permissible in principle, subject to the lessor's consent (which can be provided for in the lease agreement). All recognized schools agree sub-lease is valid if the sub-lease rent equals or is less than what the sub-lessor pays the original lessor. Views diverge where sub-lease rent EXCEEDS the original rent: Shafi'is and (as the preferred Hanbali view) allow the sub-lessor to keep the surplus; Imam Abu Hanifa holds the surplus must go to charity - UNLESS the sub-lessor has developed/improved the property or rents it in a different currency than what he pays the original lessor, in which case he may keep the surplus. Contemporary scholars prefer the Shafi'i/Hanbali view - sub-leasing is permissible regardless of rental amount. However, if a lessee sub-leases to MULTIPLE sub-lessees or invites others to merely SHARE rental income (without transferring any ownership portion) for a fee, this is NOT acceptable - it amounts to trading a receivable (the right to receive rent) at a discount, which is Riba. On security: since Ijarah creates a debt (payable rental), the lessor can demand security/guarantee from the lessee; taking security is also justified because the leased asset is held by the lessee as a trustee (fiduciary), who can be held liable for damage proven to result from his negligence. In cases of such loss or rental payment default, the lessor can recover the ACTUAL loss (excluding cost-of-funds/opportunity cost) from the security - any amount recovered as income beyond this would be Riba. On liabilities: a lessee/employee bears NO liability except where proven transgression, wilful waste, or damage - in which case he must compensate the lessor or replace the property. All ownership-derived liabilities fall on the lessor; usage-related liabilities fall on the lessee (e.g. for a leased house: property taxes = owner's responsibility; water/electricity/day-to-day usage expenses = lessee's responsibility). Contemporary Shari'ah experts link the lessor's expense obligation to whatever is customarily understood as necessary for the asset's continued performance/usufruct, with the parties free to agree otherwise. The leased property remains at the LESSOR's risk throughout the lease for factors beyond the lessee's control; the lessee is only liable to compensate for harm from his own misuse/negligence. A jointly-owned property can be leased out, with risk and rental distributed among co-owners per their ownership proportion.","examExplanation":"Sub-lease: permissible with lessor's consent. Sub-lease rent <= original rent: universally allowed. Sub-lease rent > original rent (surplus kept by sub-lessor): Shafi'i/Hanbali (preferred contemporary view) = allowed to keep surplus; Hanafi (Abu Hanifa) = surplus must go to charity UNLESS the sub-lessor improved the property or rents in a different currency. Sharing rental income across MULTIPLE sub-lessees without transferring ownership = NOT allowed (amounts to selling a receivable at a discount = Riba). Security: lessor can demand security/guarantee (Ijarah creates rental debt); recovery limited to ACTUAL loss (excluding opportunity cost/cost-of-funds) - any excess = Riba. Liability split: OWNERSHIP-related (taxes, major repairs) = LESSOR; USAGE-related (utilities, day-to-day upkeep) = LESSEE. Lessee liable ONLY for proven negligence/misuse/wilful damage; otherwise, asset stays at lessor's risk throughout.","keyPoints":["Sub-leasing is permissible with the lessor's consent; all schools agree it's valid when sub-lease rent equals or is less than the original rent.","When sub-lease rent exceeds the original rent, Shafi'i and (preferred) Hanbali views allow the sub-lessor to keep the surplus; the Hanafi view requires the surplus go to charity, UNLESS the sub-lessor improved the property or charges in a different currency than his own rent payment.","Sharing rental income across multiple sub-lessees WITHOUT transferring any ownership portion is not permissible - this amounts to selling the right to receive rent (a receivable) at a discount, which is Riba.","Ownership-related expenses/liabilities (e.g. property taxes, major repairs) fall on the LESSOR; usage-related expenses (e.g. utilities, day-to-day operation) fall on the LESSEE.","The lessee bears NO liability for asset loss/damage except where proven transgression, wilful waste, or negligence on his part - otherwise, the asset remains at the lessor's risk throughout the lease.","The lessor can demand security from the lessee, but on default/damage can recover only the ACTUAL loss (excluding cost-of-funds/opportunity cost) - any amount recovered beyond actual loss is Riba."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["Sub-leasing requires the lessor's consent.","Sharing rental income among multiple parties without transferring any ownership portion of the asset is not permissible.","Recovery from security on lessee default is limited to the lessor's actual loss, excluding cost-of-funds or opportunity cost."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think all schools agree a sub-lessor can never profit from sub-leasing. This is only the strict Hanafi position (absent property improvement or currency difference); the Shafi'i/Hanbali view - preferred by contemporary scholars - permits keeping the surplus freely."],"distinctions":["A genuine sub-lease (transferring usufruct, sub-lessor bears some responsibility) vs merely selling/sharing the right to receive rental income without any ownership transfer (prohibited, amounts to selling a receivable at a discount)."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Bai' al Dayn","Takaful"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.3-11.3.5","pages":[284,285,286]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Abu Hanifa","b":"Shafi‘is and Hanbalis (preferred today)","text":"Abu Hanifa: surplus rent from a sub-lease goes to charity unless the lessee improved the property or rents in another currency; Shafi‘is/Hanbalis: the sub-lessor may keep the surplus (p. 284)."}]},{"id":"t11.3.4","section":"11.3.4","title":"Security/Guarantee in Ijarah","pages":[285,285],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["ijarah","rihn"],"intuition":"Can a lessor take security, and what can it recover from it?","simpleExplanation":"Because rent due is a debt and the lessee holds the asset as a trustee, the lessor can demand security or a guarantee. On negligence or default it recovers actual loss (not cost of funds/opportunity cost); anything above due rent would be Riba.","academicExplanation":["Ijarah creates a debt of payable rent, so the lessor may demand security/guarantee; also permissible because the lessee holds the asset as trustee and is liable for proven negligence. On such loss or rent default, the lessor recovers actual loss, excluding cost of funds or opportunity cost, from the security; any amount taken as income over due rent would be Riba (AAOIFI 5/2/5, 6/1–6/3, 7/1/4)."],"examExplanation":"Security allowed; recover only actual loss/due rent — no cost of funds; excess = Riba.","keyPoints":["Opportunity cost excluded."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Recovering from the security deposit","kind":"textbook","text":"A lessee defaults on 60,000 of rent; the lessor holds a 100,000 deposit. It may deduct the 60,000 owed and any actual loss, but not a cost-of-funds charge, and must return the rest (p. 285)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A lessor may recover cost of funds from the lessee’s security on default.","right":"Only the actual loss can be recovered; anything above due rent is Riba (p. 285)."}],"relatedTopics":["t7.15","t11.3.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"From the lessee’s security, a lessor may recover:","options":["Rent for the remaining lease period plus cost of funds","Actual loss/due rent only, excluding opportunity cost","Any amount it chooses","Nothing"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 285."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t11.3.5","section":"11.3.5","title":"Liabilities of the Parties","pages":[285,285],"tier":"core","concepts":["ijarah","risk-sharing"],"intuition":"Who pays for what during a lease?","simpleExplanation":"The lessee is liable only for misuse or negligence. Ownership liabilities (property taxes, major repairs, risk of loss beyond the lessee’s control) are the lessor’s; usage liabilities (water, electricity, running costs) are the lessee’s. Shari’ah boards allow customary expenses to be allocated by free agreement. Joint owners share risk and rent in proportion to shares.","academicExplanation":["No liability on a lessee or employee unless transgression, wilful waste or damage is established; then he compensates or replaces. Ownership liabilities are the lessor’s; use liabilities the lessee’s — e.g. property taxes on the owner, water tax and electricity on the tenant (AAOIFI 5/1/7). Contemporary Shari’ah boards link the owner’s responsibility for expenses on which continued performance depends to custom and free agreement, so contrary agreement may shift them. The leased property remains at the lessor’s risk throughout: harm beyond the lessee’s control is the lessor’s, misuse or negligence the lessee’s. A jointly owned property may be leased; owners bear risk and share rent pro rata."],"examExplanation":"Lessor: ownership liabilities (taxes on property, major repairs, loss beyond lessee control). Lessee: usage costs and negligence. Joint owners pro rata.","keyPoints":["Risk of loss without lessee fault stays with lessor throughout.","Jointly owned assets can be leased; risk and rent are shared by ownership proportions."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Leased house bills","kind":"textbook","text":"Property taxes fall on the owner; water, electricity and other use-related bills fall on the lessee (p. 285)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t4.2.9","t11.4.4","t11.5.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In a house lease, which cost is the lessor’s responsibility?","options":["Electricity bills","Water tax","Property taxes relating to ownership","Day-to-day cleaning"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 285."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"table":{"caption":"Allocation of liabilities in Ijarah","head":["Lessor (owner)","Lessee (user)"],"rows":[["Property/ownership taxes","Water, electricity, running costs"],["Major repairs, defects preventing use","Day-to-day maintenance"],["Loss/destruction without lessee fault","Loss due to misuse or negligence"],["Takaful of the asset (can be priced into rent by consent)","Following policy conditions"]]}},{"id":"t11.3.6","section":"11.3.6","title":"Termination/Amendment and Failure in Payment of Rent","pages":[286,287],"tier":"core","concepts":["ijarah","late-payment-penalty"],"intuition":"When does a lease end — and can the lessor demand the rest of the rent if it ends early?","simpleExplanation":"Ijarah is binding; no unilateral termination, but amendment or termination by consent. It ends if the asset can no longer give usufruct (unless Mosufah bil Zimmah — replace the unit). Sale to the lessee ends it; sale to a third party transfers it. Death does not end it (heirs of the lessee may end it if too onerous). The lessor may terminate for the lessee’s contravention — but can claim only due rent (plus negligence damages), never rent for the remaining period. Advance rent for an unexpired period is refunded on force majeure. Late rent: no increase (Riba); a charity undertaking may be stipulated; banks should rely on repossession or collateral.","academicExplanation":["11.3.6: Ijarah is binding; a unilateral unconditional termination option is un-Islamic; amendment or termination by mutual consent. If damaged so as to give no usufruct, the contract ends; if an impediment prevents the normal objective, the lessee may terminate; rent may be adjusted for partial damage. In Ijarah Mosufah bil Zimmah the lessor replaces the unit. If the lessee stops using the asset without consent, rent continues. Sale to the lessee terminates it; sale to a third party does not — rights and obligations transfer; the lessee’s consent is not required; an uninformed purchaser may cancel. Contemporary jurists: death of either party does not end Ijarah, though the lessee’s heirs may terminate if too onerous. The lessor may provide for termination on contravention; the lessee then pays due rent, not the remaining period’s rent. Conventional leases requiring remaining rent on termination reveal an interest-bearing loan under the cover of lease — against Shari’ah. On termination the lessor takes the asset back; negligence damages may be claimed; default may be a termination ground. Advance rent for the unexpired period is refunded on force majeure or mutual dissolution, unless the lessee fails to return the asset.","11.3.7: due rent is a debt; any charge on it is Riba. Scholars allow a donation/penalty to charity, stipulated ab initio, varying with period of default and possibly a percentage per annum; it must not be the lessor’s income. Hence banks should include a clause allowing repossession or enforcement of collateral for wilful default."],"examExplanation":"Binding; mutual termination; ends on loss of usufruct (replace unit if Mosufah bil Zimmah); not ended by death or sale to third party. On lessor termination for breach: due rent + negligence damages only — no remaining-period rent. Late rent: charity undertaking; repossession/collateral.","keyPoints":["Remaining-period rent clause = hallmark of disguised interest.","Advance rent refunded on force majeure.","Rent continues if lessee stops using without consent.","If the lessor sells the asset to a third party, the lease continues with the buyer; the lessee’s consent is not needed.","Late-payment penalties go to charity; the bank may instead repossess or enforce collateral for wilful default."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Termination for breach","kind":"textbook","text":"If the lessor terminates because the lessee breached the agreement, the lessee pays rent due to that date (and any damage from negligence) — never the rent for the remaining period, which conventional leases often demand (p. 286)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"The death of either party ends an Ijarah.","right":"Contemporary jurists hold it does not end, though the lessee’s heirs may terminate if the rent becomes too onerous (p. 286)."}],"relatedTopics":["t7.13","t11.5.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A lessor terminates an Ijarah because the lessee breached the agreement. What can the lessor claim?","options":["Rent for the entire remaining period","Rent due up to termination (plus damages for negligence), not future rent","Double rent","Interest on unpaid rent"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 286."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch11-t7"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch11-t7","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"11.3.6-11.3.7","sectionTitle":"Termination/Amendment of the Contract and Implications / Failure in Payment of Due Rent","title":"Termination, Amendment, and Rent Default Rules in Ijarah","covers":["t11.3.6"],"overview":"Explains why Ijarah is a binding contract that can't be unilaterally terminated at will, the specific events that do trigger termination, and the charity-bound (not bank-income) penalty for rent default.","simpleExplanation":"A lease is a firm commitment - neither side can just cancel it whenever they feel like it, unless both agree, or something specific happens (like the asset being destroyed, or the tenant breaking the contract terms). If the asset is fully destroyed, the lease ends. If the customer buys the asset, the lease ends (they now own it). If it's sold to someone else instead, the lease just continues with the new owner. If a tenant stops paying rent, the bank can end the lease and take the asset back - but it can only collect rent already owed, never rent for the remaining unused period, and any late-payment penalty has to go to charity, not the bank's profit.","academicExplanation":"Ijarah is fundamentally a binding contract - unilateral, unconditional termination by either party is contrary to justice/equity and thus un-Islamic; both parties CAN amend or terminate with mutual consent. The contract terminates automatically if the leased asset is damaged to the point of no longer yielding usufruct. If there's an impediment to achieving the contract's normally expected objective, the lessee can terminate - in which case parties may adjust rental for fairness given partial damage/possible beneficial use. For Ijarah Mosufah bil Zimmah (unidentified unit leased), destruction doesn't terminate the contract - the lessor must replace with an asset of agreed specification. If the lessee simply stops using the asset without the lessor's consent, rental accrual continues regardless. If the asset is sold TO THE LESSEE, Ijarah terminates (ownership transfer). If sold to a THIRD PARTY, the contract does NOT terminate - rights/obligations transfer to the purchaser (if uninformed of the lease, the purchaser can terminate the sale; if informed and consenting, he steps into the previous owner's shoes) - the lessee's consent is not required for such a sale. Contemporary jurists (departing from earlier disagreement) hold Ijarah does NOT terminate upon either party's death, though the lessee's heirs can terminate if the contract becomes too onerous for their resources. The lessor MAY provide a right to terminate for lessee contract breach - in which case the lessee owes only the rent already DUE, not rent for the remaining period (unlike conventional leases, which often require full/partial remaining-period rent on termination - a structure the author identifies as effectively disguising an interest-bearing loan as a lease, and therefore against Shari'ah/equity). Upon termination, the lessor takes the asset back; the lessee pays rent due up to the termination date, plus compensation if termination resulted from his misuse/negligence - but never rent for the remaining unused period. If the lessee prepaid rent for a period and must return the asset due to force majeure, the unused advance rent must be refunded (the lease is deemed validly dissolved); the same applies for mutual dissolution. If the asset is NOT returned (enabling the bank to re-lease), the remainder is not refunded. On rent default: due rental constitutes a debt subject to all debt rules - a charge on overdue rental is Riba, prohibited. To deter wilful default, scholars allow a pre-agreed donation/penalty payable to CHARITY (varying by default period, e.g. a percentage per annum) - any amount over the agreed rental must never become part of the lessor's income; it must go to charity. Since this penalty cannot be bank income, it's advisable to include a clause allowing the bank to repossess the asset or enforce collateral on wilful default, as the primary recovery mechanism.","examExplanation":"Ijarah = binding contract; unilateral unconditional termination by either party = un-Islamic. Automatic termination triggers: total asset destruction (loses all usufruct), sale TO the lessee (ownership transfer). NOT terminated by: sale to a third party (obligations transfer to new owner), death of either party (contemporary view - though heirs may terminate if too onerous), lessee simply not using the asset (rent still accrues). Breach-based termination: lessor may reserve a right to terminate for lessee's breach, but can only collect rent ALREADY DUE, never remaining-period rent (conventional leases requiring full remaining rent = disguised interest loan, against Shari'ah). Force majeure/mutual dissolution: unused advance rent must be refunded. Rent default: charging extra on overdue rent = Riba (prohibited); a charity-bound penalty clause is allowed as deterrent (never bank income); repossession/collateral enforcement is the bank's real recovery tool.","keyPoints":["Ijarah is a binding contract; unilateral, unconditional termination by either party is not permitted - amendment/termination requires mutual consent, except for specific recognized triggers.","The contract automatically terminates if the leased asset is totally destroyed (loses all usufruct), or if the asset is sold TO the lessee (ownership transfer ends the lease relationship).","If the leased asset is sold to a THIRD PARTY, the Ijarah continues - rights/obligations transfer to the new owner, without requiring the lessee's consent.","On lessee breach, the lessor may terminate and repossess, but can only collect rent ALREADY DUE - never rent for the remaining, unused lease period; requiring full remaining-period rent (as conventional leases often do) effectively disguises an interest-bearing loan as a lease, which the author identifies as un-Islamic.","Charging any extra amount on overdue rental as bank income is Riba and prohibited - a charity-bound penalty clause (proceeds never becoming bank income) is the permitted deterrent, alongside repossession or collateral enforcement as the bank's real recovery mechanism.","If a lessee has prepaid rent and must return the asset due to force majeure or mutual dissolution, the unused portion of the advance rent must be refunded to him."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["Unilateral, unconditional termination of Ijarah by either party is not permitted; termination requires mutual consent or a recognized triggering event.","On lessee breach and lease termination, only rent already due up to the termination date can be collected - never rent for the remaining unused period.","Any penalty charged for late rent payment must be directed to charity, never becoming part of the lessor's (bank's) income."],"principles":["Requiring a defaulting lessee to pay rent for the remaining, unused lease period effectively converts the lease into a disguised interest-bearing loan, contrary to Shari'ah principles of justice and equity."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students often assume that, like conventional leases, an Islamic bank can demand the full remaining-period rent upon early termination due to lessee default. This is explicitly identified in the book as un-Islamic - the bank can only collect rent already due, plus compensation for actual damage caused by misuse/negligence, never the unearned remaining-period rent."],"distinctions":["Sale of the leased asset TO the lessee (terminates Ijarah, since ownership transfers) vs sale to a THIRD PARTY (Ijarah continues, obligations transfer to the new owner)."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Charity Account","Force Majeure"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.6-11.3.7","pages":[286,287]}}],"subsections":[{"number":"11.3.6","title":"Termination/Amendment of the Contract","page":286,"points":["Binding; mutual consent","Destruction; Mosufah bil Zimmah","Sale; death","No remaining-period rent"]},{"number":"11.3.7","title":"Failure in Payment of Due Rent","page":287,"points":["Rent = debt; no increase","Charity penalty","Repossession/collateral"]}]},{"id":"t11.4","section":"11.4","title":"Modern Use of Ijarah: Financial, Security and Operating Leases","pages":[287,289],"tier":"core","concepts":["ijarah","conventional-leasing"],"intuition":"How do conventional leases work — and why are most of them disguised loans?","simpleExplanation":"Leasing began as real business but is widely used as finance (tax benefits). Financial lease/hire-purchase: long term, rent = cost + interest, starts when the lessor pays the supplier, lessee bears risk, prepayment fines, compound interest on default. Security/financing lease: a disguised security agreement transferring all risks to the lessee. Operating lease: lessor retains ownership and takes back the asset — compatible with Shari’ah if other conditions are met; suits aircraft and ships.","academicExplanation":["Leasing is a normal real-sector activity, used for financing largely due to tax concessions; in Islamic finance it matters also because of the asset-based nature of investment; bank leasing is governed by the rules of Ijarah. Forms used by NBFIs and banks (e.g. in Germany and Japan): financial lease (hire–purchase), financing (security) lease and operating lease.","11.4.1 Financial lease/hire–purchase: period ≈ useful life to amortise cost with market return; rent computed as total cost plus stipulated interest divided by months; lease begins when the price is paid, even before delivery; ownership risk on the lessee; the lessor has no further interest; lessee buys at a set or market price; not cancellable; prepurchase with a fine (e.g. 5% of remaining funds); asset is security, repossessed without court order; tax depreciation benefit; lessor can sell the equipment. Compound interest on default; outcome can be worse than a credit purchase — the lessee must keep paying even if he no longer needs the asset and cannot sell it.","11.4.2 Security or financing lease: a financing transaction and disguised security agreement, transferring all ownership risks and rewards to the lessee. 11.4.3 Operating lease: owner gives possession for rent, retains ownership and takes it back at the end — fully compatible with Shari’ah if other conditions are fulfilled; suits high-cost, long-production assets (aircraft, ships); rarely used by banks; NBFIs maintain assets for re-lease and bear obsolescence, recession and demand risk."],"examExplanation":"Financial lease/hire-purchase: cost+interest rent from payment date, lessee bears risk, prepayment fine, compound interest → non-compliant. Security lease: disguised loan. Operating lease: compatible if conditions met (aircraft, ships).","keyPoints":["Conventional rent may start before delivery — prohibited in Ijarah.","Operating lease closest to classical Ijarah."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Stuck with a five-year lease","kind":"textbook","text":"Under a conventional five-year lease a lessee who no longer needs the asset after two years must keep paying and cannot sell it, whereas a credit buyer could sell the asset to repay (p. 288)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t11.4.4","t11.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which conventional lease does the author consider fully compatible with Shari’ah if other conditions are met?","options":["Hire–purchase","Security lease","Operating lease","Finance lease with fixed residual value"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 289."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch11-t8"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch11-t8","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"11.4-11.4.3","sectionTitle":"Modern Use of Ijarah / Financial Lease or Hire-Purchase / Security or Financing Lease / Operating Lease","title":"Modern Forms of Leasing: Financial Lease, Security Lease, and Operating Lease","covers":["t11.4"],"overview":"Surveys three conventional leasing structures - financial lease/hire-purchase, security/financing lease, and operating lease - as background for identifying which features are or aren't Shari'ah-compliant.","simpleExplanation":"There are three common types of leasing in the conventional finance world. A 'financial lease' (or hire-purchase) is really a financing arrangement dressed up as a lease - the bank calculates rent to recover its full cost plus a return, and the customer ends up buying the asset. A 'security lease' is basically a loan disguised as a lease, with all risk shifted to the customer. An 'operating lease' is the most genuine kind - the owner keeps the asset and takes it back at the end, and it's used for expensive, long-lived assets like aircraft and ships.","academicExplanation":"Leasing forms a sizeable part of global financial services; originally a normal real-sector business activity (like sale, not financing), it has been widely adopted for financing purposes due to tax concessions in many countries. For Islamic finance, leasing operations are governed by Fiqh Ijarah rules. NBFIs/NBFCs worldwide, and banks in countries like Germany and Japan, use three main leasing forms: financial lease (hire-purchase), financing lease (security lease), and operating lease. Financial Lease/Hire-Purchase: the lease period typically spans the asset's entire useful life, letting the lessor amortize cost plus a market return; banks pay the supplier (directly or via the lessee), calculate total cost plus stipulated interest, divide by total lease months for the monthly rent, and the lease commences on the day PRICE IS PAID (regardless of whether the lessee has taken delivery) - meaning rent liability starts even before delivery, and ownership risk is borne by the LESSEE throughout (unlike Islamic Ijarah). The lessor recovers cost+interest and has no further interest in the asset; the lessee purchases at a predetermined or market price; the lease is not cancellable before expiry without mutual consent, though prepurchase is normally allowed (often with an early-termination fine, e.g. 5% of remaining funds). The leased asset serves as security (repossession without court order on default); high depreciation allowances offer lessor tax benefits; the lessor can also sell the equipment mid-lease with rental payments accruing to the buyer. Compound interest on default/delay can make financial lease MORE exploitative than an outright credit purchase (e.g. a 5-year lessee who no longer needs the asset after 2 years must keep paying, unlike a credit buyer who could sell the asset to clear the debt). Security/Financing Lease: purely a financing transaction - a disguised security agreement for the amount financed, involving effective transfer of ALL ownership risks/rewards to the lessee. Operating Lease: the owner retains ownership, giving only possession/use to the lessee for rental; the lessor takes the asset back at lease end. This structure is fully compatible with Shari'ah (subject to other conditions), and is particularly suited to high-cost, long-production-time assets (aircraft, ships). Rarely used by banks; NBFIs sometimes use it for specialized machinery, maintaining asset inventories to re-lease repeatedly, bearing obsolescence/recession/demand-decline risk themselves.","examExplanation":"Three conventional lease types: (1) Financial lease/hire-purchase - full cost+interest recovery, rent starts from PAYMENT date (not delivery), lessee bears ALL ownership risk, ends in ownership transfer; can be MORE exploitative than credit purchase due to compound interest and inability to exit by reselling. (2) Security/financing lease - pure disguised financing (security agreement), full risk/reward transferred to lessee. (3) Operating lease - lessor retains ownership, bears risk, takes asset back at end; fully Shari'ah-compatible structure (subject to other conditions); suited to high-cost/long-life assets (aircraft, ships); rarely used by banks (more common among NBFIs for specialized machinery).","keyPoints":["Financial lease (hire-purchase): rent starts accruing from the date the LESSOR PAYS the supplier (not from delivery to the lessee), and ownership risk is borne by the lessee throughout - both features conflict with Shari'ah Ijarah principles.","Financial lease can be MORE exploitative than an outright credit purchase, since compound interest often applies on default/delay, and the lessee cannot exit early by simply reselling the asset (unlike a credit-purchase buyer).","Security/financing lease is, in substance, purely a disguised financing/security agreement - all ownership risks and rewards effectively transfer to the lessee, making it economically equivalent to interest-based lending.","Operating lease - where the lessor retains ownership and bears its risk throughout, taking the asset back at lease end - is the conventional lease structure most compatible with Shari'ah, subject to other conditions being met.","Operating lease particularly suits high-cost, long-production-time assets (aircraft, ships); it is used more often by NBFIs (for specialized machinery, bearing obsolescence/demand risk) than by banks."],"definitions":[{"term":"Financial Lease (Hire-Purchase)","definition":"A conventional lease structure where rent is calculated to fully amortize the lessor's cost plus a market return over the asset's useful life, with the lessee bearing all ownership risk and typically ending in ownership transfer."},{"term":"Operating Lease","definition":"A lease where the lessor retains ownership and bears its associated risk throughout, giving the lessee only possession/use for rental, and reclaims the asset at lease end."}],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume any conventional 'lease' is structurally close to Islamic Ijarah. In fact, financial lease and security lease both shift full ownership risk to the lessee and start rent accrual before delivery - both directly conflicting with core Shari'ah Ijarah requirements; only operating lease is structurally compatible."],"distinctions":["Financial/security lease (all ownership risk on lessee, rent starts at payment - not compliant as-is) vs operating lease (ownership risk stays with lessor, structurally compatible with Shari'ah)."],"relatedConcepts":["Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek","Ownership Risk","Riba"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4-11.4.3","pages":[287,288,289]}}],"subsections":[{"number":"11.4.1","title":"Financial Lease or Hire–Purchase","page":288,"points":["Rent = cost + interest","Starts at payment","Lessee bears risk"]},{"number":"11.4.2","title":"Security or Financing Lease","page":288,"points":["Disguised security agreement"]},{"number":"11.4.3","title":"Operating Lease","page":289,"points":["Compatible with Shari’ah","Lessor bears obsolescence risk"]}],"table":{"caption":"Conventional lease forms (pp. 288–289)","head":["Form","Key feature","Shari’ah view"],"rows":[["Financial lease / hire–purchase","Rent from payment date; lessee bears ownership risk; sale built in","Not acceptable as structured"],["Security (financing) lease","Disguised secured loan; all risks on lessee","Not acceptable"],["Operating lease","Lessor keeps ownership; asset returned at end","Compatible, if lessor bears ownership costs"]]}},{"id":"t11.4.4","section":"11.4.4","title":"Appraisal of Conventional Leases from the Shari’ah Angle","pages":[289,289],"tier":"core","concepts":["ijarah","conventional-leasing"],"intuition":"Four features of modern leases — which break Shari’ah rules?","simpleExplanation":"(1) Ownership transfers at expiry (hire-purchase includes sale from the start). (2) Rent starts when funds are paid to the supplier — Shari’ah: only once the lessee can use the asset. (3) All risks shifted to the lessee, residual value fixed in advance — Shari’ah: lessor bears defects preventing use, lessee only day-to-day maintenance. (4) Even conventional operating leases put risks on the lessee; in Islamic operating leases the lessor bears upkeep and ownership costs, for a specified period.","academicExplanation":["(1) At expiry ownership transfers free or at a nominal price; in hire–purchase the contract includes sale from the start and payments include rent and cost; in finance leases transfer may or may not be agreed. (2) The lessor charges rent as soon as he pays the supplier — leasing before buying and possessing, getting reward without ownership risk; in Shari’ah rent starts from the date the lessee can benefit, after delivery. (3) The lessor shifts all risks to the lessee, especially when residual value is fixed; in Shari’ah, expenses to rectify defects preventing use are the lessor’s, day-to-day maintenance the lessee’s — the major difference. (4) Conventional operating leases also place risks and expenses on the lessee; Islamic operating leases require the lessor to bear upkeep, risks and costs of ownership, and are for a specified period, not the entire useful life, ending unless renewed by consent."],"examExplanation":"Problems: sale built into lease; rent before delivery; all risk on lessee/fixed residual; lessee bears costs even in operating lease. Islamic: separate transfer; rent after usable delivery; lessor bears corpus risk; specified period.","keyPoints":["Major difference: who rectifies defects preventing use."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"When rent starts","kind":"textbook","text":"Under a conventional lease, rent starts the day the lessor pays the supplier. Under Shari’ah rules, rent starts only once the asset is delivered and the lessee can use it (p. 289)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"In an Islamic operating lease the lessee bears all risks and expenses, as in conventional practice.","right":"The Islamic lessor must bear upkeep of the corpus and all ownership risks; the lessee bears day-to-day running costs (p. 289)."}],"relatedTopics":["t11.5","t11.5.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"From when may an Islamic lessor charge rent?","options":["When funds are released to the supplier","When the lessee is able to benefit from the delivered asset","When the MoU is signed","When the L/C is opened"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 289."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch11-t9"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch11-t9","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"11.4.4","sectionTitle":"Appraisal of Conventional Leases from the Shari'ah Angle","title":"Appraisal of Conventional Leases from the Shari'ah Angle","covers":["t11.4.4"],"overview":"Pinpoints the four specific features of conventional leases that clash with Shari'ah - ownership transfer method, when rent starts accruing, risk-shifting to the lessee, and expense allocation.","simpleExplanation":"The book identifies exactly what's wrong with conventional leases from an Islamic perspective: (1) ownership transfer is often baked into the lease from day one instead of being a separate later decision; (2) rent starts accruing the moment the bank pays for the asset, even before the customer can actually use it - but Islamic law says rent should only start once the customer can actually benefit from the asset; (3) all the risk gets pushed onto the customer, when Islamic law says the owner (lessor) should bear major repair costs, not just day-to-day upkeep; (4) even in conventional 'operating' leases, all risk sits with the lessee, whereas an Islamic operating lease keeps ownership risk with the lessor.","academicExplanation":"Four important features of most modern (conventional) leases, assessed against Shari'ah: (1) At hiring-contract expiry, ownership of the leased article transfers to the lessee, free or at nominal/token price - particularly in hire-purchase, it's mutually agreed AT THE OUTSET that the Ijarah contract itself includes the sale, with periodic payments comprising both rental and asset cost; in finance lease, periodic payments are purely rental, though the parties may or may not agree in the lease that ownership transfers at termination. (2) The lessor starts charging rental as soon as funds are given to the asset's supplier - effectively leasing an asset before buying/possessing it, earning reward without bearing ownership risk. Per Shari'ah, rent must be charged only from when the LESSEE is in a position to benefit from the leased asset (i.e. after taking delivery), not from the day funds are released or price paid to the supplier. (3) The lessor shifts ALL risks to the lessee, particularly where the asset's residual value is fixed in advance in the contract. Per Shari'ah, expenses to rectify defects preventing the lessee's use are the LESSOR's responsibility, while the lessee is responsible only for day-to-day maintenance/running expenses - this is described as the major difference between conventional and Islamic leases. (4) Even in conventional OPERATING leases, all risks/expenses fall on the lessee. But in Islamic operating leases, the lessor must bear upkeep responsibilities and all risks/costs of ownership; further, the operating lease is not for the asset's entire useful life but for a specified period, ending unless renewed by mutual consent.","examExplanation":"Four Shari'ah objections to conventional leases: (1) ownership transfer built into the lease from the start (vs Islamic requirement: kept as a SEPARATE, later transaction); (2) rent starts at PAYMENT to supplier (vs Islamic requirement: rent starts only once the LESSEE can actually use/benefit from the asset, i.e. post-delivery); (3) all risk shifted to lessee, including major repair costs (vs Islamic requirement: LESSOR bears defect-rectification/major-repair costs; lessee bears only day-to-day usage costs); (4) even conventional operating leases push all risk to the lessee (vs Islamic operating lease: lessor bears upkeep and ownership risk throughout, and the lease is for a specified, not the entire useful, period).","keyPoints":["Shari'ah objection 1: conventional hire-purchase bakes ownership transfer into the lease contract from the very outset (as part of the same bargain), rather than as a separate, subsequent transaction.","Shari'ah objection 2: conventional leases start charging rent from the moment funds are paid to the supplier, even before the lessee can use the asset - Shari'ah requires rent to begin only once the lessee is actually in a position to benefit from the delivered asset.","Shari'ah objection 3: conventional leases shift ALL risk (including major repair/defect-rectification costs) to the lessee, whereas Shari'ah reserves defect-rectification/major-repair responsibility for the lessor (owner), leaving only day-to-day maintenance/running costs to the lessee.","Shari'ah objection 4: even conventional OPERATING leases push all risk and expense to the lessee, while an Islamic operating lease keeps ownership risk and upkeep responsibility with the lessor throughout, and is bounded to a specified period rather than the asset's entire useful life."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["Rent under Shari'ah must be charged only from the point the lessee is actually able to benefit from the delivered, usable asset - not from the date funds are disbursed to the supplier.","Defect-rectification and major repair expenses (beyond normal day-to-day maintenance) must be borne by the lessor, not the lessee."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think 'operating lease' is automatically Shari'ah-compliant just because it's not hire-purchase. The book clarifies that even conventional operating leases typically push all risk/expense onto the lessee - only a properly structured ISLAMIC operating lease (with the lessor genuinely bearing ownership risk and upkeep) is compliant."],"distinctions":["Conventional operating lease (all risk/expense on lessee, despite lessor retaining nominal ownership) vs Islamic operating lease (lessor genuinely bears ownership risk/upkeep responsibility, lease bounded to a specified period)."],"relatedConcepts":["Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek","Takaful","Riba"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.4","pages":[289]}}],"table":{"caption":"Conventional lease features vs Shari’ah requirements (Section 11.4.4)","head":["Conventional feature","Shari’ah requirement"],"rows":[["Ownership transfer built into lease","Separate sale or gift, via unilateral promise"],["Rent from date funds paid to supplier","Rent only once lessee can use the delivered asset"],["All risks and costs on lessee; residual value fixed","Lessor bears ownership risk and defect repairs; lessee day-to-day maintenance"],["Operating lease: lessee bears risks","Lessor bears upkeep and ownership costs; specified period"]]}},{"id":"t11.4.5","section":"11.4.5","title":"Combining Two Contracts: Transferring Ownership at the End","pages":[289,290],"tier":"core","concepts":["imbt","wad","two-contracts"],"intuition":"The lessee has effectively paid for the asset — how can ownership pass without making the lease ‘two contracts in one’?","simpleExplanation":"The lease must not contain a precondition of sale or gift. Instead, the lessor gives a separate unilateral promise to sell (binding on the lessor; the lessee may choose) or to gift the asset at the end — gift being fair since the lessee paid cost and return. Objection ‘two bargains in one’ is answered: the Ijarah is effective now; the promise is unilateral and not a transaction until executed; no injustice or Riba (OIC Fiqh Academy, third session).","academicExplanation":["Ijarah and sale have different rules; ownership cannot be transferred by a sale contract effective on a future date. In commercial leases the lessee pays towards buying the property; justice requires title to pass on full payment — but how, compliantly? Scholars: no precondition of sale or gift in the lease; the lessor may give a separate unilateral promise to sell at termination, binding on the promisor; the lessee has an option; a bilateral promise would become a contract and is prohibited. Alternatively a separate promise to gift — appropriate as the bank recovers cost and return. Objection: IMBT is two contracts in one. Answers: (1) the parties agree on the nature: rent set so that cost and rent are recovered; (2) it consists of an immediately effective Ijarah and a unilateral promise that may or may not become effective — not a transaction until entered into; (3) no injustice, Riba or dispute; it is justifiable that the lessee gets title. A sale at the end of the lease does not contravene any basic principle (OIC Fiqh Academy, third session)."],"examExplanation":"No sale/gift precondition in Ijarah; separate unilateral promise (sale or gift) binding on promisor only; ‘two contracts in one’ objection answered (effective Ijarah + unilateral promise; no Riba/injustice).","keyPoints":["Bilateral promise = contract → prohibited.","Gift promise suits full cost recovery."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Promise to gift at the end","kind":"textbook","text":"A bank leases a car for five years with rent set to recover cost and profit. Instead of a sale clause, it gives a separate unilateral promise to gift the car when the lease ends — fair, since the lessee has paid the full cost and return (pp. 289–290)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A bilateral binding promise to sell and buy at lease end is acceptable.","right":"Scholars require a unilateral promise binding the promisor; the lessee keeps the option not to buy — a bilateral promise becomes a contract (p. 290)."}],"relatedTopics":["t5.5.5","t11.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why is a bilateral promise to buy/sell the leased asset at the end avoided?","options":["It is too expensive","A bilateral binding promise becomes a contract (sale), making two contracts in one","It violates tax laws","It is required by AAOIFI"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 290."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch11-t10"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch11-t10","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"11.4.5","sectionTitle":"Combining Two Contracts","title":"Combining Two Contracts: The 'Two Bargains, Kept Separate' Structure of Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek","covers":["t11.4.5"],"overview":"Explains how Islamic finance solves the ownership-transfer problem in leasing while avoiding the prohibition on 'two contracts in one bargain' - via a separate, unilateral promise to sell or gift.","simpleExplanation":"If a customer effectively pays off the full cost of an asset plus rental through their lease payments, it seems fair that they should eventually own it. But Islamic law generally doesn't allow bundling a sale into the same contract as a lease from day one. The solution: keep the lease and the eventual transfer as two SEPARATE, independent things. The lease happens now; separately, the bank makes a one-sided PROMISE that it will sell (or gift) the asset to the customer later - a promise the bank must honor if the customer wants to exercise it, but the customer is never obligated to buy.","academicExplanation":"Ijarah and sale are governed by different rules, particularly regarding ownership transfer timing (instant in sale, even on credit; retained by lessor throughout in Ijarah). Ownership transfer via a sale contract that becomes effective on a FUTURE date is not permitted. Yet in most commercial leases, the lessee's payments (beyond pure rental) go toward eventually buying the property - fairness suggests that as his ownership stake grows, the rental proportion of his payment should shrink, with full ownership transferring once he's paid the full cost plus rental. The Shari'ah-compliant solution recommended by contemporary scholars: the lease agreement must NOT contain a precondition of sale or gift after the lease period; instead, the LESSOR enters a SEPARATE, UNILATERAL promise to sell the leased asset at lease termination. The governing principle: a unilateral promise to contract at a future date is permitted - the PROMISOR (the bank) is bound to fulfill it, but the PROMISEE (the lessee) is NOT bound to actually purchase; the lessee thus has an OPTION to purchase which he may or may not exercise, but if he chooses to exercise it, the promisor (bank) cannot refuse, being bound by its promise. This deliberately avoids a BILATERAL promise between both parties, which would itself constitute a prohibited contract. Similarly, instead of a sale promise, the lessor can make a separate promise to GIFT the asset at lease end (justified because the bank typically recovers full cost + profit margin through rental, making it the lessee's fair right to eventually own the asset, with a gift being the natural transfer mechanism). On the 'two bargains in one' objection to Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek: this perception is rejected on three grounds: (1) the transaction is essentially ONE arrangement - a lease with rental fixed so the lessor recovers cost + rent during the lease period, and both parties agree to this nature upfront; (2) it consists of an Ijarah contract (immediately effective) PLUS a unilateral promise (which may or may not become effective later) - the promise is not itself a binding transaction until actually entered into by the parties, so there aren't really 'two contracts,' only one contract plus one non-binding-on-the-promisee promise; (3) the arrangement involves no injustice, Riba, or dispute-generating element to either party - it is justifiable precisely because the lessee, having paid cost + rental, ends up entitled to ownership. On this basis, a sale executed at lease-period end does not contravene any basic Shari'ah principle.","examExplanation":"Problem: ownership transfer via a future-dated sale contract is not permitted, but bundling sale+lease from the outset also risks 'two contracts in one bargain.' Solution: keep lease and eventual sale/gift as two INDEPENDENT things - the lease is immediately effective; separately, the LESSOR (bank) makes a UNILATERAL promise (binding on the bank only, NOT on the lessee) to sell or gift the asset at lease end. Lessee has an OPTION (may or may not exercise); if exercised, bank cannot refuse (bound by its promise). A BILATERAL (mutual) promise is avoided deliberately, since that would itself constitute a prohibited premature contract. 'Two bargains in one' objection rebutted: really ONE lease contract + a non-binding-on-promisee unilateral promise, involving no Riba/injustice/dispute risk.","keyPoints":["A sale contract that becomes effective on a future date cannot be used to transfer ownership at lease end - this is not permitted.","The Shari'ah-compliant solution: the LESSOR makes a SEPARATE, UNILATERAL promise (not a term within the lease contract itself) to sell or gift the asset at lease termination.","A unilateral promise binds only the PROMISOR (the bank) - the promisee (lessee) has an OPTION to purchase which he may or may not exercise; but if he does exercise it, the bank cannot refuse.","A BILATERAL (mutual, two-sided) promise between lessor and lessee is deliberately avoided, since it would itself constitute a prohibited premature contract.","The 'two contracts in one bargain' objection to Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek is rebutted on three grounds: it is essentially one lease arrangement plus a non-binding-on-the-promisee unilateral promise (not yet a transaction); the promise only becomes effective if and when actually exercised; and the structure involves no Riba, injustice, or dispute-generating element."],"definitions":[{"term":"Unilateral Promise (in Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek)","definition":"A one-sided undertaking by the lessor (bank) to sell or gift the leased asset to the lessee at lease termination - binding on the bank if the lessee chooses to exercise the resulting option, but not binding on the lessee to actually purchase."}],"conditions":["The ownership-transfer promise (sale or gift) must be a SEPARATE, unilateral undertaking by the lessor, not a precondition or integral term embedded within the Ijarah contract itself.","The ownership-transfer arrangement must not be structured as a bilateral (mutual) promise between lessor and lessee, as this would itself constitute a prohibited premature contract."],"principles":["A unilateral promise to enter a contract at a future date is permissible - the promisor is bound to fulfill it if the promisee chooses to exercise the resulting option, but the promisee incurs no corresponding obligation to do so."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students often think Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek violates the 'no two contracts in one bargain' rule because it combines leasing and eventual ownership transfer. The book explicitly rebuts this: it is structurally ONE lease contract plus a separate, non-binding-on-the-lessee unilateral promise - not two simultaneous binding contracts."],"distinctions":["A unilateral promise (permissible: binds only the promisor, promisee has a free option) vs a bilateral/mutual promise (prohibited in this context: would constitute a premature, binding sale contract for an asset the lessor doesn't yet fully control in sale terms)."],"relatedConcepts":["Khiyar","Bai' al 'Inah","Diminishing Musharakah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.5","pages":[289,290]}}]},{"id":"t11.4.6","section":"11.4.6","title":"Takaful/Insurance Expenses","pages":[291,291],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["takaful","ijarah"],"intuition":"Who pays to insure a leased car, and what if the claim falls short?","simpleExplanation":"Islamic lessors should use Takaful (insurance allowed only transitionally where Takaful is unavailable). As owner, the bank is the insurer/beneficiary; it may include Takaful cost in the rent by mutual consent. If the lessee breaches policy conditions barring recovery, he is liable; otherwise the bank bears the loss, including any shortfall in the Takaful claim. If ownership transfer becomes impossible without lessee fault, the lessee is compensated for rent above market rent.","academicExplanation":["Regulators often require leased assets to be insured; Islamic banks should use Takaful, avoiding conventional insurance (Riba and Gharar); many use insurance for lack of Takaful — allowed only transitionally. Criticism: banks pass premiums to lessees through rentals although the lessor has the insurable interest. The bank as owner should be the insurer and beneficiary; including Takaful in acquisition cost for rent is allowed since rent is by mutual consent. If transfer of ownership becomes impossible without cause attributable to the lessee, he must be compensated by the difference between contractual rent paid and market rent (AAOIFI 8/8) — some banks do not abide by this, contrary to the spirit of Islamic finance. The lessee receives a copy of the policy and must observe its conditions; if non-observance bars recovery, he is liable; absent fault, the lessor bears loss; if the Takaful claim is less than the loss, the remainder cannot be charged to the lessee."],"examExplanation":"Takaful (not insurance, except transitionally); bank = insured owner; cost may be priced into rent by consent; lessee liable only if he breaches policy conditions; claim shortfall borne by bank; AAOIFI 8/8 compensation if transfer impossible without lessee fault.","keyPoints":["Takaful shortfall cannot be charged to the lessee."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Takaful shortfall","kind":"textbook","text":"If the Takaful company pays less than the bank’s loss, the bank bears the balance; the lessee is liable only if he breached the policy conditions (p. 291)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Passing Takaful cost into the rent means the lessee is the insured party.","right":"The bank as owner holds the insurable interest; it may build the cost into the agreed rent by consent, but it remains the policyholder and beneficiary (p. 291)."}],"relatedTopics":["t16.5","t11.5.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A leased car is destroyed without lessee fault; the Takaful claim is less than the bank’s loss. Who bears the shortfall?","options":["The lessee","The bank (lessor)","The Takaful operator only","Shared 50:50"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 291."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch11-t11"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch11-t11","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"11.4.6","sectionTitle":"Takaful/Insurance Expenses","title":"Takaful and Insurance Expenses in Ijarah","covers":["t11.4.6"],"overview":"Explains why Takaful is preferred over conventional insurance for leased assets, who should bear the cost as a matter of principle, and how banks recover it through rental while remaining accountable for any shortfall.","simpleExplanation":"Banks should ideally insure leased assets through Takaful (the Islamic alternative), not conventional insurance, though many still use conventional insurance temporarily due to limited Takaful availability. Since the bank owns the asset, it's technically the one with an 'insurable interest' and should be the policyholder - but banks are allowed to fold this cost into the rental they charge, as long as the customer agrees to that rental. If something goes wrong and full ownership transfer becomes impossible through no fault of the customer, the bank must refund him the difference between what he paid and the fair market rental - and if the Takaful payout doesn't cover the full loss, the bank (not the customer) absorbs the shortfall.","academicExplanation":"Many countries regulatorily require leasing entities to insure leased assets. Islamic banks should use Shari'ah-approved Takaful rather than conventional insurance (which involves Riba and Gharar), though many currently use conventional insurance due to insufficient Takaful availability - permitted by Shari'ah scholars only as a TRANSITIONAL measure. Scholars criticize the practice of Islamic banks passing Takaful premium costs onto the lessee as part of lease instalments, on the basis that the LESSOR has the insurable interest (as owner) and should bear the premium. This is technically correct - as asset owner, the bank should be the insurer/beneficiary of the Takaful policy. However, Islamic banks normally include Takaful expenses within the asset's acquisition cost when determining rental, which Shari'ah scholars permit on the basis that rental is subject to mutual consent - if the lessee agrees to the resulting rental amount, the contract remains Shari'ah-acceptable. Regarding insurable interest: it rightly belongs to the bank as lessor. But if transfer of ownership becomes impossible for reasons NOT attributable to the lessee, the lessee must be protected by being paid the difference between the rent he actually paid (per the lease agreement) and the fair market rental of similar assets - some Islamic banks fail to observe this rule, which the author flags as against the spirit of Islamic finance, calling for Shari'ah advisor attention. On loss/damage effects: it is usually stipulated that the lessee receives a copy of the Takaful policy and must observe its conditions; if the asset is destroyed and the LESSEE's failure to observe policy conditions bars recovery, the lessee is held liable. Absent any fault/negligence by the lessee, the LESSOR bears all responsibility for asset damage/loss. If the Takaful claim paid is LESS than the actual loss/expense incurred, the shortfall CANNOT be charged to the lessee - the bank itself must bear it.","examExplanation":"Takaful preferred over conventional insurance (Riba/Gharar concerns); conventional insurance permitted only transitionally where Takaful is unavailable. Insurable interest belongs to the BANK (as owner) - it should technically be the policyholder/beneficiary. BUT banks may include Takaful cost within acquisition cost when calculating rental (permitted via mutual consent principle) - just can't pass it as a separate, unexplained charge. Key protection: if ownership transfer becomes impossible for reasons NOT attributable to the lessee, the bank must refund him the difference between rent paid and fair market rental. Loss allocation: lessee liable only if HIS failure to follow Takaful policy conditions causes non-recovery; otherwise, lessor bears all loss/damage responsibility, including any shortfall between Takaful claim paid and actual loss (never passed to the lessee).","keyPoints":["Islamic banks should use Takaful (not conventional insurance, due to Riba/Gharar) for leased assets; conventional insurance is permitted only as a transitional measure where Takaful is unavailable.","The insurable interest for a leased asset rightly belongs to the LESSOR (bank), as the owner - it should technically be the Takaful policyholder and beneficiary.","Banks may include Takaful expenses within the asset's acquisition cost when calculating rental (permitted via the principle of mutual consent to the resulting rental), rather than charging it as a separate itemized cost to the lessee.","If ownership transfer becomes impossible for reasons NOT attributable to the lessee, the bank must refund him the difference between the rent he actually paid and the fair market rental of similar assets - a rule the author notes some banks fail to observe.","If a Takaful claim payout is LESS than the actual loss/expense incurred, the shortfall cannot be charged to the lessee - the bank must bear it, unless the lessee's own failure to follow policy conditions caused the shortfall."],"definitions":[{"term":"Insurable Interest","definition":"The legal/financial stake that justifies a party being the beneficiary of an insurance/Takaful policy - in Ijarah, this rightly belongs to the lessor (bank) as owner of the leased asset."}],"conditions":["Conventional insurance for leased assets is permitted only as a transitional measure where Shari'ah-compliant Takaful is unavailable.","If ownership transfer becomes impossible for reasons not attributable to the lessee, the bank must refund the difference between the actual rent paid and the fair market rental of similar assets.","Any shortfall between a Takaful claim payout and the actual loss incurred must be borne by the bank, not charged to the lessee, absent lessee fault."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think including Takaful cost in the rental calculation is itself a Shari'ah violation because insurable interest belongs to the bank. The book clarifies this is permitted via mutual consent to the resulting rental - what is NOT permitted is unfairly making the lessee bear losses beyond his own fault (e.g. a Takaful payout shortfall, or failing to refund excess rent if ownership transfer becomes impossible through no fault of his)."],"distinctions":["Takaful cost folded into rental via mutual consent (permitted) vs charging the lessee separately as the 'insured party' bearing the full insurable-interest role that rightly belongs to the bank (the criticized practice)."],"relatedConcepts":["Gharar","Riba","Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.6","pages":[291]}}]},{"id":"t11.5","section":"11.5","title":"Islamic Banks’ Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek (IMBT)","pages":[291,293],"tier":"core","concepts":["imbt"],"intuition":"How do Islamic banks turn a lease into a Shari’ah-compliant path to ownership?","simpleExplanation":"Objections to modern leases concern ownership transfer, rent accrual and ownership risks. IMBT fixes these: rent starts when the usable asset is delivered; if the client buys as agent, a separate prior agency applies and pre-delivery loss is the bank’s; the bank bears risk while the client is agent. Hire-purchase is unsuitable. AAOIFI: ownership transfer via separate promise to sell (token, remaining amount or market value), promise to gift, or promise of a contingent gift on final payment. Sale and lease-back needs ~1 year before transfer back (avoid ‘Inah).","academicExplanation":["Shari’ah objections to modern leases relate to (i) ownership transfer procedure, (ii) accrual of rentals, (iii) lessor’s ownership risks and expenses. Islamic banks adopt IMBT — basically Ijarah with ownership transfer kept separate; close to finance lease except: (1) rent starts when the asset is supplied in usable form, not on payment; (2) if the price is paid to the lessee, a prior agency agreement is required and destruction before delivery is the principal’s loss; (3) the bank bears asset risk while the client is its purchasing agent. Hire–purchase (lease and sale from the start) is unsuitable. Banks buy in response to requests for leases ending in transfer through a separate formal sale or gift. AAOIFI: ownership cannot transfer by a sale contract effective in future; methods: (1) promise to sell for token or other consideration, accelerated payment of the remainder, or market value; (2) promise to gift at the end; (3) promise to gift contingent on an event (e.g. payment of remaining instalments). Transfer must be independent of the Ijarah; the promise unilateral, binding on the promisor, with the other free not to proceed; in (1) and (2) a new contract is needed; in (3) title passes automatically on fulfilment (AAOIFI 8/1–8/7). Transfer may also occur before the end at a stipulated or market price, or through Diminishing Musharakah. Some banks take the lessee’s undertaking to pay market or prestipulated price on termination, with a price schedule including the bank’s loss on early termination. In sale and lease-back IMBT there must be a reasonable period — normally one year — before transfer back, to avoid ‘Inah (AAOIFI 3/4, 8/6)."],"examExplanation":"IMBT = Ijarah + separate unilateral promise for transfer. Differences from finance lease: rent from usable delivery; agency prior; bank bears risk. AAOIFI transfer methods: promise to sell (token/remaining/market), promise to gift, contingent gift. Sale & lease-back: ~1 year gap.","keyPoints":["Contingent gift transfers title automatically on the condition.","Hire–purchase unsuitable.","One-year gap in sale and lease-back to avoid ‘Inah.","Sale and lease-back IMBT: allow about one year before transfer back to avoid ‘Inah."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Contingent gift","kind":"textbook","text":"Under AAOIFI’s third method, the bank promises the car will become the lessee’s as a gift once the last instalment is paid. When that condition is fulfilled, title passes without any new contract (pp. 291–293)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A hire–purchase contract becomes Islamic when renamed IMBT or Ijarah-wal-Iqtina‘.","right":"Renaming changes nothing; the lease must stay a pure Ijarah with ownership risk on the bank and a separate transfer contract (p. 294)."}],"relatedTopics":["t11.5.1","t11.4.5","t12.9"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Under AAOIFI, in which IMBT transfer method does ownership pass without signing a new document?","options":["Promise to sell at market value","Promise to gift at the end","Gift contingent on payment of remaining instalments","None"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 292."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch11-t12"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch11-t12","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"11.5-11.5.1","sectionTitle":"Islamic Banks' Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek / Procedure for Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek","title":"Islamic Banks' Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek: Full Procedure","covers":["t11.5","t11.5.1"],"overview":"Walks through the complete bank procedure for Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek - from customer request through asset purchase, lease commencement, rental payment, and the three methods of eventual ownership transfer - highlighting how it differs from a conventional finance lease.","simpleExplanation":"This is the real-world version of how Islamic banks do lease financing. The customer tells the bank what they need, signs an initial agreement, and puts down earnest money. The bank then buys the asset itself (or has the customer buy it as the bank's agent). The actual lease only starts once the asset is delivered and usable - not when the bank pays for it. Throughout the lease, the bank keeps bearing ownership risk and major costs. At the end, ownership passes to the customer through one of three separate methods: a promise to sell (for a price), a promise to gift it for free, or a promise to gift it once remaining payments are made.","academicExplanation":"The core Shari'ah objections to modern leases relate to: (i) the ownership-transfer procedure, (ii) rental accrual timing, and (iii) the lessor's responsibility for ownership-related risks/expenses. Addressing these, Islamic banks use Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek - the transaction stays fundamentally an Ijarah, with ownership transfer kept SEPARATE from the main contract. This closely resembles conventional finance lease but with three key differences: (1) in a finance lease, rental accrues from the moment the lessor pays for the asset; in Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek, rental starts only when the asset is SUPPLIED to the lessee in USABLE form; (2) price can be paid to the supplier OR the lessee in both structures, but if paid to the LESSEE in the Islamic structure, a separate AGENCY agreement (preceding the lease agreement, governed by full Wakalah rules) is required - and if the asset is destroyed before delivery to the lessee in usable form, the loss falls on the PRINCIPAL (bank), not the agent; (3) in Islamic lease, asset risk remains the BANK's as long as the client acts merely as its purchasing agent, whereas in conventional finance lease, all risk sits with the lessee from the start. Because hire-purchase bundles lease and sale from the outset, it is unsuitable for Islamic banks. For Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek, banks purchase assets on specific customer request, with ownership eventually transferring via a SEPARATE, formal sale or gift contract after the lease term. Ownership CANNOT be transferred via a sale contract (bundled with the Ijarah) effective on a future date; instead, transfer must use one of: (1) a promise to sell for a token/other consideration, by accelerating remaining payments, or at market value; (2) a promise to gift (no consideration) at lease end; (3) a promise to gift contingent on a particular event (e.g. payment of remaining instalments). All three must be INDEPENDENT of the Ijarah contract, not integral to it; the promise must be UNILATERAL and binding on the promisor only, with the other party free not to proceed. For methods 1 and 2, a NEW contract must be drawn up at the time of transfer (ownership doesn't transfer merely by virtue of the earlier promise); for method 3, if the Ijarah has separate documentation gifting the asset contingent on remaining-instalment payment, ownership transfers automatically upon fulfillment, without any further document. Ownership can also transfer PRIOR to lease-period end (at a pre-stipulated or market price, or via Diminishing Musharakah, where the bank's ownership share gradually transfers as rental decreases accordingly). In sale-and-lease-back cases, there should be a reasonable gap (typically suggested as one year) between the purchase/lease contracts and any later transfer back to the customer - long enough that the asset/value might genuinely have changed - to avoid Bai' al 'Inah. Procedure (11.5.1): (1) client conveys requirement, signs an MoU, gives an undertaking plus Hamish Jiddiyah (earnest money) to confirm seriousness; per AAOIFI, this earnest money should ideally be kept as an investment trust invested by the bank on a Mudarabah basis (i.e. as a PLS deposit in the client's name), though with client consent it can become bank debt/liability, or be treated as advance rental payment. (2) The bank purchases the asset directly or appoints an agent - AAOIFI prefers a third-party agent, though the client can also be appointed; if the client indicates the vendor, the bank can get a performance bond; the bank remains liable for ownership-related risks/expenses throughout. Notably, unlike Murabaha, the bank need NOT first take possession before delivering to the lessee - if the client is appointed purchasing agent, the lease can become operative directly from the date the client (as agent) takes delivery, without an additional handover procedure (unlike MPO, which disallows simultaneous transfer). (3) Sometimes bank and client jointly purchase the asset (Shirkatulmilk partnership by ownership), with the bank leasing its share to the client on a Diminishing Musharakah basis - rental proportional to the bank's ownership share, decreasing as the client buys out more of the bank's share. (4) When the asset is purchased and possessed (by the bank or its agent), the formal lease agreement is executed; rental starts accruing from this point once installation is complete and the asset is usable (if the lessee delays using it for reasons attributable to himself, rental is still owed). (5) On rental default, the bank can demand acceleration (if agreed in the lease agreement) - constituting early termination, with the bank taking the asset back or the lessee purchasing per the agreement's terms; on foreclosure, only rent already due can be deducted, never remaining-period rent. As rental implicitly includes recovery of the asset's cost, AAOIFI directs that if property transfer becomes impossible for reasons not attributable to the lessee (e.g. asset destruction/theft, or the lease's continuity becoming impossible), rental must be adjusted to the prevailing market value, with any EXCESS the lessee paid over market rental refunded to him - similarly, any Takaful claim excess over the bank's cost/expenses should go to the client.","examExplanation":"Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek vs conventional finance lease - 3 key differences: (1) rental starts at DELIVERY in usable form (vs conventional: at payment to supplier); (2) if price paid to the lessee (not supplier), a SEPARATE agency agreement is required, and pre-delivery destruction loss falls on the PRINCIPAL/bank (vs conventional: simply part of financing risk on lessee); (3) asset risk stays with the BANK throughout the agency-purchase period (vs conventional: all risk on lessee from the start). Ownership transfer at lease end uses ONE of 3 independent methods: promise to sell (token/accelerated/market price), promise to gift (unconditional), or promise to gift contingent on remaining-instalment payment - ALL must be unilateral, binding only on the promisor (bank), and a SEPARATE transaction from the Ijarah itself. Procedure: MoU + Hamish Jiddiyah -> asset purchase (direct or via agent, AAOIFI prefers third-party) -> formal lease executed on possession, rent starts at delivery in usable form -> rental payments (bank bears ownership risk/major expenses) -> ownership transfer via one of the 3 independent promise methods.","keyPoints":["Three key differences between Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek and a conventional finance lease: rental starts at DELIVERY in usable form (not at payment to supplier); paying price to the lessee (rather than supplier) requires a separate agency agreement, with pre-delivery destruction loss falling on the bank as principal; and asset risk stays with the bank throughout the agency-purchase period.","Ownership transfer at lease end must use one of three methods, each an INDEPENDENT transaction from the Ijarah contract itself: a unilateral promise to sell (token/accelerated/market price), a unilateral promise to gift unconditionally, or a promise to gift contingent on a specific event (e.g. remaining instalments paid).","All ownership-transfer promises must be unilateral, binding only on the promisor (the bank) - the lessee retains a free option not to proceed.","Unlike Murabaha (which requires the bank to first take possession before a distinct offer/acceptance to the client), in Ijarah the lease can become operative directly from the date the client, as purchasing agent, takes delivery - no additional handover procedure is required.","Hamish Jiddiyah (earnest money) taken at the MoU stage should ideally be kept as a Mudarabah-based investment trust in the client's name (per AAOIFI), rather than simply becoming bank debt, though the latter or treatment as advance rental is also possible with client consent.","If property transfer becomes impossible for reasons not attributable to the lessee, rental must be adjusted to prevailing market value, with any excess the lessee paid refunded - and any Takaful claim excess over the bank's cost/expenses must also go to the client."],"definitions":[{"term":"Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek","definition":"Lease culminating in transfer of ownership to the lessee, structured so the lease and the eventual sale/gift remain separate, independent contracts (unlike conventional hire-purchase, which bundles them together from the outset)."}],"conditions":["Ownership cannot be transferred via a sale contract bundled with the Ijarah and made effective on a future date - it must use a separate, unilateral promise (sale, gift, or contingent gift).","The ownership-transfer promise must be unilateral and binding only on the promisor (lessor); the lessee retains a free option not to proceed.","In sale-and-lease-back arrangements, a reasonable gap (commonly suggested as one year) is needed between the sale/lease contracts and any later transfer back to the customer, to avoid Bai' al 'Inah."],"principles":[],"processSteps":["Customer Need and MoU: The client conveys his asset requirement and enters an MoU with the bank stipulating the overall deal structure, giving an undertaking and Hamish Jiddiyah (earnest money) to confirm his seriousness in taking the asset on lease once purchased.","Bank Purchases the Asset: The bank purchases the asset directly or appoints an agent (a third party is preferred per AAOIFI, though the client is also possible); the bank remains liable for all ownership-related risks and expenses throughout this stage.","Formal Lease Agreement and Delivery: Once the asset is purchased and taken into possession (by the bank or by the client as its agent), the formal lease agreement is executed; rental begins accruing only once the asset is delivered to the lessee in a fully usable form (not from the date of purchase payment).","Rental Payments Over the Lease Term: The lessee pays rental (which implicitly recovers the asset's cost); the bank continues to bear ownership-related risks and major expenses (e.g. Takaful, defect rectification), while the lessee bears only usage-related, day-to-day expenses.","Separate Unilateral Promise for Ownership Transfer: Independent of the Ijarah contract itself, the bank (lessor) makes a unilateral promise to sell (for a token, accelerated, or market price), to gift unconditionally, or to gift contingent on a specified event (e.g. full payment of remaining instalments) - binding on the bank alone.","Ownership Transfer at Lease End: If the lessee exercises the option to purchase (for the sale-promise methods, via a newly drawn sale contract) or the contingent-gift condition is fulfilled, formal ownership title transfers to the lessee, completing the Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek structure."],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think the Hamish Jiddiyah taken at the MoU stage is simply the bank's money to use freely. Per AAOIFI's preferred treatment, it should ideally be held as a Mudarabah-based investment trust in the client's own name (a PLS deposit), not automatically treated as a straightforward bank liability, unless the client separately consents to that treatment."],"distinctions":["Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek (rent starts at delivery in usable form, ownership transfer via a separate independent promise) vs conventional finance lease/hire-purchase (rent starts at payment to supplier, sale bundled into the lease from the outset)."],"relatedConcepts":["Wakalah","Hamish Jiddiyah","Diminishing Musharakah","Bai' al 'Inah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5-11.5.1","pages":[291,292,293,294,295]},"transactionSteps":[{"step":"Customer Need and MoU","description":"The client conveys his asset requirement and signs an MoU with the bank stipulating the overall deal structure, giving an undertaking and Hamish Jiddiyah (earnest money) to confirm his commitment to lease the asset once purchased."},{"step":"Bank Purchases the Asset","description":"The bank purchases the asset directly or appoints an agent (AAOIFI prefers a third party, though the client can also be appointed); the bank remains liable for all ownership-related risks and expenses throughout this acquisition stage."},{"step":"Formal Lease Agreement and Delivery","description":"Once the asset is purchased and taken into possession, the formal lease agreement is executed; rental begins accruing only once the asset is delivered to the lessee in a fully usable form - not from the date the purchase price was paid."},{"step":"Rental Payments Over the Lease Term","description":"The lessee pays rental, which implicitly recovers the asset's cost for the bank; the bank continues bearing ownership-related risks and major expenses (Takaful, defect rectification), while the lessee bears only day-to-day usage expenses."},{"step":"Separate Unilateral Promise for Ownership Transfer","description":"Independent of the Ijarah contract, the bank makes a unilateral promise to sell (token/accelerated/market price), to gift unconditionally, or to gift contingent on an event (e.g. full payment of remaining instalments) - binding on the bank alone, with the lessee free not to proceed."},{"step":"Ownership Transfer at Lease End","description":"If the lessee exercises the purchase option (via a newly drawn sale contract) or the contingent-gift condition is fulfilled, formal ownership title transfers to the lessee, completing the Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek structure."}]}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Conventional finance lease","b":"IMBT","text":"Finance lease: rent from payment date, lessee bears risk, sale built in. IMBT: rent from usable delivery, bank bears ownership risk, transfer by separate promise."}],"table":{"caption":"AAOIFI methods of transferring ownership in IMBT","head":["Method","New contract needed?"],"rows":[["Promise to sell for a token/other consideration, remaining amount, or market value","Yes — formal sale"],["Promise to gift at the end of the lease","Yes — gift deed"],["Promise of a gift contingent on paying remaining instalments","No — title passes on fulfilment"]]}},{"id":"t11.5.1","section":"11.5.1","title":"Procedure for IMBT, Sale and Lease-back, and Destruction of the Asset","pages":[293,295],"tier":"core","concepts":["imbt","hamish-jiddiyah","wakalah"],"intuition":"Step by step, how does a bank run an auto or machinery Ijarah?","simpleExplanation":"(1) MoU and client’s undertaking with HJ (trust; AAOIFI: invest as Mudarabah deposit; may be advance rent). (2) Bank buys directly or via agent (third party preferred; client may import as agent; costs reimbursed; bank bears ownership risk). Unlike Murabaha, the lease may start when the client-agent takes delivery. (3) Or joint purchase and lease of the bank’s share (DM). (4) Formal lease on possession; rent from usable form. (5) Default → acceleration if agreed; only due rent from security. Then a separate sale/gift. If transfer becomes impossible without lessee fault, refund rent above market rent. Sale and lease-back: permissible but exceptional; sale first; transfer back after a reasonable period. Destruction ends the lease; no rent while unusable.","academicExplanation":["Procedure: (1) the client’s requirement and MoU; an undertaking with earnest money (HJ) kept as trust — if used with permission it becomes the bank’s debt; AAOIFI recommends keeping it as an investment trust (Mudarabah PLS deposit in the client’s name); may be advance rent with consent (AAOIFI 2/3). (2) The bank buys directly or via an agent; for imports the customer may be agent, opening an L/C and paying duties, taxes and transport, all reimbursed and part of cost; AAOIFI prefers a third-party agent but allows the client; a performance bond for client-nominated vendors; the bank remains liable for ownership risks and expenses (AAOIFI 3/7). Unlike Murabaha, the bank need not first take possession and then deliver: if the client is purchasing agent, the lease may start when he takes delivery as agent — since ownership, risk and reward remain with the lessor. (3) Joint purchase — Shirkatulmilk — with the bank leasing its share (Diminishing Musharakah), rent proportional to its share and decreasing as the client buys units (AAOIFI 3/6). (4) After purchase and possession, the formal lease is executed; rent accrues when installation is complete and usable; delays attributable to the lessee do not stop rent. (5) On default the bank may accelerate if agreed — early termination; the bank takes the asset back or the lessee buys it per the agreement; in foreclosure only due rent may be deducted, not remaining rent (AAOIFI 6/5, 7/2/1, 7/2/2).","Then a separate sale or gift contract using the three methods. Rent includes the asset cost, but legally is all rent; accepted by scholars since rent is by consent and mutually beneficial. AAOIFI 8/8: if transfer is impossible (destruction, theft) or the lease cannot continue without lessee fault, rent is adjusted to market — the excess of contractual over market rent refunded; Takaful proceeds above the bank’s cost and expenses should go to the client. Hire–purchase renamed IMBT or Ijarah-wal-Iqtina‘ is not compliant; but institutions following scholars’ procedures in letter and spirit should not be unnecessarily criticised (Vogel and Hayes).","Sale and lease-back: the customer sells an asset he owns and leases it back — legally permissible but ideally avoided and not a major mode; acceptable if the client wants to escape Riba with no alternative — for new assets or conversion from conventional financing. The sale must precede the lease; the asset may be sold back only after a reasonable period (to avoid ‘Inah). Destruction/theft: total destruction terminates a lease of an identified asset; the lessee, a fiduciary, is liable for misconduct or negligence; partial destruction impairing benefit allows termination or rent amendment; no rent for periods of non-usability without lessee negligence."],"examExplanation":"IMBT steps: MoU + undertaking + HJ (Mudarabah trust); bank buys (agent possible; bank bears risk); lease may start on agent’s delivery; formal lease on usable asset; default → acceleration, only due rent from security; separate sale/gift. AAOIFI 8/8 refund. Sale & lease-back exceptional; sale first; delayed transfer back. Destruction ends lease.","keyPoints":["HJ may be invested as a Mudarabah deposit for the client.","Lease can start at agent’s delivery (unlike Murabaha).","Renaming hire–purchase does not make it compliant.","AAOIFI recommends keeping Hamish Jiddiyah as a Mudarabah (PLS) deposit in the client’s name; with consent it can be advance rent.","On default with acceleration, only due rent may be taken from security, not future rent."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Rent from the agent’s delivery date","kind":"textbook","text":"Unlike MPO, in Ijarah the lease can start the day the client, as agent, takes delivery — no separate transfer is needed, because ownership stays with the bank (p. 293)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t11.5","t11.6","t14.4.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why can an Ijarah start when the client, as the bank’s agent, takes delivery, whereas in Murabaha the bank must first take possession before selling?","options":["Ijarah has no rules","In Ijarah ownership and risk stay with the lessor during the lease, so no separate transfer is needed","Murabaha is a loan","Because of tax"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 293."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"processSteps":["Client request + MoU; undertaking to lease with Hamish Jiddiyah","Bank purchases asset directly or through an agent (bank bears ownership risk)","Asset delivered in usable form (to client as agent or lessee)","Formal Ijarah agreement executed; rent accrues from usable delivery","Client pays rent; bank bears ownership costs (Takaful, major repairs)","At end: separate sale or gift under the bank’s unilateral promise"]},{"id":"t11.5.2","section":"11.5.2","title":"Issues Concerning Modern Use of Ijarah","pages":[295,296],"tier":"core","concepts":["ijarah","risk-management"],"intuition":"What practical problems do Islamic lessors face, and what principles must never be compromised?","simpleExplanation":"Five issues: (1) major repairs are the lessor’s — shifting them is invalid; (2) specific-asset leases signed at disbursement earn no rent until delivery — use a promise to lease first and price the whole funded period; (3) supply-stage damage isn’t the agent’s unless at fault; (4) usufruct may fall — rent can be reduced or cancelled (e.g. crop disaster); (5) default penalties go to charity. Ijarah return isn’t truly fixed: the bank bears purchase, Takaful and upkeep costs (supplier discounts are its right). Two non-negotiables: risk can’t be separated from ownership; lease and sale stay separate.","academicExplanation":["Five sets of issues: (1) burdens of the asset — repairs beyond normal maintenance fall on the lessor; a clause shifting ownership costs other than operating expenses to the lessee is invalid as unjust enrichment. (2) Lack of knowledge: if a specified asset is leased, the agreement should be executed only after possession by the lessor (or lessee as agent); if signed at disbursement and delivery is delayed, no rent is due for the gap — so enter a ‘promise to lease’ first and execute Ijarah when available; pricing may consider the whole period funds are invested (supply + lease). (3) Supply-stage damage: the agent is not responsible unless at fault; advance rent adjusted unless the lease is extended equivalently. (4) Future usufruct may fall; Islamic law allows cancellation if usufruct falls below normal expectations; nearly all schools allow reducing or abolishing rent of farmland damaged by drought or floods (Imam Muhammad: Ijarah becomes invalid and the lessee owes a conventional wage). (5) Default: penalties go to charity. The perception that Ijarah yields a fixed return is incorrect: the bank bears acquisition and import expenses (it may price them in), is entitled to supplier discounts as owner, and pays Takaful and upkeep; Takaful claims may not cover losses — so net return is uncertain. Solutions must not be stratagems: (i) risk cannot be separated from ownership — the bank must take Dhaman; (ii) lease and sale must be separate and independent."],"examExplanation":"Issues: lessor bears major repairs; sign lease after possession (promise first); agent not liable for supply-stage damage absent fault; reduce rent if usufruct falls; penalties to charity. Ijarah return not truly fixed. Two principles: risk with ownership; lease and sale separate.","keyPoints":["Supplier discounts belong to the bank as owner.","Farmland rent reduced/abolished for natural calamity."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Drought on leased farmland","kind":"textbook","text":"Nearly all schools allow reducing or abolishing rent if a leased farm’s crop is ruined by drought or flood; Imam Muhammad says the Ijarah becomes invalid and the lessee owes a customary wage (p. 296)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Ijarah gives banks a fixed, certain return.","right":"The bank bears acquisition, import, Takaful and major upkeep costs and claim shortfalls, so the net return is not certain (p. 296)."}],"relatedTopics":["t11.3.5","t11.4.4","t4.2.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to the author, why is Ijarah not truly a ‘fixed return’ mode for banks?","options":["Rent is always variable","The bank bears acquisition, Takaful and upkeep costs and asset risk, so net return is uncertain","Rent cannot be agreed","Banks cannot price leases"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 296."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch11-t13"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch11-t13","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"11.5.2","sectionTitle":"Issues Concerning Modern Use of Ijarah","title":"Operational Issues in Modern Ijarah Practice","covers":["t11.5.2"],"overview":"Catalogs five practical problems banks face applying Ijarah - expense allocation errors, execution timing mistakes, in-transit damage responsibility, usufruct instability, and rent default penalties - plus the myth that Ijarah gives banks a guaranteed fixed return.","simpleExplanation":"Banks make some common mistakes with Ijarah: they sometimes wrongly try to push major repair costs onto the customer (not allowed - that's the bank's job as owner); they sometimes sign the lease too early (before actually having the asset ready), which means they can't legally charge rent for the gap period; they need to remember that in-transit damage during the supply phase isn't the customer's fault unless proven otherwise; leases can be cancelled if the asset's usefulness is destroyed by something like a natural disaster; and if a customer pays late, the penalty has to go to charity, not the bank. Contrary to popular belief, Ijarah doesn't actually give banks a guaranteed fixed return - as asset owners, they're on the hook for all sorts of ownership costs and can't count on a certain net income.","academicExplanation":"Islamic banks face five sets of issues in modern Ijarah practice. (1) Burden allocation: Shari'ah places the duty of repairing goods (beyond normal maintenance) solely on the lessor, since repairs benefit him as owner - a clause shifting these ownership-related costs to the lessee is invalid, as it unjustly enriches the lessor. (2) Execution timing due to bank unfamiliarity with Ijarah principles: if a SPECIFIED unit of asset is to be leased, the Ijarah agreement should only be executed once the asset is in the lessor's (or agent-lessee's) possession; if the agreement is instead signed at the time funds are disbursed to the supplier, and there's a delivery delay, NO RENTAL IS DUE for the gap between the agreement date and actual delivery. The fix: banks should first enter a 'promise to lease,' executing the actual Ijarah contract only once the asset is available from the supplier - while factoring the WHOLE period (supply + lease) into rental determination. (3) Damage during the supply stage: if damage occurs while the client-as-agent is procuring the asset, he is NOT responsible unless his fault is proven; any rent received in advance should be adjusted accordingly, unless the lease is agreed to be extended by an equivalent period past its original expiry. (4) Usufruct instability risk: since usufruct extends into the future, it may be reduced or become risky/unstable - Islamic law allows lease cancellation (or rent reduction/abolition) if events cause usufruct to fall below normal expectations, e.g. nearly all schools allow rental reduction/abolition for leased farmland damaged by natural calamity (drought, floods); per Imam Muhammad, if leased-land crops are destroyed by natural calamity, the Ijarah becomes invalid and the lessee is instead entitled to a conventional wage. (5) Rent default penalties must go to charity (a 'problem' from the bank's profitability perspective, since it cannot retain such penalties as income). A further important correction: the common perception that Ijarah gives banks a FIXED, guaranteed return is INCORRECT - since banks must purchase/import assets and bear all associated expenses (which they CAN factor into rental, but as a matter of principle still bear as owner), discounts from suppliers on leased-asset purchases belong to the bank (as asset owner), and major ownership expenses (Takaful, upkeep) fall to the bank - a Takaful claim payout might not fully cover repair/loss costs, meaning banks cannot be CERTAIN of achieving their targeted net return. The proper solution to these operational challenges is NOT stratagems/workarounds that compromise integrity, but adherence to two core principles: (a) risk cannot be separated from ownership - since the leased asset remains in the bank's ownership, the corresponding responsibility (Dhaman) must genuinely be borne by the bank; (b) lease and sale are contracts of different natures and must be kept separate and independent of each other. If these two principles are respected, banks retain flexibility in HOW they structure leasing, mitigate risk, and transfer ownership.","examExplanation":"5 practical Ijarah issues: (1) major repair/defect-rectification costs cannot be shifted to the lessee (lessor's job) - a contrary clause is invalid; (2) if the Ijarah agreement is signed before the asset is available and delivery is delayed, NO rental is due for the gap - fix: use a 'promise to lease' first, execute the actual Ijarah only once the asset is available; (3) in-transit/supply-stage damage is NOT the client-agent's fault unless proven, with advance rent adjusted accordingly; (4) usufruct instability (e.g. natural calamity destroying farmland crops) can invalidate/reduce the lease - per Imam Muhammad, this converts to a conventional wage entitlement; (5) rent-default penalties must go to charity, limiting bank profitability from defaults. Myth debunked: Ijarah does NOT give banks a guaranteed fixed return - banks bear all ownership expenses (Takaful shortfalls, repair costs) as a matter of principle, even though they can factor expected costs into rental pricing. Two guiding principles for resolving these issues: risk cannot be separated from ownership (bank must genuinely bear Dhaman/responsibility); lease and sale must remain separate, independent contracts.","keyPoints":["A clause shifting major repair/defect-rectification costs (beyond normal maintenance) to the lessee is invalid - Shari'ah places this burden solely on the lessor, since repairs benefit him as owner.","If the Ijarah agreement is signed before the asset is actually available/possessed and delivery is delayed, NO rental is due for the gap period - the fix is to use a 'promise to lease' first, executing the actual contract only once the asset is delivered.","Damage occurring during the asset-supply stage (while the client acts as purchasing agent) is not the client's responsibility unless his fault is proven.","Islamic law allows lease cancellation or rent reduction if usufruct falls below normal expectations due to external events (e.g. natural calamity destroying leased farmland's crops) - per Imam Muhammad, this can even invalidate the Ijarah, converting the lessee's entitlement to a conventional wage.","The common perception that Ijarah gives banks a fixed, guaranteed return is INCORRECT - banks bear real ownership expenses (Takaful shortfalls, repair costs, supplier discounts belonging to them) as a matter of principle, meaning their targeted net return is not certain.","The two guiding principles for resolving Ijarah operational challenges: risk cannot be separated from ownership (the bank must genuinely bear the responsibility/Dhaman for what it owns), and lease and sale must remain separate, independent contracts."],"definitions":[{"term":"Dhaman","definition":"Responsibility/liability - the principle that risk cannot be separated from ownership, meaning a lessor who retains ownership of a leased asset must genuinely bear the corresponding responsibility for it."}],"conditions":["A contractual clause shifting major repair costs (beyond normal maintenance) from the lessor to the lessee is invalid.","No rental is due for any period between an Ijarah agreement's signing and the asset's actual delivery, if the agreement was signed before the asset was available."],"principles":["Risk cannot be separated from ownership - a lessor retaining ownership of a leased asset must genuinely bear the corresponding responsibility (Dhaman) for it.","Lease and sale are contracts of different natures and must be kept separate and independent of each other."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Natural Calamity and Leased Farmland","kind":"textbook","text":"Nearly all schools of Islamic jurisprudence allow reduction or abolition of rental for leased farmland whose produce is damaged by natural calamity (drought, floods). Per Imam Muhammad, if the crop of leased land is destroyed by such a calamity, the Ijarah itself becomes invalid, and the lessee is instead entitled only to a conventional wage."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often assume Ijarah is a 'safe,' fixed-income mode for Islamic banks, similar to a bond. The chapter explicitly corrects this: because banks bear genuine ownership risk and expenses (repair costs, Takaful shortfalls), their net return is not guaranteed."],"distinctions":["A 'promise to lease' (used before the asset is available, no rent accrues) vs the actual Ijarah contract (executed only once the asset is delivered and usable, rent then begins accruing)."],"relatedConcepts":["Force Majeure","Takaful","Wakalah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.2","pages":[295,296]}}],"principles":["Risk cannot be separated from ownership (Dhaman).","Lease and sale are different contracts and must be kept separate."]},{"id":"t11.5.3","section":"11.5.3","title":"Assignment of Leased Assets and Securitisation of Leases (Ijarah Sukuk)","pages":[297,297],"tier":"core","concepts":["ijarah-sukuk","sukuk"],"intuition":"How does Ijarah become a tradable Sukuk?","simpleExplanation":"The lessor may sell the leased asset (whole or part) to a third party with its rights and obligations; the lease continues. But assigning only the rent stream for money is selling money for money — not allowed. Selling the asset to many investors via certificates creates Ijarah Sukuk: proportionate ownership with owner’s rights and obligations; returns are quasi-fixed (maximum = rent, less unexpected costs); holders bear market, maintenance, Takaful and default risk and pro rata loss on total destruction.","academicExplanation":["Experts allow the lessor to sell the asset in whole or part to a third party with its rights and obligations; the sale is valid and Ijarah may continue — useful for high-cost assets (aircraft, ships, equipment, roads, bridges). If ownership is not transferred and only the rent is assigned for money, it is not allowed — money for money must be equal. The new owner enjoys the lessor’s rights and bears its liabilities — potential for securitisation and a secondary market. Sold to many investors, proportions are evidenced by certificates — Ijarah Sukuk or leasing certificates — representing proportionate ownership of physical assets tied to the Ijarah; holders assume owner/lessor rights and obligations and must maintain the asset (Kahf). Risks: real market risk on asset pricing, maintenance and Takaful costs, and the lessee’s ability to pay; total destruction → pro rata loss. Hence quasi-fixed return: the contractual rent is a maximum subject to deduction for unexpected expenses."],"examExplanation":"Leased asset can be sold with the lease; rent-only assignment prohibited. Ijarah Sukuk = pro rata ownership of leased assets; holders bear owner risks/costs; quasi-fixed return (rent = maximum).","keyPoints":["Rent stream alone cannot be sold.","Ijarah Sukuk rent is a maximum return, reduced by unexpected expenses; holders bear pro rata loss if the asset is destroyed."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Airport financed by Ijarah Sukuk","kind":"textbook","text":"A government contracts to build an airport, leases it and sells Ijarah Sukuk to the public; proceeds pay the contractor and holders receive only actual rental income (p. 298)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t15.3.5","t11.5.4","t8.8.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A lessor wants to sell only its right to future rentals (not the asset) at a discount. This is:","options":["Permissible securitisation","Not permissible — it is selling money for money","Ijarah Sukuk","Required by AAOIFI"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 297."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch11-t14"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch11-t14","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"11.5.3-11.5.4","sectionTitle":"Assignment of the Leased Assets and Securitization of Leases / Potential of Ijarah","title":"Assignment and Securitization of Leases: Ijarah Sukuk and Ijarah's Financing Potential","covers":["t11.5.3","t11.5.4"],"overview":"Explains how a lessor can sell a leased asset (with the lease continuing), why this creates strong securitization potential via Ijarah Sukuk, and surveys Ijarah's broad flexibility and untapped financing potential across sectors.","simpleExplanation":"A bank that owns a leased asset can sell it (whole or in part) to someone else, and the lease just continues with the new owner stepping into the old owner's shoes. But you can't just sell the RIGHT TO COLLECT RENT on its own without transferring ownership - that would be selling money for money, which isn't allowed. This ability to sell ownership shares in a leased asset is what makes 'Ijarah Sukuk' possible - certificates representing a share in a real leased asset, whose holders earn rental income but also share the real risks (maintenance costs, tenant default, asset destruction). Because Ijarah is so flexible (rent can be paid before, during, or after use; the asset doesn't even need to exist yet, if well-described), it has huge, still-largely-untapped potential for financing everything from cars to government infrastructure like airports.","academicExplanation":"Shari'ah experts permit the lessor to sell the leased asset (in whole or in part) to any third party, transferring the associated rights/obligations, while the Ijarah continues validly. This is particularly relevant for high-cost assets (aircraft, ships, industrial equipment, roads, bridges). However, if ownership is NOT transferred and only the rental right is assigned as a purely monetary transaction, this is NOT allowed - it would amount to selling money (rental) for money, requiring equal-for-equal exchange (i.e. one cannot charge more rent than one is paying, which defeats the purpose of such an assignment). The new party to whom the leased asset is sold assumes every right the former lessor had, and every liability under the lease transaction - this creates strong potential for securitization and a secondary market on an Ijarah basis. If sold to many investors, each individual's proportionate ownership can be evidenced by certificates ('Ijarah Sukuk' or leasing certificates), representing proportionate ownership of the physical assets tied to the Ijarah contract - holders assume owner/lessor rights and obligations to that extent, including the duty to maintain the asset so the lessee can derive maximum usufruct. Consequently, Ijarah Sukuk are subject to REAL risks: market risk from potential asset price changes, maintenance/insurance (Takaful) cost changes, and the lessee's ability/willingness to pay rental instalments; in total asset destruction, Sukuk holders suffer loss pro rata to their ownership. Because of this, Ijarah Sukuk may generate only a QUASI-fixed return, since default or unforeseen expenses could arise - the contractual rent amount represents a MAXIMUM return subject to deduction for unexpected expenses, not a guarantee. On Ijarah's broader potential: it is the most important financing mode for Islamic banks across retail, corporate, and public sectors, with huge unrealized potential - used directly for plant/machinery, automobiles, housing, consumer durables, and indirectly via Sukuk issuance by corporate/government sectors; it's conducive to fixed-asset formation and medium/long-term investment. Key flexibility features driving this potential: (a) rental payment timing can be entirely unrelated to the usufruct period (payable before, during, or after, as mutually agreed) - though accrual still depends on the lessee's actual ability to use the asset; (b) Ijarah can be contracted on an EXISTING asset, or one yet to be constructed, as long as it is fully described (not for unidentified specific items) and both the asset and rent are clearly known at contract time; (c) rate can be fixed or floating with a mutually agreed formula (floor and cap), constant/increasing/decreasing via benchmarking for renewable terms; (d) Ijarah Sukuk holders jointly acquire ownership, bear price risk/ownership costs, and share rent from leasing to users. This flexibility lets governments use Ijarah as an alternative to interest-based borrowing (provided they have durable usable assets - commercial viability is not required, only genuine use), with Sukuk proceeds used to purchase leasable assets and rental distributed to holders, tradable in the secondary market at market price (purchasers stepping into sellers' pro rata ownership and all rights/obligations). A worked example: a cash-short government wanting to build an airport can contract a builder, then undertake to lease and sell the airport to the public via Ijarah Sukuk of various denominations/maturities - the government pays the contractor from Sukuk proceeds and is not obliged to pay investors anything beyond the facility's actual income.","examExplanation":"Lessor CAN sell the leased asset (whole/part) to a third party - lease continues, rights/obligations transfer to the new owner. Lessor CANNOT assign merely the RENT RIGHT without transferring ownership as a pure monetary transaction (= selling money for money = must be equal-for-equal, defeating the purpose = not allowed). Selling ownership shares creates Ijarah Sukuk - certificates of proportionate ownership in the real leased asset, NOT a guaranteed-return bond: holders bear market risk, maintenance/Takaful cost risk, lessee-default risk, and pro rata destruction loss - so returns are QUASI-fixed at best (rent = maximum return, subject to deduction for unexpected expenses). Ijarah's flexibility (rent timing independent of usufruct period, can cover to-be-constructed but well-described assets, fixed or floored/capped floating rates) gives it huge financing potential, including government infrastructure Sukuk (e.g. airport financing) as an interest-free borrowing alternative.","keyPoints":["A lessor can sell the leased asset (whole or part) to a third party, with the lease continuing and rights/obligations transferring to the new owner.","Assigning only the right to collect rental (without transferring ownership) as a purely monetary transaction is NOT allowed - it would amount to selling money for money, which must be equal-for-equal, defeating the purpose of such an assignment.","Ijarah Sukuk represent proportionate ownership in real leased assets, NOT a guaranteed fixed return - holders bear genuine market risk, maintenance/Takaful cost risk, lessee-payment risk, and pro rata loss on total asset destruction, making returns at most quasi-fixed.","Ijarah can be contracted on an existing asset OR one yet to be constructed, as long as it is fully described (not identified to a specific unresolved item) and both asset and rent are clearly known at contract time.","Ijarah's flexibility (rent timing independent of usufruct period, fixed-or-floored/capped floating rates, well-described future assets) gives it large, still-largely-unrealized financing potential across retail, corporate, and public sectors, including government infrastructure financing as an interest-free borrowing alternative."],"definitions":[{"term":"Ijarah Sukuk","definition":"Certificates (also called leasing certificates) representing proportionate ownership of physical assets tied to an Ijarah contract; holders assume owner/lessor rights and obligations to that extent, and their return is quasi-fixed (a maximum, subject to deduction for unexpected expenses), not guaranteed."}],"conditions":["Assigning only the right to collect rental, without transferring ownership, as a purely monetary transaction, is not permitted - it would constitute selling money for money without equal value."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Government Airport Financing via Ijarah Sukuk","kind":"textbook","text":"A cash-short government wanting to build an airport contracts a builder, then undertakes to lease and sell the airport to the public via Ijarah Sukuk of various denominations and maturities. The government pays the contractor from Sukuk proceeds and is not obliged to pay investors anything different from the facility's actual income."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think Ijarah Sukuk offer a guaranteed fixed return like a conventional bond. The chapter is explicit that the rent amount represents only a MAXIMUM return, subject to deduction for unexpected expenses, maintenance costs, or lessee default - making the actual return quasi-fixed at best."],"distinctions":["Selling the leased ASSET itself (permitted, lease continues with the new owner) vs assigning only the RIGHT TO RENT without ownership transfer (not permitted, amounts to selling money for money)."],"relatedConcepts":["Sukuk","Securitization","Riba"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.3-11.5.4","pages":[297,298]}}]},{"id":"t11.5.4","section":"11.5.4","title":"Potential of Ijarah","pages":[297,298],"tier":"core","concepts":["ijarah","ijarah-sukuk"],"intuition":"Why does the author call Ijarah the most important mode?","simpleExplanation":"Ijarah serves retail, corporate and public sectors directly (machinery, autos, housing, durables) and via Sukuk. Flexibility: rent can be paid before, during or after the period (accrual depends on usability); assets can be existing or to be built (if described); rent fixed or floating with floor and cap; Sukuk holders co-own and share rent. Governments with durable assets can replace interest borrowing; Ijarah Sukuk are tradable at market price, aiding liquidity. Example: an airport financed by Ijarah Sukuk.","academicExplanation":["Ijarah is the most important mode for Islamic banks’ retail, corporate and public-sector financing with huge unrealised potential: directly for plant, machinery, autos, housing and durables, indirectly for Sukuk; conducive to fixed-asset formation. Features: payment of rent can be unrelated to usufruct timing (before, during, after), though accrual depends on possible usage; Ijarah can be on existing assets or ones to be constructed if fully described and not identified items, with asset and rent known; rate fixed or floating with a clear formula, floor and cap; first-term rent clear; holders of Ijarah Sukuk co-own, bear price risk and ownership costs and share rent. Governments can use it instead of interest borrowing if they have durable usable assets (commercial viability does not matter); Sukuk proceeds buy assets for lease; rents distributed; tradable at market price, purchasers stepping into pro rata ownership — helping liquidity management. Example: a government builds an airport via a contractor, undertakes to lease it, and sells Ijarah Sukuk of different denominations and maturities; the lease value (≥ construction cost) is spread across Sukuk; the government pays the contractor from proceeds and owes investors only actual income from the facility."],"examExplanation":"Ijarah: most important mode — retail, corporate, public; Sukuk. Flexibility: timing of rent, future assets, floating rent with floor/cap, co-ownership. Govt Ijarah Sukuk (airport example); tradable → liquidity.","keyPoints":["Commercial viability of government assets does not matter; usability does.","Rent can be paid before, during or after the usufruct period, though it accrues only when use is possible.","Ijarah can be on an asset yet to be built if fully described (not identified).","Floating rent needs a clear formula with a floor and a cap."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Airport via Ijarah Sukuk","kind":"textbook","text":"A government contracts an airport’s construction, undertakes to lease it and issues Ijarah Sukuk of various denominations and maturities; proceeds pay the contractor; investors receive only actual income from the facility."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t15.3.5","t18.3.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"What does the author say is necessary for a government to use Ijarah Sukuk?","options":["Commercially profitable assets","Durable usable assets (commercial viability does not matter)","A AAA rating","Gold reserves"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 298."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t11.6","section":"11.6","title":"Summary of Guidelines for Islamic Bankers on Ijarah (Boxes 11.1–11.4)","pages":[298,305],"tier":"core","concepts":["ijarah","imbt","risk-management"],"intuition":"What checklist and tools should an Ijarah banker keep at hand?","simpleExplanation":"Guidelines: no difference between operating and finance lease if the four essentials are met and ownership risk stays with the lessor; lease of an identified asset can’t start before possession; no hire-and-purchase in one contract; transfer by separate sale/gift deed, promise binding only on promisor (AAOIFI’s lessor-promise preferred over lessee undertakings used in Pakistan); lessor bears corpus costs (Takaful, accidental repairs), lessee operating costs; joint acquisition with proportional rent. Box 11.1 risk tools; Box 11.2 auto Ijarah vs conventional; Box 11.3 case study; Box 11.4 accounting.","academicExplanation":["Guidelines: (1) no difference between operating and finance leases in Islamic principles if the four essentials (parties, subject, consideration, period) are met; IMBT is the modern form; ownership risk with lessor; sale separate. (2) Lease of an identified asset cannot commence before possession; if possession timing is unknown, the arrangement is provisional. (3) Two contracts in one are impermissible — no hire and purchase in a single agreement. (4) At the end, transfer to the client or sell in the market; proper sale or gift deed; promise binding only on the promisor; AAOIFI provides for the lessor’s promise, while many banks (e.g. Pakistan) take the lessee’s undertaking deemed binding on him — abiding by AAOIFI seems nearer the spirit. (5) Lessor bears corpus expenses (Takaful, accidental repairs); lessee operating expenses; Takaful and costs may be recovered in rent transparently; Takaful paid by the customer as agent is reimbursed. (6) Joint acquisition and lease of the bank’s share with proportional rent.","Box 11.1 risk mitigation: refusal to lease → binding promise + HJ, sell and recover actual loss; rent default → charity undertaking, HJ, collateral; major maintenance/destruction → Takaful; early termination → take back and sell, or undertaking to buy at a pre-agreed price schedule; careless use → trust receipt making negligence the customer’s; inflation → benchmarked floating rent with floor and cap; customer may not buy at maturity → lease assets with resale value or separate promise to purchase.","Box 11.2 auto Ijarah vs conventional: hire–purchase vs no void condition, sale not part; customer bears all loss vs bank bears ownership risk, customer usage costs; insurance by lessee vs Takaful at lessor’s expense (may raise rent by consent); insurance shortfall on customer vs bank bears claim-settlement risk; rent continues after theft/destruction vs rent void; unilateral lessor termination vs binding, termination only on lessee contravention; late fees as income (Riba) vs charity undertaking; automatic transfer vs formal sale deed optional; upfront downpayment, insurance, first rent vs refundable security deposit, recovery of actual expenses only.","Box 11.3 case (ABC Textile, Merit Islamic Bank; 10% security deposit): trucks — direct lease (bank buys from market); cars (L/C already opened), dyeing plant and looms (client-owned) — sale and lease-back, with the condition of no early retirement before one year and documents (sale deed, undertaking, Ijarah agreement, schedule, promissory note, promise to sell/purchase). Looms damaged by poor maintenance — client repairs at his cost; rent continues; Shari’ah advisor may consider relaxation. Sale of ten cars at Rs.400,000 each vs outstanding Rs.350,000 + prepaid Rs.20,000 = Rs.370,000 each → covers Rs.3.7m, profit Rs.300,000 (Rs.30,000 per car). A car destroyed without negligence: loss is the bank’s; refund Rs.50,000 security; under AAOIFI 8/8 also give Takaful surplus Rs.450,000 − Rs.370,000 = Rs.80,000 → total Rs.130,000.","Box 11.4 accounting (AAOIFI): operating Ijarah — assets at historical cost, depreciated, ‘investments in Ijarah assets’; revenue allocated over the term; initial direct costs allocated or expensed; repair provisions. IMBT through gift — no residual value deducted. IMBT for token or specified amount — residual value deducted; if the lessee doesn’t buy, lower of cash equivalent and book value; if he promised but doesn’t buy, shortfall a receivable. IMBT by sale before term for remaining instalments — normal depreciation. Impairment where instalments exceed fair rent without lessee fault → liability."],"examExplanation":"Guidelines (six). Box 11.1 risk tools (promise+HJ, charity undertaking, Takaful, price schedule, trust receipt, floating rent with cap/floor, resaleable assets). Box 11.2 auto Ijarah vs conventional. Box 11.3 numbers: Rs.3.7m outstanding, Rs.300,000 profit; Rs.130,000 to client on destruction. Box 11.4 accounting by IMBT type.","keyPoints":["AAOIFI lessor promise preferred over lessee undertaking.","Trust receipt binds lessee to careful use.","Case: destroyed car → Rs.50,000 deposit + Rs.80,000 Takaful surplus."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Box 11.3 — destroyed car","kind":"textbook","text":"Outstanding investment and prepaid expenses Rs.370,000; Takaful claim Rs.450,000; security deposit Rs.50,000. The bank returns the Rs.50,000 deposit and, per AAOIFI 8/8, the Rs.80,000 Takaful surplus — Rs.130,000 in total."},{"title":"Box 11.3 — neglected looms","kind":"textbook","text":"The Takaful evaluator found five looms broke down through the client’s poor maintenance; the client repairs them at his cost and rent continues (p. 303)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t11.5.1","t14.4.1","t4.2.9"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In Box 11.3, the client offers Rs.400,000 per car for ten cars; outstanding investment is Rs.350,000 and prepaid expenses Rs.20,000 per car. What is the bank’s total profit?","options":["Rs.500,000","Rs.300,000","Rs.200,000","Rs.3.7 million"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"10 × (400,000 − 370,000) = Rs.300,000 (p. 303)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch11-t15"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch11-t15","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"11.6","sectionTitle":"Summary of Guidelines for Islamic Bankers on Ijarah","title":"Summary Guidelines and Risk Management for Islamic Bankers on Ijarah","covers":["t11.6"],"overview":"Consolidates the chapter's key guidelines for Islamic bankers on Ijarah, contrasts Islamic Ijarah with conventional auto leasing, and catalogs the practical risk-mitigation toolkit banks use.","simpleExplanation":"The book wraps up with a checklist for bankers: operating and finance leases aren't fundamentally different in Islamic law as long as the four essentials are met and ownership risk stays with the lessor; a lease on a specific asset can't start before the bank actually has it; you can't bundle hire and purchase into one single agreement; at lease end, transfer ownership through a proper separate sale or gift, with the promise binding only on whoever made it; and the lessor always pays for ownership-related costs like Takaful and major repairs, while the tenant pays for day-to-day running costs. A side-by-side comparison with conventional auto leasing shows exactly where Islamic Ijarah differs - especially on who bears the risk if the car is stolen or destroyed, and what happens if rent is paid late.","academicExplanation":"Six consolidated guidelines: (1) There is no fundamental Islamic-finance difference between operating and finance leases - if all four essential Ijarah elements (contracting parties, subject matter, consideration, period) are properly observed, Ijarah can be used as a modern financing mode via Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek; the deciding factor is that ownership-related risk must remain with the lessor, and sale must be kept separate from the lease. (2) The lease of an IDENTIFIED asset cannot commence before the bank takes possession of it; if the possession timing is unknown, the whole arrangement remains provisional. (3) Combining two contracts (hire and purchase) into a single agreement is not permissible - IFIs cannot build a hire-purchase-style combined agreement. (4) At lease-period end, the bank can transfer ownership to the client or dispose of the asset in the open market; if transferring to the lessee, a proper sale agreement or gift deed must be executed, and the promise to transfer is binding ONLY on the promisor - the other party retains the option not to proceed. AAOIFI's Standard provides for the PROMISE running from the LESSOR; many Islamic banks (e.g. in Pakistan) instead take an undertaking from the LESSEE and deem it binding on him - the author considers abiding by AAOIFI's Standard (promise from the lessor) more justifiable and closer to the spirit of Shari'ah. (5) The lessor bears corpus-related expenses (Takaful, accidental repairs); the lessee bears operating expenses related to running the asset - Takaful and similar bank-incurred costs can be recovered in the lease rental (subject to transparency/mutual understanding), and if the customer pays Takaful as the bank's agent, the bank reimburses him. (6) A bank can jointly acquire an asset with a customer wanting to lease it, then lease out its own share as per the customer's undertaking - rental received must be proportional to the bank's ownership share. Box 11.1 (Risk Mitigation) matches key Ijarah risks to tools: customer refusing to take a purchased asset on lease -> binding promise to lease + Hamish Jiddiyah (actual loss recoverable from it); rental default -> charity-bound undertaking + Hamish Jiddiyah + collateral realization; major maintenance/destruction risk -> Takaful; early termination -> asset resale in the market, or a pre-agreed purchase-price undertaking for riskier cases; careless lessee use -> a trust receipt binding the lessee to use the asset as trustee, with negligence-caused loss borne by him; inflation/rate-of-return risk -> a benchmarked floating rental rate (with floor/cap); sale-at-maturity risk (customer may not buy) -> only leasing assets with sufficient resale value, or obtaining a separate purchase promise. Box 11.2 contrasts Islamic Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek with conventional auto leasing across several dimensions: conventional financing leases contain impermissible hire-purchase bundling, while Ijarah keeps sale separate; conventional leases make the customer responsible for ALL loss/damage regardless of fault, while Islamic Ijarah keeps ownership risk with the bank (customer bears only usage expenses); conventional insurance is charged directly to the lessee as an independent cost, while Takaful should be at the lessor's expense (recoverable via rent increase WITH lessee consent); if conventional insurance underpays, the customer covers the balance, while under Ijarah the bank itself bears Takaful claim settlement risk; conventional leases continue charging rent on a stolen/destroyed vehicle until insurance settlement, while under Ijarah, rent becomes void immediately upon destruction/theft (since rent is consideration for usage, which no longer exists); conventional leases often give the lessor unrestricted unilateral termination power, while Ijarah is binding and terminable only for lessee contravention (per an agreed clause); conventional late-payment charges become lessor income, while under Ijarah, the customer may undertake to pay a charity amount for late rent, but the bank cannot charge any further return; conventional leases automatically transfer the vehicle to the customer at lease end, while under Ijarah, the customer is NOT obliged to purchase and may do so via a formal sale deed only if he considers it beneficial; and conventional leases require substantial upfront payment (downpayment, first year's insurance, first month's rent), while Islamic banks normally take only a refundable security deposit, recoverable only to the extent of actual expenses (excluding cost of funds) if the lease is not finalized.","examExplanation":"6 core guidelines: (1) no fundamental Islamic distinction between operating/finance lease if 4 essentials + lessor-bears-risk + sale-kept-separate are observed; (2) identified-asset lease cannot start before bank possession; (3) hire+purchase cannot be one combined agreement; (4) ownership transfer via separate sale/gift, promise binding only on promisor - AAOIFI structures the promise as running FROM THE LESSOR (author's preferred, more Shari'ah-aligned approach), though many banks instead take a binding undertaking FROM THE LESSEE; (5) lessor bears corpus costs (Takaful, major repairs), lessee bears operating costs; (6) joint asset acquisition -> rental proportional to bank's ownership share. Box 11.2 key contrasts vs conventional auto lease: Islamic Ijarah keeps ALL ownership risk with the bank (theft/destruction voids rent immediately, vs conventional continuing to charge rent until insurance settles); late payment goes to charity in Ijarah (vs lessor income conventionally); customer NOT obliged to buy at lease end in Ijarah (vs automatic transfer conventionally); Islamic banks take only a refundable security deposit (vs substantial non-refundable upfront conventional payments).","keyPoints":["No fundamental Islamic-finance distinction exists between operating and finance leases - what matters is that all four Ijarah essentials are observed, ownership risk stays with the lessor, and sale is kept separate from the lease.","AAOIFI structures the ownership-transfer promise as running FROM THE LESSOR (binding the bank, giving the lessee a free option); many Islamic banks instead take a binding undertaking FROM THE LESSEE - the author considers the AAOIFI approach more justifiable and closer to the spirit of Shari'ah.","A key Box 11.2 contrast: under conventional auto leasing, the lessor continues charging rent on a stolen/destroyed vehicle until insurance settlement; under Islamic Ijarah, rent becomes void immediately upon destruction/theft, since rent is consideration for usage which no longer exists.","Under Islamic Ijarah, late-payment charges must go to charity, and the bank cannot charge any further return on late rent - unlike conventional leases, where late fees become lessor income.","Islamic banks normally take only a refundable security deposit (recoverable only to the extent of actual expenses, excluding cost of funds, if the lease isn't finalized) - unlike conventional leases, which typically require substantial non-refundable upfront payments (downpayment, first year's insurance, first month's rent)."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["Combining hire and purchase into a single agreement is not permissible in Shari'ah.","The ownership-transfer promise must be binding only on the promisor, with the other party retaining a free option not to proceed."],"principles":["The deciding factor for whether a modern lease qualifies as valid Ijarah is that ownership-related risk must remain with the lessor throughout, and the sale/gift transaction must be kept separate from the lease."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Box 11.2 - Stolen/Destroyed Vehicle Comparison","kind":"textbook","text":"Under conventional auto leasing, if the leased vehicle is stolen or completely destroyed, the leasing company continues charging lease rent until the insurance claim is settled. Under Islamic Ijarah, since rent is consideration for usage of the asset, the concept of rental becomes void immediately upon the asset's theft or destruction - the bank cannot charge rental for a period when the asset no longer exists to be used."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume Islamic banks always take the promise-to-transfer-ownership from the lessee (since this is common practice in some jurisdictions like Pakistan). The book clarifies that AAOIFI's Standard actually structures this promise as running FROM THE LESSOR, which the author considers the more Shari'ah-aligned approach."],"distinctions":["AAOIFI's preferred structure (promise to transfer running from the LESSOR, binding the bank) vs common bank practice in some jurisdictions (binding undertaking taken from the LESSEE) - the author favors the former as closer to the spirit of Shari'ah."],"relatedConcepts":["Hamish Jiddiyah","Takaful","Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.6","pages":[298,299,300,301]}}],"table":{"caption":"Box 11.2 — Auto Ijarah vs conventional leasing (condensed)","head":["Issue","Conventional lease","Islamic IMBT"],"rows":[["Structure","Hire–purchase (sale built in)","Pure Ijarah; sale separate"],["Loss/damage","Customer bears all","Bank bears ownership risk; customer usage costs"],["Insurance","Lessee pays; bears shortfall","Takaful at lessor’s expense; bank bears claim risk"],["Theft/total loss","Rent continues until claim settled","Rent ceases"],["Termination","Lessor may terminate unilaterally","Binding; only for lessee’s contravention"],["Late payment","Extra charge as income (Riba)","Charity undertaking only"],["End of term","Automatic transfer","Optional formal sale deed"],["Upfront","Downpayment, insurance, first rent","Refundable security deposit; only actual expenses recoverable"]]},"calc":{"type":"ijarah-case","note":"Reproduces Box 11.3 Answers 3 and 4: early purchase price vs outstanding investment, and refund on destruction under AAOIFI 8/8."}}],"questions":[{"id":"q11.1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which is NOT an essential of an Ijarah contract?","options":["Known usufruct","Specified period","Transfer of ownership of the asset","Agreed rent"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"Ownership is not transferred in Ijarah (p. 280).","topicId":"t11.2","chapter":11,"section":"11.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall essentials","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.2","pages":[280,280]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.2","type":"tf","prompt":"A bank may charge rent from the date it pays the supplier, even if the asset has not yet been delivered to the lessee.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Rent is due only once the lessee can use the asset (pp. 282, 289).","topicId":"t11.4.4","chapter":11,"section":"11.4.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply rent-accrual rule","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.4","pages":[289,289]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.3","type":"scenario","prompt":"A leased truck is destroyed in a flood; the lessee was not negligent. What happens?","options":["The lessee keeps paying rent and replaces the truck","The lease of that identified asset terminates; the lessor bears the loss and cannot claim further rent","Rent doubles","The lessee pays half"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 295.","topicId":"t11.5.1","chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply destruction rules","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","pages":[293,295]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.4","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are permissible in Ijarah according to Chapter 11? (Select all.)","options":["Rent increasing 5% per year as agreed upfront","Rent linked to inflation for future periods","Unilateral rent increase by the lessor mid-term","Rent benchmarked to a reference rate with first-period rent fixed","Charging rent for the remaining period on early termination"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3],"explanation":"pp. 283–286.","topicId":"t11.3.2","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply rent rules","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","pages":[283,284]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.5","type":"match","prompt":"Match each lease type to its Shari’ah status per the chapter.","pairs":[["Operating lease (lessor keeps risk)","Compatible if other conditions met"],["Hire–purchase","Not suitable — sale built into lease"],["Security lease","Disguised security agreement"],["IMBT","Compliant if ownership transfer separate"]],"explanation":"Sections 11.4–11.5.","topicId":"t11.4","chapter":11,"section":"11.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Classify lease types","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4","pages":[287,289]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.6","type":"identify","prompt":"A lease ending in transfer of ownership through a separate unilateral promise to sell or gift is:","options":["Ijarah Mosufah bil Zimmah","Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek","Hire–purchase","Musharakah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 291.","topicId":"t11.5","chapter":11,"section":"11.5","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify IMBT","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5","pages":[291,293]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.7","type":"comparison","prompt":"Which statement correctly compares conventional auto leasing and Islamic IMBT (Box 11.2)?","options":["Both continue rent after theft","Conventional rent continues until the insurance claim is settled; in IMBT rent ceases if the vehicle is stolen or destroyed","IMBT lets the bank terminate unilaterally","Conventional leases have no late fee"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 301.","topicId":"t11.6","chapter":11,"section":"11.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Compare leasing products","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.6","pages":[298,305]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.8","type":"application","prompt":"Box 11.3 variant: outstanding investment + prepaid = Rs.370,000; Takaful claim Rs.420,000; security deposit Rs.50,000; car destroyed without negligence. Per AAOIFI 8/8 the client should receive:","options":["Rs.50,000","Rs.100,000","Rs.470,000","Nothing"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Deposit 50,000 + surplus (420,000 − 370,000 = 50,000) = Rs.100,000. (Practice variant — generated for learning.)","topicId":"t11.6","chapter":11,"section":"11.6","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply AAOIFI 8/8","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.6","pages":[298,305]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.9","type":"tf","prompt":"In sale and lease-back IMBT, the asset may be transferred back to the client immediately after the lease starts.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"A reasonable period (normally one year) is needed to avoid ‘Inah (p. 293).","topicId":"t11.5","chapter":11,"section":"11.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply sale and lease-back rule","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5","pages":[291,293]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.10","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why is assigning only the rental stream of a lease (without the asset) for money not allowed?","options":["Rents are too small","It amounts to selling money for money, which must be equal","It requires Takaful","It is Musharakah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 297.","topicId":"t11.5.3","chapter":11,"section":"11.5.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain securitisation rules","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.3","pages":[297,297]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.11","type":"order","prompt":"Order the IMBT procedure described in Section 11.5.1.","items":["MoU and undertaking with Hamish Jiddiyah","Bank purchases asset (directly or via agent)","Asset delivered in usable form","Formal Ijarah executed; rent accrues","Separate sale/gift at the end"],"explanation":"pp. 293–294.","topicId":"t11.5.1","chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Sequence IMBT","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","pages":[293,295]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.12","type":"short","prompt":"Explain why the ‘two contracts in one’ objection to IMBT is rejected.","keywords":["unilateral","promise","separate","Riba","effective"],"correctAnswer":"IMBT consists of an Ijarah that takes effect immediately and a separate unilateral promise to sell or gift at the end, which binds only the promisor and is not a transaction until executed. There is no sale precondition in the lease, no Riba or injustice, and it is fair that the lessee who paid cost plus rent gets ownership (accepted by the OIC Fiqh Academy).","explanation":"Section 11.4.5.","topicId":"t11.4.5","chapter":11,"section":"11.4.5","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Evaluate IMBT structure","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.5","pages":[289,290]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.13","type":"scenario","prompt":"A lessee sub-leases a shop at a higher rent than he pays, with the lessor’s consent. According to contemporary scholars:","options":["He must give the surplus to charity","He may keep the surplus (Shafi‘i/Hanbali view preferred)","Sub-lease is prohibited","The lessor gets the surplus"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 285.","topicId":"t11.3.3","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply sub-lease rules","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.3","pages":[284,285]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.14","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the literal root of the word Ijarah?","options":["Ribh","Al-Ajr — compensation or counter value","Salaf","Kafalah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 279.","topicId":"t11.1","chapter":11,"section":"11.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the meaning of Ijarah","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.1","pages":[279,279]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.15","type":"match","prompt":"Match the term to its meaning.","pairs":[["Ujrah","Rent for things"],["Ajr","Wage for people"],["Ajr al-Musammah","Rent fixed in the contract"],["Ajr al-Mithl","Rent set by a judge/arbitrator"]],"explanation":"p. 280.","topicId":"t11.2","chapter":11,"section":"11.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Ijarah terminology","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.2","pages":[280,280]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.16","type":"scenario","prompt":"A leased car is damaged in a flood while properly parked. Who bears the loss?","options":["The lessee","The lessor — the lessee is a trustee liable only for negligence","Shared equally","The Takaful company only, with the lessee paying any gap"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 280, 285.","topicId":"t11.2","chapter":11,"section":"11.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the trustee principle","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.2","pages":[280,280]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.17","type":"tf","prompt":"If a lessee receives the leased asset as a gift from the lessor, the Ijarah continues until its term ends.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"The lease ceases when the lessee becomes owner (p. 280).","topicId":"t11.2.1","chapter":11,"section":"11.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Contrast Ijarah and ownership","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.2.1","pages":[280,281]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.18","type":"identify","prompt":"Which of these CAN be leased?","options":["Fuel","Money","A delivery van","Candles"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"Only assets whose corpus is not consumed (p. 281).","topicId":"t11.3","chapter":11,"section":"11.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify leasable assets","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3","pages":[281,282]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.19","type":"application","prompt":"Someone “leases” 100,000 dollars to a trader for a monthly rent. What is the Shari’ah view?","options":["Valid Ijarah","It is deemed a loan; the rent is interest","Valid if short-term","A Mudarabah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 281.","topicId":"t11.3","chapter":11,"section":"11.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the rule on leasing money","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3","pages":[281,282]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.20","type":"comparison","prompt":"What was the classical difference between Hanafis and Shafi‘is on when rent becomes due?","options":["No difference","Hanafis: when usufruct emerges; Shafi‘is: at contract, presuming use — both now require the asset in the lessee’s usable possession","Shafi‘is: never","Hanafis: before delivery"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 282.","topicId":"t11.3","chapter":11,"section":"11.3","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Compare views on accrual of rent","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3","pages":[281,282]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.21","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank wants to lease a specific machine it has not yet bought. What should it do?","options":["Sign the Ijarah now","Take a promise to lease (with earnest money or advance rent) and sign the Ijarah after it owns the machine","Charge rent from payment date","Use Murabaha only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 283.","topicId":"t11.3.1","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply execution rules for specified assets","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.1","pages":[282,283]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.22","type":"tf","prompt":"Ijarah Mosufah bil Zimmah can be executed for future enforcement even before the lessor has a particular unit.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"It is a described, not identified, asset (p. 283).","topicId":"t11.3.1","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply future-enforcement rules","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.1","pages":[282,283]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.23","type":"multi","prompt":"Which rent-setting methods are permissible in long-term Ijarah? (Select all.)","options":["Rent based on the lessor’s total asset cost","Periodic increase by an agreed proportion (e.g. 5% a year)","Floating rent tied to a benchmark with cap and floor","Unilateral increase by the lessor at will"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"p. 283.","topicId":"t11.3.2","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply rent-setting rules","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","pages":[283,284]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.24","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why do scholars suggest a ceiling on how far rent can move with a reference rate?","options":["To guarantee profit","To avoid Gharar and Jahala for both parties","Because benchmarks are Haram","For tax reasons"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 284.","topicId":"t11.3.2","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain caps on floating rent","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","pages":[283,284]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.25","type":"identify","prompt":"According to Abu Hanifa, when may a sub-lessor keep surplus rent from a sub-lessee?","options":["Always","If he improved the property or rents in a different currency","Never, under any circumstances","Only for houses"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 284.","topicId":"t11.3.3","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.3","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall views on sub-lease surplus","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.3","pages":[284,285]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.26","type":"tf","prompt":"A lessor may take the cost of funds out of the lessee’s security deposit when rent is late.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Only actual loss; extra over due rent is Riba (p. 285).","topicId":"t11.3.4","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Apply rules on security in Ijarah","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.4","pages":[285,285]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.27","type":"match","prompt":"Match each expense to who bears it in a house lease.","pairs":[["Property tax","Lessor"],["Electricity and water bills","Lessee"],["Structural repair after an earthquake","Lessor"],["Damage from lessee’s misuse","Lessee"]],"explanation":"p. 285.","topicId":"t11.3.5","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Allocate lease expenses","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.5","pages":[285,285]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.28","type":"scenario","prompt":"A lessee breaches the lease and the bank terminates it with two years remaining. What can the bank claim?","options":["All remaining rent","Rent due up to termination plus any loss from the lessee’s negligence","Double rent","Nothing at all"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 286.","topicId":"t11.3.6","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply termination rules","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.6","pages":[286,287]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.29","type":"tf","prompt":"If the lessor sells the leased asset to a third party, the lessee’s consent is required and the lease ends.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Consent is not needed and the lease continues with the new owner (p. 286).","topicId":"t11.3.6","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall effects of selling a leased asset","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.6","pages":[286,287]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.30","type":"multi","prompt":"Which features make conventional financial leases non-compliant? (Select all.)","options":["Rent starts when the lessor pays the supplier","Lessee bears ownership risk","Sale is built into the lease","The lessor owns the asset during the lease"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"pp. 288–289.","topicId":"t11.4","chapter":11,"section":"11.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Evaluate conventional leases","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4","pages":[287,289]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.31","type":"identify","prompt":"Which conventional lease form is considered compatible with Shari’ah if the lessor bears ownership costs?","options":["Hire–purchase","Security lease","Operating lease","Sale-and-repurchase"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 289.","topicId":"t11.4","chapter":11,"section":"11.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify compliant lease forms","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4","pages":[287,289]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.32","type":"comparison","prompt":"In an Islamic operating lease, who bears the costs of the corpus of the asset?","options":["The lessee","The lessor","The supplier","The Takaful company"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 289.","topicId":"t11.4.4","chapter":11,"section":"11.4.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Contrast conventional and Islamic operating leases","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.4","pages":[289,289]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.33","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why do scholars prefer a gift as the way of transferring ownership in IMBT?","options":["Gifts avoid taxes","The lessee has usually paid the full cost plus return through rent, so it is fair that ownership passes free","Sale is prohibited","Gifts are always binding"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 290.","topicId":"t11.4.5","chapter":11,"section":"11.4.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain gift-based transfer","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.5","pages":[289,290]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.34","type":"scenario","prompt":"The Takaful claim on a destroyed leased asset is less than the bank’s loss, and the lessee was not at fault. Who bears the shortfall?","options":["The lessee","The bank","Split 50/50","The supplier"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 291.","topicId":"t11.4.6","chapter":11,"section":"11.4.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply Takaful shortfall rules","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.6","pages":[291,291]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.35","type":"multi","prompt":"Which AAOIFI-approved methods can transfer ownership at the end of IMBT? (Select all.)","options":["Promise to sell for a token or market price","Promise to gift at the end","Gift contingent on paying the remaining instalments","A sale contract signed with the lease, effective at the end"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"A future-dated sale within the lease is not allowed (p. 292).","topicId":"t11.5","chapter":11,"section":"11.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall IMBT transfer methods","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5","pages":[291,293]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.36","type":"tf","prompt":"Under a contingent gift in IMBT, ownership passes automatically once the last instalment is paid, without a new document.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"p. 292.","topicId":"t11.5","chapter":11,"section":"11.5","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Apply the contingent gift method","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5","pages":[291,293]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.37","type":"comparison","prompt":"How does the start of rent differ between Murabaha (MPO) and Ijarah when the client acts as agent?","options":["No difference","In MPO a separate offer and acceptance is needed after the agent takes delivery; in Ijarah the lease can start as soon as the agent takes delivery, since the bank remains owner","Ijarah always needs physical delivery to the bank first","MPO starts at payment"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 293.","topicId":"t11.5.1","chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Compare MPO and Ijarah procedure","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","pages":[293,295]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.38","type":"application","prompt":"A client converting a conventional machinery loan wants sale and lease-back IMBT and asks to buy the machine back after three months. What should the bank say?","options":["Agree","Allow a reasonable period (about one year) so the asset or its value may change, to avoid ‘Inah","Refuse all sale and lease-back","Charge a penalty"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 293, 295.","topicId":"t11.5.1","chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply sale and lease-back rules","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","pages":[293,295]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.39","type":"order","prompt":"Order the IMBT steps described in Section 11.5.1.","items":["MoU and undertaking with Hamish Jiddiyah","Bank buys the asset directly or through an agent","Formal lease signed once the asset is usable; rent accrues","Separate sale or gift transfers ownership at the end"],"explanation":"pp. 293–294.","topicId":"t11.5.1","chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Sequence IMBT","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","pages":[293,295]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.40","type":"identify","prompt":"According to Imam Muhammad, if a leased farm’s crop is destroyed by a natural calamity, the lessee owes:","options":["Full rent","A customary wage, as the Ijarah becomes invalid","Nothing and must compensate the lessor","Double rent"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 296.","topicId":"t11.5.2","chapter":11,"section":"11.5.2","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall rules on impaired usufruct","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.2","pages":[295,296]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.41","type":"multi","prompt":"What are the two non-negotiable principles for Islamic banks in Ijarah (Section 11.5.2)? (Select all.)","options":["Risk cannot be separated from ownership","Lease and sale must be separate and independent","Rent must be fixed for life","Takaful must be conventional"],"correctAnswer":[0,1],"explanation":"p. 296.","topicId":"t11.5.2","chapter":11,"section":"11.5.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall core Ijarah principles","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.2","pages":[295,296]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.42","type":"scenario","prompt":"A lessor wants to sell only its right to future rentals to an investor for cash, keeping the asset. Is this allowed?","options":["Yes","No — it sells money for money; the asset (with rights and obligations) must be sold","Yes at par only","Only to banks"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 297.","topicId":"t11.5.3","chapter":11,"section":"11.5.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply rules on assigning leases","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.3","pages":[297,297]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.43","type":"tf","prompt":"Ijarah Sukuk holders suffer a pro rata loss if the leased asset is totally destroyed.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"They are owners (p. 297).","topicId":"t11.5.3","chapter":11,"section":"11.5.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Understand risk in Ijarah Sukuk","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.3","pages":[297,297]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q11.44","type":"application","prompt":"Box 11.3 variant: a leased car is destroyed without the client’s fault. Outstanding investment plus prepaid costs = Rs.370,000; Takaful pays Rs.450,000; security deposit Rs.50,000. What should the bank give the client under AAOIFI 8/8?","options":["Rs.50,000","Rs.130,000","Rs.80,000","Nothing"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Deposit 50,000 plus claim surplus 80,000 (p. 303).","topicId":"t11.6","chapter":11,"section":"11.6","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply AAOIFI 8/8 to a loss case","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.6","pages":[298,305]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q-ch11-1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Ijarah is the sale of which of the following?","explanation":"Ijarah is the sale of usufruct (Manafa'ah) - the right to use an asset or benefit from labour/service - not the corpus/'Ain of the asset itself.","topicId":"t11.1","chapter":11,"section":"11.1","topicLabel":"Ijarah as a Contract of Usufruct","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define Ijarah as a sale of usufruct and state its essential elements.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.1","pages":[279]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The corpus ('Ain) of an asset","The usufruct (right to use) of an asset or service","A debt instrument","A future commodity"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-2","type":"short","prompt":"List the five essentials of a valid Ijarah contract.","explanation":"These are the five essentials given in section 11.2.","topicId":"t11.2","chapter":11,"section":"11.2","topicLabel":"Essentials of Ijarah and How It Differs from Bai'","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"State the essential elements of Ijarah.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.2","pages":[280]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"(1) It is a contract; (2) known usufruct is transferred; (3) of a particular asset; (4) for a specified time period; (5) against agreed-upon rental."},{"id":"q-ch11-3","type":"tf","prompt":"If a lessee becomes owner of the leased corporeal property (e.g. through gift or inheritance) during the lease term, the Ijarah contract automatically ceases.","explanation":"True - since Ijarah requires ownership to remain with the lessor while only usufruct transfers, the lessee becoming owner (by any means) ends the basis for the Ijarah relationship.","topicId":"t11.2.1","chapter":11,"section":"11.2.1","topicLabel":"Essentials of Ijarah and How It Differs from Bai'","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish Ijarah from Bai'.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.2.1","pages":[280]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":true},{"id":"q-ch11-4","type":"scenario","prompt":"A person 'leases' a sum of money to another party for a fee over a fixed period. Under Ijarah rules, how is this transaction characterized?","explanation":"Money's usufruct cannot be enjoyed without consuming it, so it cannot be leased under Ijarah - such a transaction is deemed a loan, and any 'rent' charged on it is Riba.","topicId":"t11.3","chapter":11,"section":"11.3","topicLabel":"General Juristic Rules of Ijarah","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"State which assets can be leased and why money cannot.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3","pages":[281]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["A valid Ijarah, since money has value","Deemed a loan; any rent charged on it amounts to interest (Riba)","A valid Salam contract","A valid Musawamah"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-5","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to Al-Kasani's conditions, when does rent become due in Ijarah?","explanation":"No rent becomes due merely from contract execution - delivery/availability is essential, though advance rent can be taken once availability for the lease period is ensured.","topicId":"t11.3","chapter":11,"section":"11.3","topicLabel":"General Juristic Rules of Ijarah","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain when rent accrues in Ijarah.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3","pages":[282]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Immediately upon signing the contract, regardless of delivery","Only once the leased subject is delivered and made available to the lessee (though advance rent can be taken once availability is ensured)","Only after the full lease period ends","Rent is never due in Ijarah"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why can an Ijarah contract be validly agreed for future enforcement, unlike Bai'?","explanation":"Future enforcement in Ijarah is allowed because ownership remains with the lessor, who bears asset risk - unlike a future-dated sale, which would improperly delay the definite ownership transfer central to Bai'.","topicId":"t11.3.1","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.1","topicLabel":"Execution of an Ijarah Contract","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain when an Ijarah contract can be executed for future enforcement.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.1","pages":[282,283]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because Ijarah involves no risk at all","Because ownership (and its risk) remains with the lessor throughout, avoiding the Gharar concerns that arise from a future-dated sale","Because Ijarah contracts are never binding","Because Ijarah always involves a third-party guarantee"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-7","type":"scenario","prompt":"An Islamic bank prices a long-term Ijarah's rental using LIBOR as the reference benchmark for periodic adjustments, while genuinely bearing full ownership risk of the leased asset. Is this Shari'ah-compliant, per the chapter?","explanation":"The author explicitly rejects the minority view that LIBOR-benchmarking makes a lease resemble interest; what matters is genuine risk-bearing by the lessor, not the benchmark choice.","topicId":"t11.3.2","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","topicLabel":"Determination of Rent","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Analyze the rules governing rent determination and benchmarking.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","pages":[283,284]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["No - any use of an interest rate benchmark makes the transaction Haram","Yes - the author argues that as long as the lessor bears genuine ownership/corpus risk, benchmarking pricing to LIBOR does not itself create Riba, though non-interest benchmarks are still preferable","No - Ijarah rent must always be fixed and can never be benchmarked","Yes, but only if the lessee also bears ownership risk"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-8","type":"tf","prompt":"A floating rental structure in Ijarah is valid only if a ceiling and floor limit how much the rent can move relative to the benchmark.","explanation":"True - scholars recommend a ceiling/floor (e.g. rent cannot move more than 5%) to avoid Gharar and Jahala.","topicId":"t11.3.2","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","topicLabel":"Determination of Rent","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Analyze the rules governing rent determination.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","pages":[284]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":true},{"id":"q-ch11-9","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which combination of schools represents the preferred contemporary view allowing a sub-lessor to keep a surplus when sub-lease rent exceeds the rent he pays the original lessor?","explanation":"Shafi'is allow the sub-lessor to keep the surplus, and this is the preferred view in the Hanbali school too - the position contemporary scholars prefer.","topicId":"t11.3.3","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.3","topicLabel":"Sub-leasing, Security, and Liability Allocation","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Analyze the rules governing sub-leasing.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.3","pages":[284,285]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Hanafi and Maliki","Shafi'i and Hanbali","Only Maliki","None - it is always prohibited"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-10","type":"scenario","prompt":"A lessee invites several others to share in the rental income he receives from sub-lessees, without transferring any portion of ownership to them, in exchange for a fee. Is this permissible?","explanation":"Sharing rental income without transferring any ownership portion is not acceptable in Shari'ah - it amounts to trading a receivable at a discount, which is Riba.","topicId":"t11.3.3","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.3","topicLabel":"Sub-leasing, Security, and Liability Allocation","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Analyze the rules governing sub-leasing.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.3","pages":[285]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, since sub-leasing is generally allowed","No - this amounts to selling a receivable (right to rent) at a discount, which is Riba","Yes, if the lessor consents","Yes, provided the amounts are disclosed"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-11","type":"mcq","prompt":"Match each expense category to the correct responsible party: (1) Property tax on a leased house; (2) Electricity bill from using the house.","explanation":"Ownership-related expenses (property tax) fall on the lessor (owner); usage-related expenses (electricity) fall on the lessee.","topicId":"t11.3.5","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.5","topicLabel":"Sub-leasing, Security, and Liability Allocation","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain the liabilities of lessor and lessee.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.5","pages":[285]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"matching","options":["1-Lessor, 2-Lessee","1-Lessee, 2-Lessor","Both are the lessor's responsibility","Both are the lessee's responsibility"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch11-12","type":"scenario","prompt":"A lessee breaches an Ijarah agreement, and the lessor exercises a contractual right to terminate and repossess. What can the lessor legitimately collect from the lessee?","explanation":"The lessor can collect only rent already due up to termination, plus compensation for actual damage caused by the lessee's misuse/negligence - never rent for the remaining unused period.","topicId":"t11.3.6","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.6","topicLabel":"Termination, Amendment, and Rent Default Rules","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain the rules on termination and amendment.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.6","pages":[286,287]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Rent for the full remaining lease period","Only rent already due up to the termination date (plus compensation for any actual damage from misuse/negligence)","Double the remaining rent as a penalty","Nothing at all"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-13","type":"tf","prompt":"A charge on overdue Ijarah rental collected as bank income is permissible, since rental constitutes a debt.","explanation":"False - a charge on overdue rental would be Riba. A penalty is permitted only if directed to charity, never as bank income.","topicId":"t11.3","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.7","topicLabel":"Termination, Amendment, and Rent Default Rules","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain rules on failure to pay rent.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.7","pages":[287]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch11-14","type":"mcq","prompt":"In a conventional financial lease, from what point does rental typically begin accruing?","explanation":"In conventional financial lease, the lease commences on the day the price is paid by the lessor, irrespective of whether the lessee has taken delivery - conflicting with Shari'ah's delivery-based accrual rule.","topicId":"t11.4","chapter":11,"section":"11.4.1","topicLabel":"Modern Forms of Leasing","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish conventional financial, security, and operating leases from Shari'ah-compliant Ijarah.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.1","pages":[288]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["From the date the lessee takes delivery of the asset","From the date the lessor pays the supplier, regardless of whether the lessee has taken delivery","From the date the lease agreement is drafted","From the date the lease terminates"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-15","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are identified as Shari'ah objections to conventional lease structures?","explanation":"The Shari'ah objections are: bundled ownership transfer, rent accruing before usable delivery, and shifting all risk to the lessee. The lessor bearing major repairs is the SHARI'AH REQUIREMENT, not an objection to conventional practice (which instead shifts these costs to the lessee).","topicId":"t11.4.4","chapter":11,"section":"11.4.4","topicLabel":"Appraisal of Conventional Leases from the Shari'ah Angle","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify the specific Shari'ah objections to conventional lease structures.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.4","pages":[289]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Ownership transfer is often bundled into the lease contract from the outset","Rent starts accruing before the lessee can benefit from the asset","The lessor bears major repair/defect-rectification costs","All risks are typically shifted to the lessee"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3]},{"id":"q-ch11-16","type":"short","prompt":"Explain how Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek avoids the prohibition on combining two contracts into one bargain.","explanation":"This reflects the three-ground rebuttal given in section 11.4.5.","topicId":"t11.4.5","chapter":11,"section":"11.4.5","topicLabel":"Combining Two Contracts","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain the 'two contracts kept separate' principle behind Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.5","pages":[290]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"The transaction is structured as ONE lease contract (immediately effective) plus a SEPARATE unilateral promise (not itself a binding transaction until exercised) by the lessor to sell or gift the asset at lease end. Since the promise binds only the promisor (lessor) and the promisee (lessee) has a free option not to proceed, it does not constitute a second binding contract - avoiding the prohibited combination of two contracts in one bargain."},{"id":"q-ch11-17","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why is a BILATERAL (mutual) promise to transfer ownership avoided in Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek?","explanation":"A mutual (bilateral) promise, where both parties are bound, would function as a contract in itself - which is prohibited in this context, since the sale conditions (e.g. seller possession) aren't yet fulfilled at the promise stage.","topicId":"t11.4.5","chapter":11,"section":"11.4.5","topicLabel":"Combining Two Contracts","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the three permissible methods for transferring ownership at lease end.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.5","pages":[290]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Bilateral promises are always more expensive","A bilateral promise would itself constitute a prohibited premature contract","Bilateral promises are only for Musharakah","There is no reason to avoid it"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-18","type":"scenario","prompt":"An Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek asset's ownership transfer becomes impossible for reasons not attributable to the lessee (e.g. asset destruction). The lessee had been paying rent higher than fair market rental due to the bank including acquisition/Takaful costs. What must the bank do?","explanation":"If ownership transfer becomes impossible for reasons not attributable to the lessee, the bank must refund the difference between the rent actually paid and the fair market rental of similar assets, per AAOIFI clause 8/8.","topicId":"t11.4.6","chapter":11,"section":"11.4.6","topicLabel":"Takaful and Insurance Expenses in Ijarah","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain who bears Takaful/insurance costs and the protections owed to the lessee.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.6","pages":[291]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Nothing - the lessee bears the full loss","Refund the lessee the difference between the rent he paid and the fair market rental of similar assets","Double-charge the lessee for the remaining term","Transfer ownership regardless of the asset's destruction"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-19","type":"mcq","prompt":"In Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek, when does rental start accruing (as opposed to a conventional finance lease)?","explanation":"Unlike a conventional finance lease (rent from payment date), Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek's rental starts only when the asset is supplied to the lessee in usable form.","topicId":"t11.5","chapter":11,"section":"11.5","topicLabel":"Islamic Banks' Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek: Full Procedure","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Walk through the full bank procedure for Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5","pages":[291,292]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["From the date the bank pays the supplier","From the time the asset is supplied to the lessee in useable form","From the date the MoU is signed","From the date Hamish Jiddiyah is taken"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-20","type":"mcq","prompt":"Arrange the Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek steps in order: (A) Formal lease agreement executed, rent begins at delivery; (B) Bank purchases the asset (directly or via agent); (C) Client conveys requirement, MoU + Hamish Jiddiyah; (D) Ownership transfer via separate unilateral promise; (E) Rental payments over the lease term.","explanation":"Correct order: MoU/Hamish Jiddiyah (C) -> bank purchases asset (B) -> formal lease/delivery (A) -> rental payments (E) -> ownership transfer via separate promise (D).","topicId":"t11.5","chapter":11,"section":"11.5","topicLabel":"Islamic Banks' Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek: Full Procedure","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Walk through the full bank procedure for Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","pages":[291,292,293,294]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"ordering","options":["C, B, A, E, D","B, C, A, D, E","C, A, B, E, D","A, B, C, D, E"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch11-21","type":"tf","prompt":"Unlike Murabaha to Purchase Orderer, in Ijarah the lease can become operative directly from the date the client (as purchasing agent) takes delivery, without an additional handover procedure.","explanation":"True - the chapter explicitly notes this contrast: MPO disallows simultaneous transfer by the bank and agent, while in leasing the lease period can begin right from the agent-lessee's delivery date.","topicId":"t11.5.1","chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","topicLabel":"Islamic Banks' Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek: Full Procedure","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Walk through the full bank procedure for Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","pages":[293]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":true},{"id":"q-ch11-22","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank signs an Ijarah agreement at the moment it disburses funds to the supplier, but the asset is delivered to the lessee two months later due to supply delay. Is rent owed for those two months?","explanation":"The chapter explicitly states that if the agreement is signed at disbursement and there's a delivery delay, no rental is due for the gap - banks should instead use a 'promise to lease' first.","topicId":"t11.5.2","chapter":11,"section":"11.5.2","topicLabel":"Operational Issues in Modern Ijarah Practice","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Identify common operational mistakes in Ijarah execution.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.2","pages":[295,296]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, since the agreement was already signed","No - no rental is due for the period between the agreement date and actual delivery","Yes, but at half the normal rate","It depends solely on the bank's discretion"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-23","type":"mcq","prompt":"Per Imam Muhammad, what happens to an Ijarah contract on leased farmland if the crop is destroyed by a natural calamity?","explanation":"Per Imam Muhammad, if leased land's crop is destroyed by natural calamity, the Ijarah becomes invalid and the lessee is entitled to a conventional wage instead.","topicId":"t11.5.2","chapter":11,"section":"11.5.2","topicLabel":"Operational Issues in Modern Ijarah Practice","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain usufruct instability risk and its treatment.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.2","pages":[295]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The Ijarah remains fully valid and rent continues as agreed","The Ijarah becomes invalid and the lessee is entitled instead to a conventional wage","The lessor must double the rental as compensation","The lessee automatically becomes the owner"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-24","type":"tf","prompt":"Ijarah gives Islamic banks a guaranteed, fixed return, since rent is contractually agreed in advance.","explanation":"False - this is explicitly identified as a misconception; banks bear real ownership expenses (Takaful shortfalls, repair costs) that make their targeted net return uncertain.","topicId":"t11.5.2","chapter":11,"section":"11.5.2","topicLabel":"Operational Issues in Modern Ijarah Practice","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Correct the misconception that Ijarah gives a guaranteed return.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.2","pages":[296]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch11-25","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank wants to raise cash by selling only its right to collect rental on a leased asset to a third party, without transferring ownership of the asset itself. Is this permissible?","explanation":"Assigning only the rental right (a monetary transaction) without transferring ownership is not allowed - it amounts to selling money for money, which requires equal value.","topicId":"t11.5.3","chapter":11,"section":"11.5.3","topicLabel":"Assignment and Securitization of Leases","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain how Ijarah Sukuk and lease assignment work.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.3","pages":[297]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, since Ijarah rentals are freely assignable","No - this would amount to selling money (rental) for money, which is not permitted","Yes, if the lessee consents","Yes, if done through Tawarruq"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-26","type":"tf","prompt":"Ijarah Sukuk holders are guaranteed the full contractual rent amount regardless of market conditions or lessee default.","explanation":"False - Ijarah Sukuk returns are only quasi-fixed; holders bear market risk, maintenance/Takaful cost risk, and lessee-payment risk, and suffer pro rata loss in total asset destruction. The contractual rent represents a maximum return, subject to deduction.","topicId":"t11.5.3","chapter":11,"section":"11.5.3","topicLabel":"Assignment and Securitization of Leases","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain how Ijarah Sukuk enable securitization and their risk profile.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.3","pages":[297]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch11-27","type":"mcq","prompt":"AAOIFI's Standard structures the ownership-transfer promise in Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek as running from whom, and how does the author assess this vs common bank practice?","explanation":"AAOIFI provides for the promise to run from the LESSOR; many Islamic banks instead take a binding undertaking from the lessee, but the author considers AAOIFI's approach more justifiable and closer to Shari'ah's spirit.","topicId":"t11.6","chapter":11,"section":"11.6","topicLabel":"Summary Guidelines and Risk Management for Islamic Bankers on Ijarah","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain the three permissible methods for transferring ownership at lease end.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.6","pages":[298,299]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["From the lessee, and the author considers this the correct approach","From the lessor, and the author considers this more justifiable and closer to the spirit of Shari'ah than taking a binding undertaking from the lessee","From a third-party guarantor, which the author rejects entirely","AAOIFI takes no position on this issue"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-28","type":"scenario","prompt":"A leased vehicle under Islamic Ijarah is stolen. Per Box 11.2's comparison with conventional leasing, what happens to rent?","explanation":"Under Islamic Ijarah, rent becomes void immediately upon theft/destruction (since rent is consideration for usage) - unlike conventional leasing, which continues charging rent until insurance settlement.","topicId":"t11.6","chapter":11,"section":"11.6","topicLabel":"Summary Guidelines and Risk Management for Islamic Bankers on Ijarah","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Contrast Islamic Ijarah with conventional auto leasing.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.6","pages":[300,301]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Rent continues to accrue until the Takaful claim is settled, just like conventional leasing","Rent becomes void immediately, since rent is consideration for usage which no longer exists","The lessee must pay double rent as a penalty","The bank automatically transfers ownership to the lessee"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-29","type":"mcq","prompt":"Match each Box 11.1 risk to its mitigation tool: (1) Customer refuses to take a purchased asset on lease; (2) Rate-of-return risk due to inflation; (3) Careless lessee use requiring major repairs.","explanation":"Box 11.1 pairs: refusal to lease -> binding promise + Hamish Jiddiyah; inflation/rate-of-return risk -> benchmarked floating rental with floor/cap; careless use -> trust receipt binding lessee as trustee for negligence-caused loss.","topicId":"t11.6","chapter":11,"section":"11.6","topicLabel":"Summary Guidelines and Risk Management for Islamic Bankers on Ijarah","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply risk mitigation tools for Ijarah.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.6","pages":[299,300]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"matching","options":["1-Binding promise + Hamish Jiddiyah, 2-Benchmarked floating rental (floor/cap), 3-Trust receipt binding lessee to use as trustee","1-Takaful, 2-Trust receipt, 3-Binding promise","1-Benchmarked rental, 2-Trust receipt, 3-Hamish Jiddiyah","1-Trust receipt, 2-Hamish Jiddiyah, 3-Benchmarked rental"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch11-30","type":"mcq","prompt":"What upfront payment do Islamic banks normally take from an Ijarah customer, compared to conventional leasing's typical upfront requirements?","explanation":"Per Box 11.2, Islamic banks normally take only a refundable security deposit, while conventional leasing typically requires substantial non-refundable upfront payments.","topicId":"t11.6","chapter":11,"section":"11.6","topicLabel":"Summary Guidelines and Risk Management for Islamic Bankers on Ijarah","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Contrast Islamic Ijarah with conventional auto leasing.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.6","pages":[301]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The same non-refundable downpayment, insurance premium, and first month's rent as conventional leasing","Only a refundable security deposit, recoverable to the extent of actual expenses (excluding cost of funds) if the lease is not finalized","No upfront payment of any kind","A non-refundable processing fee equal to one year's rent"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-31","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following CANNOT validly be the subject of an Ijarah lease, per the chapter?","explanation":"Money/edibles/fuel (their use requires consuming them) and land incapable of any usufruct (e.g. through salinity) cannot be leased. An existing house and jointly-owned property CAN be validly leased.","topicId":"t11.3","chapter":11,"section":"11.3","topicLabel":"General Juristic Rules of Ijarah","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State which assets can be leased and why.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3","pages":[281]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Money, edibles, or fuel (usufruct not possible without consuming the corpus)","Land so damaged by salinity that it cannot produce any usufruct","A liveable, existing house","An asset owned jointly by several people"],"correctAnswer":[0,1]},{"id":"q-ch11-32","type":"scenario","prompt":"A lessee sub-leases a shop at exactly the same rent he pays the original lessor. Is this permissible, and does the school of thought matter here?","explanation":"All recognized schools of Islamic jurisprudence are unanimous on the permissibility of sub-lease if the rent from the sub-lease is equal to or less than the rent payable to the original owner/lessor; disagreement arises only when sub-lease rent is higher.","topicId":"t11.3.3","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.3","topicLabel":"Sub-leasing, Security, and Liability Allocation","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Analyze the rules governing sub-leasing.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.3","pages":[284]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes - this is unanimously permitted by all recognized schools since sub-lease rent does not exceed the rent paid to the owner","No - sub-leasing is never permitted","Yes, but only Hanafis permit it","No, unless the lessor charges a fee for consent"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch11-33","type":"tf","prompt":"The death of either the lessor or the lessee automatically terminates an Ijarah contract, per contemporary jurists.","explanation":"False - contemporary jurists hold Ijarah is not terminated by the death of either party, though a deceased lessee's heirs may terminate it if the lease has become too onerous for them.","topicId":"t11.3.6","chapter":11,"section":"11.3.6","topicLabel":"Termination, Amendment, and Rent Default Rules","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain the rules on termination and amendment.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.6","pages":[286]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch11-34","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why must a 'sale and lease-back' Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek arrangement leave a reasonable period (commonly around one year) before any transfer of ownership back to the original client?","explanation":"The gap between the sale-and-leaseback purchase and any transfer back to the customer must be long enough that the leased property or its value might have changed, to avoid the arrangement becoming Bai' al 'Inah.","topicId":"t11.5.1","chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","topicLabel":"Islamic Banks' Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek: Full Procedure","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the sale-and-leaseback structure and its Shari'ah safeguards.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","pages":[293]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["To allow time for Takaful registration only","To avoid the transaction becoming prohibited Bai' al 'Inah, since the gap must be long enough that the property's value might genuinely have changed","Because tax law requires a one-year holding period everywhere","There is no such requirement"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch11-35","type":"short","prompt":"Describe how Diminishing Musharakah can be used as an alternative to a unilateral promise to sell/gift for transferring ownership in Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek.","explanation":"This reflects the Diminishing Musharakah alternative described in section 11.5.","topicId":"t11.5","chapter":11,"section":"11.5","topicLabel":"Islamic Banks' Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek: Full Procedure","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the alternative methods for transferring ownership at lease end.","source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5","pages":[292,294]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"The bank and client jointly purchase the asset, creating a partnership by ownership (Shirkatulmilk). The bank leases out its own share to the client on the Diminishing Musharakah principle. As the client periodically purchases portions of the bank's share, the bank's ownership share - and the rental it receives, which must be proportional to its remaining share - decreases accordingly, until full ownership passes to the client."}],"flashcards":[{"id":"f11.1","front":"Ijarah","back":"Transfer of a known usufruct of a specified asset for a specified period against specified lawful rent — the sale of usufruct (or hire of work for wages).","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t11.1","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.1","pages":[279,279]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.2","front":"Five essentials of Ijarah","back":"A contract; known usufruct transferred; of a particular asset; for a specified period; against agreed rent.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.2","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.2","pages":[280,280]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.3","front":"Ijarah vs Bai‘","back":"Ijarah: usufruct only, time-bound, lessor keeps ownership risk/expenses. Bai‘: corpus ownership passes permanently with risk.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t11.2.1","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.2.1","pages":[280,281]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.4","front":"What cannot be leased?","back":"Things consumed in use — money, food, fuel, candles, ammunition. Leasing money is a loan; its ‘rent’ is interest.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t11.3","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3","pages":[281,282]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.5","front":"When does Ijarah rent become due?","back":"Only after the asset is in the lessee’s possession in usable form (contemporary jurists, AAOIFI) — not when funds are paid to the supplier.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.3","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3","pages":[281,282]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.6","front":"Floating rent in Ijarah","back":"Allowed for future periods via a well-defined benchmark if the first period’s rent is specified; cap/floor recommended (e.g. ±5%).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.3.2","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","pages":[283,284]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.7","front":"Essential difference between Ijarah and interest-based lease finance","back":"The lessor bears full risk of the corpus; if usufruct is lost without lessee fault, no rent is due.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t11.3.2","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","pages":[283,284]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.8","front":"Lessor vs lessee liabilities","back":"Lessor: ownership taxes, major repairs, loss without lessee fault, Takaful. Lessee: usage costs, day-to-day maintenance, negligence.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t11.3.5","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.5","pages":[285,285]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.9","front":"Early termination of Ijarah for lessee breach","back":"Lessor takes the asset back; lessee pays rent due to date (and negligence damages), never rent for the remaining period.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.3.6","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.6","pages":[286,287]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.10","front":"Four Shari’ah problems in conventional leases","back":"Sale built into lease; rent before delivery; all risk on lessee/fixed residual value; lessee bears costs even in operating lease.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t11.4.4","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.4","pages":[289,289]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.11","front":"Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek (IMBT)","back":"Ijarah ending in ownership transfer through a separate unilateral promise to sell or gift; rent from usable delivery; bank bears ownership risk.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t11.5","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5","pages":[291,293]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.12","front":"AAOIFI methods of transferring ownership in IMBT","back":"Promise to sell (token, remaining amount or market value); promise to gift at end; promise of gift contingent on paying remaining instalments.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.5","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5","pages":[291,293]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.13","front":"Sale and lease-back conditions","back":"Exceptional use; sale precedes lease; not conditional; asset sold back only after a reasonable period (~1 year) to avoid ‘Inah.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.5.1","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","pages":[293,295]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.14","front":"AAOIFI 8/8 — when transfer becomes impossible without lessee fault","back":"Refund to lessee the excess of contractual rent over market rent; Takaful proceeds above bank’s cost go to the client.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t11.5.1","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","pages":[293,295]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.15","front":"Two non-negotiable principles in Ijarah (Section 11.5.2)","back":"Risk cannot be separated from ownership; lease and sale must be kept separate and independent.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t11.5.2","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.2","pages":[295,296]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.16","front":"Ijarah Sukuk","back":"Certificates of pro rata ownership in leased assets; holders bear owner risks/costs; quasi-fixed return (rent is a maximum); tradable at market price.","category":"Capital markets","topicId":"t11.5.3","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.3","pages":[297,297]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.17","front":"Box 11.1 — tools for Ijarah risks","back":"Promise + HJ; charity undertaking + collateral; Takaful; buy-back price schedule; trust receipt; benchmarked rent with floor/cap; resaleable assets or purchase promise.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t11.6","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.6","pages":[298,305]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.18","front":"Ijarah (legal definition)","back":"A contract for a known usufruct of a specified asset for a specified time against a specified lawful return — or for work against a wage.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t11.1","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.1","pages":[279,279]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.19","front":"Ajr al-Musammah vs Ajr al-Mithl","back":"Rent/wage fixed in the contract vs customary rate set by a judge or arbitrator.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t11.2","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.2","pages":[280,280]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.20","front":"Can a specified asset be leased before the lessor owns it?","back":"No — take a promise to lease; the Ijarah is signed after ownership. Described assets (Mosufah bil Zimmah) may be leased for the future.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.3.1","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.1","pages":[282,283]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.21","front":"Floating rent requirements","back":"First-period rent must be fixed; later rent may follow a benchmark, inflation or taxes, with a cap and floor to avoid Gharar.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.3.2","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","pages":[283,284]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.22","front":"Sub-lease surplus","back":"Abu Hanifa: to charity unless the lessee improved the asset or rents in another currency. Shafi‘i/Hanbali (preferred): lessee keeps it.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.3.3","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.3","pages":[284,285]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.23","front":"Death of a party in Ijarah","back":"Does not end the lease (contemporary view); the lessee’s heirs may terminate if it becomes too onerous.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.3.6","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.6","pages":[286,287]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.24","front":"Sale of a leased asset to a third party","back":"Lease continues with the buyer; lessee’s consent not needed; an uninformed buyer may cancel the purchase.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.3.6","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.6","pages":[286,287]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.25","front":"Three conventional lease forms","back":"Financial lease/hire–purchase and security lease (not acceptable as structured); operating lease (compatible if lessor bears ownership costs).","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t11.4","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4","pages":[287,289]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.26","front":"Is Ijarah return fixed?","back":"No — the bank bears acquisition, Takaful, major repair costs and claim shortfalls, so net return is uncertain.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t11.5.2","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.2","pages":[295,296]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f11.27","front":"Box 11.3 — car destroyed, AAOIFI 8/8","back":"Client gets the Rs.50,000 deposit plus Takaful surplus over outstanding liabilities (Rs.80,000) = Rs.130,000.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t11.6","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.6","pages":[298,305]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-1","front":"What does Ijarah literally mean, and what is it a sale of?","back":"Derived from al-'Ajr (compensation/consideration); Ijarah is the sale of USUFRUCT (the right to use an asset), not of the corpus/'Ain (the underlying asset itself).","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t11.1","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.1","pages":[279]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-2","front":"List the five essentials of a valid Ijarah contract.","back":"(1) It is a contract; (2) known usufruct is transferred; (3) of a particular asset; (4) for a specified time period; (5) against agreed-upon rental.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t11.2","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.2","pages":[280]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-3","front":"What is the fundamental structural difference between Ijarah and Bai' (sale)?","back":"In Bai', ownership of the corpus transfers to the buyer immediately. In Ijarah, only usufruct (right to use) transfers to the lessee; ownership and its associated risk remain with the lessor throughout.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t11.2.1","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.2.1","pages":[280,281]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-4","front":"Why can money, edibles, or fuel not be leased under Ijarah?","back":"Their usufruct cannot be enjoyed without consuming the corpus. Leasing them is deemed a loan, and any rent charged on them amounts to Riba.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t11.3","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3","pages":[281]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-5","front":"When does rent become due in Ijarah, and is this true even under the Shafi'i view?","back":"No rent becomes due merely from contract execution - the subject must be delivered and made available first. Even Shafi'is, who hold rent is due at contract finalization, presume this converges with delivery since usufruct materializes through actual use.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.3","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3","pages":[282]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-6","front":"Can an Ijarah contract for a specific, particular asset be executed before the lessor owns it?","back":"No - unlike Ijarah Mosufah bil Zimmah (asset specified by description), a lease on a particular identified asset cannot be executed before the lessor obtains ownership/possession; instead, a 'promise to lease' is used.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.3.1","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.1","pages":[282,283]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-7","front":"Does using an interest-rate benchmark like LIBOR to price Ijarah rent automatically make the lease non-Shari'ah-compliant?","back":"No - the author explicitly rejects this minority view. What matters is that the lessor genuinely bears full ownership/corpus risk; benchmarking pricing to an interest rate does not itself create Riba, though non-interest benchmarks are still preferred for clarity.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t11.3.2","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","pages":[284]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-8","front":"What is the essential Shari'ah distinction between a valid lease and interest-based lease financing?","back":"In a valid lease, the LESSOR bears full risk of the asset's corpus (losing rent claims if usufruct is lost without lessee fault). In interest-based lease financing, the lessee bears all ownership-related expense and responsibility.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t11.3.2","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","pages":[284]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-9","front":"What happens if a sub-lessor charges MORE rent than he pays the original lessor, per the Hanafi vs Shafi'i/Hanbali views?","back":"Hanafi (Abu Hanifa): the surplus must go to charity (unless he improved the property or rents in a different currency). Shafi'i/Hanbali (preferred contemporary view): the sub-lessor can keep the surplus.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t11.3.3","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.3","pages":[284]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-10","front":"How is expense liability split between lessor and lessee in Ijarah?","back":"Ownership-related expenses (property taxes, major repairs) fall on the LESSOR; usage-related expenses (utilities, day-to-day upkeep) fall on the LESSEE.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.3.5","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.5","pages":[285]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-11","front":"If a lessee breaches the contract and the lessor terminates, how much rent can the lessor collect?","back":"Only rent already due up to the termination date - never rent for the remaining, unused lease period. Charging for the remaining period would effectively disguise an interest-bearing loan as a lease.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.3.6","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.6","pages":[286,287]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-12","front":"What happens to a Ijarah contract if the leased asset is sold to a third party (not the lessee)?","back":"The Ijarah continues - it does not terminate; the rights and obligations transfer to the new owner. The lessee's consent is not required for such a sale.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.3.6","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.6","pages":[286]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-13","front":"Name a key Shari'ah flaw in conventional financial lease (hire-purchase).","back":"Rent starts accruing from the date the lessor PAYS the supplier (not from when the lessee can use the asset), and ownership risk is borne by the lessee throughout - both conflict with Shari'ah Ijarah principles.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t11.4","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.1","pages":[288]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-14","front":"Which conventional lease structure does the book identify as most compatible with Shari'ah, and why?","back":"Operating lease - the lessor retains ownership and bears its risk throughout, taking the asset back at lease end (subject to other conditions being fulfilled).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t11.4","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.3","pages":[289]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-15","front":"How does Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek solve the ownership-transfer problem while avoiding 'two contracts in one bargain'?","back":"The lease and the eventual sale/gift are kept as two independent things: the lease is immediately effective, and the lessor separately makes a UNILATERAL promise (binding only on itself) to sell or gift the asset at lease end.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.4.5","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.5","pages":[289,290]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-16","front":"Why must the ownership-transfer promise in Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek be UNILATERAL, not bilateral?","back":"A bilateral (mutual) promise would itself constitute a prohibited premature contract. A unilateral promise binds only the promisor (lessor); the lessee retains a free option to purchase or not.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.4.5","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.5","pages":[290]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-17","front":"Who bears the insurable interest for a leased asset, and can Takaful cost still be recovered via rental?","back":"The insurable interest belongs to the LESSOR (as owner), who should technically be the Takaful policyholder. Takaful cost can still be folded into the acquisition cost used to calculate rental, permitted via the principle of mutual consent to the resulting rent.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t11.4.6","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.6","pages":[291]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-18","front":"Name three key structural differences between Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek and a conventional finance lease.","back":"(1) Rent starts at DELIVERY in usable form, not at payment to the supplier; (2) if price is paid to the lessee, a SEPARATE agency agreement is required, with pre-delivery destruction loss falling on the bank; (3) asset risk stays with the bank throughout the agency-purchase period.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t11.5","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5","pages":[291,292]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-19","front":"Unlike Murabaha, when can the lease under Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek become operative if the client is the bank's purchasing agent?","back":"The lease can become operative directly from the date the client, as agent, takes delivery - no separate handover procedure is needed (unlike MPO, which disallows simultaneous transfer).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t11.5.1","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","pages":[293]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-20","front":"What are the three permissible methods for transferring ownership at the end of an Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek lease?","back":"(1) A promise to sell for a token/other consideration, accelerated payment, or market value; (2) a promise to gift the asset unconditionally; (3) a promise to gift the asset contingent on a specific event (e.g. remaining instalments paid).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t11.5","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5","pages":[292]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-21","front":"If an Ijarah agreement is signed before the asset is actually available, and delivery is delayed, is rent owed for the gap period?","back":"No - no rental is due for the period between the agreement date and actual delivery. The fix is to use a 'promise to lease' first, executing the actual Ijarah only once the asset is available.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t11.5.2","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.2","pages":[295,296]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-22","front":"Does Ijarah give Islamic banks a guaranteed, fixed return?","back":"No - this is a common misconception. Banks bear genuine ownership expenses and risks (Takaful shortfalls, repair costs, usufruct instability), so their targeted net return is not certain.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t11.5.2","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.2","pages":[296]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-23","front":"What are the two guiding principles for resolving Ijarah's operational challenges?","back":"(1) Risk cannot be separated from ownership - the bank must genuinely bear responsibility (Dhaman) for what it owns; (2) lease and sale are different-natured contracts and must be kept separate and independent.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t11.5.2","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.2","pages":[296]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-24","front":"Can a lessor assign only the right to collect rental without transferring ownership of the leased asset?","back":"No - this would amount to selling money (rental) for money, requiring equal-for-equal exchange, which is not permitted. Only selling the asset itself (with the lease continuing) is allowed.","category":"Capital markets","topicId":"t11.5.3","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.3","pages":[297]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-25","front":"Do Ijarah Sukuk offer a guaranteed fixed return like a conventional bond?","back":"No - Ijarah Sukuk represent real ownership in leased assets; holders bear market, maintenance, and lessee-default risk, so the contractual rent is only a maximum return, subject to deduction for unexpected expenses (quasi-fixed at best).","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t11.5.3","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.3","pages":[297]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-26","front":"Whose promise does AAOIFI structure the ownership-transfer promise as running from, and why does the author prefer this?","back":"AAOIFI structures it as running FROM THE LESSOR (binding the bank, giving the lessee a free option) - the author considers this more justifiable and closer to the spirit of Shari'ah than the common practice of taking a binding undertaking from the lessee.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t11.6","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.6","pages":[298,299]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-27","front":"Under Islamic Ijarah, what happens to rent if a leased car is stolen or destroyed?","back":"Rent becomes void immediately, since rent is consideration for usage which no longer exists - unlike conventional leasing, which continues charging rent until the insurance claim is settled.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t11.6","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.6","pages":[300,301]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-28","front":"Can severely salinity-damaged land, incapable of any production, be leased under Ijarah?","back":"No - Ijarah requires that usufruct genuinely be obtainable from the asset; land whose usufruct is almost impossible to realize (e.g. destroyed by salinity) cannot become the subject of a valid lease.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.3","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3","pages":[281]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-29","front":"Is sub-leasing at a rent EQUAL TO OR LOWER THAN the rent paid to the original lessor permissible?","back":"Yes - all recognized schools of Islamic jurisprudence unanimously permit sub-leasing (with the lessor's consent) when the sub-lease rent is equal to or less than the rent payable to the original owner/lessor; disagreement arises only when the sub-lease rent is HIGHER.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.3.3","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.3","pages":[284]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-30","front":"If an Ijarah lessee defaults on rental or damages the asset, what can the lessor recover from the security taken, and what is excluded?","back":"The lessor can recover only the actual loss - excluding the cost of funds or 'opportunity cost' in modern terminology. Any amount taken as income over and above the due rent would be Riba.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t11.3.4","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.4","pages":[285]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-31","front":"Does the death of the lessor or lessee automatically terminate an Ijarah contract?","back":"No - contemporary jurists hold that Ijarah is not terminated by the death of either party, though the heirs of a deceased lessee can terminate the lease if they feel it has become too onerous for their resources to continue paying rental.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t11.3.6","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.6","pages":[286]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-32","front":"Besides a promise to sell or gift, what structural alternative lets ownership transfer gradually during an Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek lease?","back":"Diminishing Musharakah: the bank and client jointly own the asset (Shirkatulmilk); the bank leases out its share, and as the client periodically buys portions of the bank's share, both the bank's ownership share and the corresponding rental decrease accordingly.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t11.5","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5","pages":[292,294]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch11-33","front":"Why must there be a 'reasonable period' (commonly suggested as around one year) between an Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek sale-and-leaseback purchase and any transfer of ownership back to the original client?","back":"To avoid the transaction becoming prohibited Bai' al 'Inah - the gap must be long enough that the leased property or its value might genuinely have changed, distinguishing it from a disguised buy-back loan.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t11.5.1","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","pages":[293]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}],"exam":[{"id":"e11.1","kind":"long","prompt":"Critically compare conventional financial leasing with Islamic Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek.","expectedStructure":["Definitions","Features of financial lease/hire–purchase","Four Shari’ah objections","IMBT solutions: rent accrual, risk, separate transfer","AAOIFI transfer methods","Box 11.2 auto comparison","Remaining issues and two core principles"],"keyConcepts":["usufruct","ownership risk","unilateral promise","Takaful"],"essentialPoints":["Rent from usable delivery","No remaining-period rent","Takaful at lessor’s cost"],"commonMistakes":["Saying IMBT is hire–purchase renamed","Putting risk on lessee"],"topicId":"t11.5","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5","pages":[291,293]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e11.2","kind":"short","prompt":"Discuss the rules on determination of rent in Ijarah, including the use of benchmarks.","expectedStructure":["Cost-based rent","No unilateral increase","Phased/indexed/benchmarked rent","LIBOR debate","First-period rent and cap/floor"],"keyConcepts":["benchmark","Gharar"],"essentialPoints":["Lessor’s corpus risk distinguishes Ijarah"],"commonMistakes":["Allowing retrospective increase"],"topicId":"t11.3.2","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.2","pages":[283,284]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e11.3","kind":"scenario","prompt":"A client wants to convert its conventional machinery loan to Islamic financing. Advise on sale and lease-back IMBT.","expectedStructure":["Permissibility and exceptional nature","Sequence: sale then lease","Time gap before transfer back","Documentation (Box 11.3)","Risks and mitigation"],"keyConcepts":["sale and lease-back","‘Inah"],"essentialPoints":["Need and willingness to avoid interest"],"commonMistakes":["Making lease a condition of the sale"],"topicId":"t11.5.1","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.1","pages":[293,295]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e11.4","kind":"conceptual","prompt":"Explain how Ijarah can be securitised and why Ijarah Sukuk yield a quasi-fixed return.","expectedStructure":["Sale of leased asset with lease","Rent-only assignment prohibited","Pro rata ownership","Risks borne by holders","Potential for governments"],"keyConcepts":["Ijarah Sukuk","tradability"],"essentialPoints":["Rent = maximum return"],"commonMistakes":["Calling Ijarah Sukuk risk-free"],"topicId":"t11.5.3","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.5.3","pages":[297,297]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e11.5","kind":"viva","prompt":"Who pays Takaful on a leased car, and who bears a shortfall in the claim?","expectedStructure":["Owner’s insurable interest","Pricing into rent","Shortfall"],"keyConcepts":["Takaful"],"essentialPoints":["Bank bears shortfall absent lessee breach"],"commonMistakes":["Charging shortfall to lessee"],"topicId":"t11.4.6","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.6","pages":[291,291]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e11.6","kind":"long","prompt":"Discuss the rules for termination of an Ijarah and the rights of each party on destruction, default and breach.","expectedStructure":["Ijarah as a binding contract","Termination by mutual consent or impossibility (total destruction)","Partial damage and rent adjustment; Mosufah bil Zimmah replacement","Lessee breach: rent due only, plus negligence loss","Default: charity penalty, acceleration if agreed, repossession","Death, sale to third party","IMBT: AAOIFI 8/8 protection"],"keyConcepts":["Ameen","Mosufah bil Zimmah","acceleration","AAOIFI 8/8","Hamish Jiddiyah"],"essentialPoints":["No rent for remaining period","No rent when asset unusable without fault","Takaful shortfall on the bank"],"commonMistakes":["Charging future rent on termination","Making lessee bear destruction risk"],"topicId":"t11.3.6","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.3.6","pages":[286,287]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-9","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Explain Ijarah and how it fundamentally differs from Bai' (sale).","expectedStructure":["Define Ijarah as a transfer of usufruct, not ownership","Contrast duration (time-bound vs instant/definite)","Explain the risk-bearing implication"],"keyConcepts":["Ijarah","Usufruct","Corpus (Ayn)"],"essentialPoints":["In Ijarah, only the usufruct (right of use) transfers to the lessee; the lessor retains ownership of the corpus and its associated risks.","Ijarah is always time-bound and must terminate at some point, while a sale is a definite, instant transfer of ownership with no comparable term.","If the lessee acquires the corpus by some other means (e.g. inheritance), the Ijarah automatically ceases."],"commonMistakes":["Describing Ijarah as simply 'renting', without noting the specific Shari'ah requirement that ownership-related risk stays with the lessor.","Confusing Ijarah with Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek, which adds a separate ownership-transfer promise."],"topicId":"t11.2.1","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.2.1","pages":[280]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-10","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Explain how Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek avoids the prohibition on combining two contracts in one bargain.","expectedStructure":["Describe the structure (lease plus unilateral promise)","Explain why a bilateral, combined agreement would be prohibited","Explain how keeping the promise separate and unilateral resolves this"],"keyConcepts":["Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek","Unilateral promise","Two contracts in one bargain"],"essentialPoints":["The lease contract takes immediate effect on its own terms; a separate, independent unilateral promise by the lessor to sell or gift the asset at the end of the term is signed alongside it.","Because the promise binds only the promisor (the lessor) and not the lessee, and is not a precondition of the lease itself, the two remain legally distinct rather than one bundled bargain.","Contemporary scholars specifically recommend a lease agreement should not contain a precondition of sale or gift after the lease period."],"commonMistakes":["Describing the ownership transfer as automatic/contractually guaranteed within the lease itself, which is what the Shari'ah objection targets.","Missing that the promise can be to sell or to gift the asset, not only to sell."],"topicId":"t11.4.5","chapter":11,"source":{"chapter":11,"section":"11.4.5","pages":[289]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}]});
